- I'm told I can't change my name yet.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.880
- Yeah, exactly, to go back to us.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:14.190
So a couple of things just to mention.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:16.980
So first of all, a big
thanks to Sue and to Rob,

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:20.580
for hosting us and
hopefully sharing with us

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:22.890
a lot of what is happening down here

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:25.830
across the immediate area,

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.050
but also the wider South West.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:30.873
And welcome to Paul
who has just joined us.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:36.180
Great, so we have a few
apologies for absence.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:40.170
So from Glen Burley, Sam
Everington, and David Bennett.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.330
Does anyone have any
declarations of interest

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:44.163
that have not already been declared?

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.450
Nope, okay, great.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:52.140
So first item is minutes
from the Board meeting

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.840
that was held in December.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.393
Has anyone got any comments on those?

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.197
Okay, we'll take those
as approved, thank you.

00:03:03.197 --> 00:03:05.460
So we're now going to go on to updates

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:09.360
from each of the Board subcommittees,

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.780
and we will start with
audit and risk, Jeremy.

00:03:12.780 --> 00:03:14.250
- Thanks, Penny.

00:03:14.250 --> 00:03:18.510
We not only had our NHS
England Audit Risk Committee,

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:20.730
we also had our first
joint audit committee

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:23.613
in the last timeframe with the department.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:26.700
And within that, one of
the things we're managing

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:31.200
is transition risk as we
transition the department

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:33.270
and NHS England together.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:34.710
Within that conversation as well,

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:36.780
that we also noted a
number of material risks,

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.450
which we've been managing in the committee

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:41.700
for a number of months, if not years.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:43.440
Cyber is a good example,

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:46.920
systems within primary care, supply chain.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:48.600
And one of the key things, I think,

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:52.980
in the next 15 months or
so as we're transitioning

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:55.140
is just to make sure we
don't like lose sight

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:56.370
of the risks that we're trying to manage

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:58.500
and we don't work on the processes

00:03:58.500 --> 00:03:59.970
as opposed to managing the risks.

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:02.580
So the committee agreed strongly

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:04.230
that we should be flagging the risks,

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:07.740
making sure they're part of
the five-year, 10-year plan,

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:09.600
we've got the appropriate
resources in place,

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.250
'cause if we're not careful

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:12.390
on focusing on the 10-year plan,

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:14.270
we'll miss some of the things
that could jeopardise it.

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:16.009
And Elizabeth and Jim
have been working on that

00:04:16.009 --> 00:04:18.300
to come back with a plan for mitigation.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:19.680
One of the things as well, I think,

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:23.310
this to some sense sits
across with DDAT as well is

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:27.120
should we put a more continuity
exercise in around cyber

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:28.770
because we are very
concerned around cyber,

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:30.270
and that's I think one of
the things for the Board

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:31.950
to consider as to should we do

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:34.440
a more comprehensive continuity process.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.450
'Cause having experienced one at M&amp;S,

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:38.040
they're not pretty when they happen,

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:39.990
so it's just something to consider.

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:40.890
That was it Penny.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:43.440
- Okay, so can I just pick up on that?

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.270
- [Jeremy] Yes.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:48.990
- So consider means we
might think about it,

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:50.220
but is it a bit stronger than that?

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:52.260
- Well, my strong recommendation is,

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:53.093
and I think the same with markets

00:04:53.093 --> 00:04:54.030
is that we should do it.

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:55.213
- Yeah,
- Yeah.

00:04:55.213 --> 00:04:56.876
- Okay, yeah, yeah.

00:04:56.876 --> 00:04:59.880
- I think we do need
a proper exercise plan

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:01.080
for what would happen.

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:04.950
- With multiple stakeholders
and not just a paper book.

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:05.783
- Is that okay, Jim?

00:05:05.783 --> 00:05:07.140
- Absolutely.
- Okay, so we take that

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.437
as a given that we will do that.

00:05:08.437 --> 00:05:09.330
- Yeah.

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:10.163
- Okay, thank you.

00:05:10.163 --> 00:05:12.326
Does anyone have any questions, Louise?

00:05:12.326 --> 00:05:13.159
- Thanks, Jeremy.

00:05:13.159 --> 00:05:15.933
Is there a case for an interim
audit on the VR scheme?

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:19.770
- I think that's being monitored.

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:21.930
I think Deloitte having
a look at the VR scheme

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:23.130
but Elizabeth, over to you.

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:26.470
- Yeah, so we've asked
Delight to do an audit

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:30.930
to take a look and work
in parallel with us.

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.220
And obviously, Louise,

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:36.577
we are also looking at
how we repurpose EHRG

00:05:38.010 --> 00:05:40.650
as a subcommittee of the people committee,

00:05:40.650 --> 00:05:42.420
which is obviously a
subcommittee of the Board.

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:44.400
So we came to work with you on that.

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:49.720
I think EHRG historically has been a group

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:54.390
which has been wholly executives

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:57.300
and we would welcome
some non-exec colleagues

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:00.150
joining that group in the interim period.

00:06:00.150 --> 00:06:02.730
But also, we've been
looking at whether or not

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:06.453
almost the rules, the
legalities of the scheme,

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:09.420
are correct, rather than the spirit

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:10.800
of what's actually happening.

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.310
And I think there's been an expression by,

00:06:14.310 --> 00:06:15.990
I think it is Jim and Penny,

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:17.100
that we should look at that

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:19.650
as a bit more of a remuneration committee.

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:21.900
I'm very conscious when
the information comes

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:24.960
from EHRG through to the people committee,

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:27.330
it's consolidated, so it
would be difficult for you

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:29.910
to really test some of
what's coming through.

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:32.910
So that's something that
we are working on together.

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:33.960
- Yeah, it's a really good point, Louise.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:35.760
I think it needs to be done now.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:37.167
You can't have Deloitte
come in in a year's time

00:06:37.167 --> 00:06:39.780
and say, "Did it work okay,"
'cause that's too late.

00:06:39.780 --> 00:06:42.360
So one of the things we've
said to internal audit

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:45.780
is we need to free up some
of their plans to be flexible

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:47.760
to looking at transition
risks as they rise,

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:48.940
and that's a very good example too.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:51.540
Yeah, I'll follow up with,
or we can follow up with.

00:06:53.788 --> 00:06:56.250
- Okay, okay good.

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:58.260
Let's go on to quality committee

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:01.140
and the National Quality Board, Robert.

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:03.210
- Yeah, so you've got notes of both,

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:04.950
and Penny, you may want to comment

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:07.110
on how we're going to bring
these together going ahead.

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:09.360
But from the quality committee,

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:11.280
three things I would highlight two,

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:15.690
as it were to celebrate
and one as a challenge.

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:20.690
So on the GP access
and unwanted variation,

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:24.180
I think it's worth noting the improvement

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:26.970
in access and patient satisfaction,

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:30.150
and improvement in hypertension detection,

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:33.780
all of which are things are
going in the right direction.

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:36.210
Another one to celebrate
I think is the MOSS,

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:39.540
the early warning scheme for maternity,

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:40.590
which has been rolled out

00:07:40.590 --> 00:07:44.010
across the whole of the
NHS remarkably rapidly,

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:46.110
and I think it's a really good innovation.

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:50.730
The mental health issue,

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:52.890
and out-of-area placements,

00:07:52.890 --> 00:07:55.260
it's a terrible patient experience,

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:57.120
and it's not eradicated yet.

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:58.980
Numbers have come down,

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:00.330
and there's a real commitment

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:02.937
to eliminate out-of-area
placements by 2028.

00:08:02.937 --> 00:08:04.890
And it's really important that we do that

00:08:04.890 --> 00:08:08.583
because it's a disgrace as
things stand at the moment.

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:11.910
- Okay.

00:08:11.910 --> 00:08:13.410
Meghana, Claire, do you want to pick up

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:14.760
on the National Quality Board?

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:15.720
- Thank you, Penny.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:17.610
So we had the National Quality Board

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:20.340
in its refreshed form,
it was the third meeting,

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:23.970
and the Board received updates
on the quality strategy

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:28.920
in its then draft, and provided feedback

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:30.810
which has been taken into account.

00:08:30.810 --> 00:08:33.720
Going forwards, there's going
to be another two meetings

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:35.910
before a final draft
of the quality strategy

00:08:35.910 --> 00:08:39.450
is taken to the NQB.

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:41.760
The NQB also supported the decision

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:44.353
to have an MSF for
children and young people,

00:08:44.353 --> 00:08:46.230
a modern service framework,

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:49.770
and received updates on
modern service frameworks

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:52.830
for cardiovascular disease and sepsis,

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:56.673
which are the first two
hoping deliver those,

00:08:57.600 --> 00:08:59.550
again, at the end of March.

00:08:59.550 --> 00:09:02.430
- Thank you, and Duncan,
do you want to add anything

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:03.960
or talk a bit more about the maternity?

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:06.420
- Well, I think just to add on maternity.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:07.680
It's great, we've gone live with the MOSS,

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:10.710
we've also launched the
Maternal Care Bundle

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:12.690
in the last few weeks
as well and this week,

00:09:12.690 --> 00:09:17.070
we've gone live with a
Inequalities Dashboard,

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:19.530
which is now available
for trusts to be able to,

00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:21.270
it pulls together all of
the kind of information

00:09:21.270 --> 00:09:23.940
that we already have on
inequalities in maternity.

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:25.800
So some really good progress

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:27.400
that's been made in those areas.

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:30.093
- Yeah, so the only thing I'd say is,

00:09:30.093 --> 00:09:31.860
what is feeling different,

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:35.100
is previously both the
previous quality strategy

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:37.170
and previous iterations of quality Boards

00:09:37.170 --> 00:09:39.630
have been very hospital-focused.

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:41.130
And I think it feels completely different,

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:43.710
and actually having had
the deep dives at committee

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:45.450
has been really helpful
and really positive,

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:46.920
and actually the changing
in the reporting.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:49.380
So I think that's a real positive change.

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:50.213
- Yeah, I agree.

00:09:50.213 --> 00:09:52.740
And I think when we come
onto the performance report,

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:56.970
we'll pick up on some of the
primary care data as well.

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:57.900
Shall I just mention,

00:09:57.900 --> 00:09:59.130
and then open it up for questions,

00:09:59.130 --> 00:10:02.220
so there is duplication obviously

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:04.140
across what the National
Quality Board is doing

00:10:04.140 --> 00:10:07.833
and the Quality Committee
of the Board of NHS England.

00:10:08.790 --> 00:10:11.820
But for governance and legal reasons,

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:13.800
we can't fully merge those.

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:16.050
So what we are planning to do,

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:18.420
and I propose we start this from March,

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:22.440
is that we hold both
meetings on the same day

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:24.240
and we essentially run them together.

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:27.240
So we've got more or less the same people.

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:30.180
The National Quality Board
includes people from the CQC,

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:33.480
they wouldn't be on the quality
committee of NHS England.

00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:35.940
But otherwise, the leadership,

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:37.440
certainly from this group, is the same.

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:40.350
So that hopefully will make
it feel a bit more seamless,

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:42.510
be more efficient in
terms of people's time,

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:46.890
and allow us to get into
different areas in more detail.

00:10:46.890 --> 00:10:47.723
That's the plan.

00:10:47.723 --> 00:10:48.810
Andrew.

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:52.470
- So just can I just go back to maternity.

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:53.940
I think we agreed some time ago

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:55.377
to do a deep dive on maternity,

00:10:55.377 --> 00:10:57.570
and I think given the kind of profile

00:10:57.570 --> 00:10:59.100
that it's got nationally,

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:00.620
I think that would be good for the Board

00:11:00.620 --> 00:11:02.250
to have oversight on.

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:03.966
And secondly, with the MOSS data,

00:11:03.966 --> 00:11:06.540
isn't there any chance of
incorporating the headlines

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:08.050
into the performance report

00:11:09.450 --> 00:11:10.560
going forward?
- So there are some...

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:12.840
So just to say on the performance report,

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:14.370
there are new measures

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:16.860
that will be coming through on that.

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:19.350
We've started collecting
real-time information

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:23.040
on some of the operational
pressures in maternity services.

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:24.030
We just need to make sure

00:11:24.030 --> 00:11:25.560
that we don't turn the MOSS tool

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:28.650
into a a performance management

00:11:28.650 --> 00:11:29.820
'cause actually we need trust

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:32.400
to be owning this locally first.

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:34.020
So, but I think what we could do

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:35.160
is put some visibility on it

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:36.990
when we do the deep dive with the Board

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:38.520
and the measures that we're proposing

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:41.960
to bring on the performance report.

00:11:41.960 --> 00:11:44.070
We just need to just make
sure the data quality on that

00:11:44.070 --> 00:11:46.290
is as it should be before it comes through

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:48.000
to the performance pack.

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:49.530
- Okay, and we do need to accelerate

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:51.973
getting that meeting in that
we were going to organise.

00:11:51.973 --> 00:11:53.880
So that'd be good.

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:58.080
Okay, so I'll move us on to
the strategy committee, Paul.

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:02.160
- Yeah, so we had the second
strategy committee meeting,

00:12:02.160 --> 00:12:04.080
the first that I joined.

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:06.330
First thing we did was agree
the terms of reference,

00:12:06.330 --> 00:12:09.360
which I was to summarise very briefly,

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:11.430
I think a number one, to
make sure we have a committee

00:12:11.430 --> 00:12:13.080
that is focused on the future

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:14.277
and where we're trying to get to.

00:12:14.277 --> 00:12:15.180
And number two,

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:18.330
making sure that we've got
real coherence and rigour

00:12:18.330 --> 00:12:19.593
in all of the various trends of work

00:12:19.593 --> 00:12:21.480
that are taking place
across the organisation

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:23.180
to help get us from here to there.

00:12:24.390 --> 00:12:25.260
With that in mind,

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:26.610
practically that means the committee

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:28.583
will be looking at work

00:12:28.583 --> 00:12:30.870
that is led both within the strategy

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:33.540
and policy directorate
but also bringing papers

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:34.800
from other parts of the organisation

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:36.900
where that feels like a
good forum for discussion

00:12:36.900 --> 00:12:37.733
and that we can make sure

00:12:37.733 --> 00:12:40.680
that that join up is taking place.

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:43.140
And also we'll be looking
very much at live work,

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:45.723
not just signing off
pieces of work at the end.

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:48.660
Linked to that, critically that will mean

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:51.510
that we'll be covering the
sort of joint strategic agenda

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:53.790
across both NHS and the department,

00:12:53.790 --> 00:12:55.770
and we'll have colleagues
coming and presenting work

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:58.320
and we can make sure again
that work is joined up

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:00.780
by looking at it in that setting.

00:13:00.780 --> 00:13:04.500
And we do now have a very long pipeline

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:06.930
of current and planned work

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:08.761
that will be making its way through

00:13:08.761 --> 00:13:11.700
over the next months.

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:13.650
We spent some time looking substantively

00:13:13.650 --> 00:13:15.960
at two or three areas,

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:18.210
one quality strategy I won't revisit

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:19.650
'cause I think we've
agreed it's going to go

00:13:19.650 --> 00:13:22.050
solely through the NQB in future,

00:13:22.050 --> 00:13:24.390
second children and young people strategy,

00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:25.890
really important piece of work,

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:27.240
started to look at some of the data

00:13:27.240 --> 00:13:28.860
around current challenges,

00:13:28.860 --> 00:13:29.940
changing nature of demand,

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:31.950
fragmentation of services,

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:33.450
and discussed a little bit the importance

00:13:33.450 --> 00:13:35.490
of cross agency work as we finalise

00:13:35.490 --> 00:13:37.080
what we want to do in this space.

00:13:37.080 --> 00:13:38.280
And there's been some early discussions

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:41.460
with colleagues from DFE to
make sure that we're starting

00:13:41.460 --> 00:13:44.850
that conversation sufficiently early.

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:46.530
And then finally, we also spoke

00:13:46.530 --> 00:13:49.950
about the center-led commercial
intervention approach,

00:13:49.950 --> 00:13:51.900
and just again revisiting that question

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:53.160
of how we get right the balance

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:56.010
between ensuring a
degree of standardisation

00:13:56.010 --> 00:13:59.400
but also some local enablement as well.

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:01.560
- Okay, I think it would be good

00:14:01.560 --> 00:14:06.560
for full transparency if
we brought, next time,

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:08.850
a list of the different projects

00:14:08.850 --> 00:14:10.312
and where we think they are

00:14:10.312 --> 00:14:14.343
in both timing and sequencing
and prioritisation.

00:14:15.210 --> 00:14:16.320
'Cause I think it is...

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:17.790
Certainly I've found it quite helpful

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:20.943
to see all of the breadth of
thinking that is happening.

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:23.193
Any questions for Paul?

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:30.410
Okay, so go on to finance
and performance, Andrew.

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:35.070
- Thank you, so we met
on the 26th of January

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:36.360
to consider one item,

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:37.830
and that was the business case

00:14:37.830 --> 00:14:42.830
for 100 million pounds
investment for NHS Online,

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:45.840
which when fully developed,

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:48.960
will hopefully treat
around 8 million people

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:51.300
on an outpatient basis.

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:53.880
I think it's fair that
a number of the execs

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:55.470
have had several issues

00:14:55.470 --> 00:14:59.699
and we've said go back to the exec team

00:14:59.699 --> 00:15:01.710
and have a conversation with Jim

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:04.350
around presenting a kind
of final business case,

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:06.360
and we want to do that, Penny,

00:15:06.360 --> 00:15:09.720
by email correspondence
in the next few weeks.

00:15:09.720 --> 00:15:12.483
But the committee supported
proposals in principle.

00:15:13.650 --> 00:15:15.660
- Okay.
- Very good initiative.

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:19.080
- Yeah, does anyone have
any comments, questions?

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:21.020
No, okay, so then we're going

00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:24.450
to Data, Digital, and
Technology committee, Mark.

00:15:24.450 --> 00:15:25.283
- Thank you, Penny.

00:15:25.283 --> 00:15:29.220
So meeting was broadly
split into two areas.

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:32.730
One was critical national infrastructure

00:15:32.730 --> 00:15:34.143
and resilience and cyber,

00:15:35.430 --> 00:15:38.790
and the other bit was the
transformation of the system.

00:15:38.790 --> 00:15:40.050
Across both areas,

00:15:40.050 --> 00:15:43.560
the teams are making solid progress

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:47.163
in what is undoubtedly a
difficult situation for them.

00:15:48.450 --> 00:15:50.730
I think most useful for this Board

00:15:50.730 --> 00:15:53.640
is I think there were four themes

00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:56.943
that sort of came out repeatedly
across the various papers.

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:03.060
One, a transformation strategy

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:07.380
directly linked to the 10-year
plan clearly is underway,

00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:09.900
but it's currently at too earlier stage

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:12.750
to really build detailed
technology against it.

00:16:12.750 --> 00:16:16.530
And I think we need to be
mindful of not pushing too hard

00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:18.750
until we've got a really
business aligned strategy

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:20.250
about what we're trying to do.

00:16:21.510 --> 00:16:26.510
Two, over time the teams
have become conditioned

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:30.960
to silo working, and I think this culture

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:33.330
is making cross-cutting initiatives

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:36.420
which I think are pretty
fundamental to the 10-year plan,

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:37.950
quite hard to deliver,

00:16:37.950 --> 00:16:39.510
and I think we need to be mindful of that

00:16:39.510 --> 00:16:42.153
and probably some
interventions going forward.

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:47.643
In terms of spending
review and investment,

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:51.100
I think there's a real need to prioritise

00:16:52.410 --> 00:16:57.090
a subset of the programmes
which are now proven

00:16:57.090 --> 00:17:00.780
and have got really big
benefits associated to them.

00:17:00.780 --> 00:17:04.380
I know later in this meeting,

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:06.150
Elizabeth mentions Wayfinder,

00:17:06.150 --> 00:17:08.500
which in particular has
massively outperformed,

00:17:09.540 --> 00:17:12.030
very good for productivity,
very good for patients,

00:17:12.030 --> 00:17:14.040
and I think we should extract those cases

00:17:14.040 --> 00:17:15.600
and really push them forward

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:19.080
and get much more widespread adoption.

00:17:19.080 --> 00:17:23.010
And then finally, the recurring thing

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:24.000
we've already talked about,

00:17:24.000 --> 00:17:26.280
which is on the other side

00:17:26.280 --> 00:17:27.930
of the restructuring that's happening,

00:17:27.930 --> 00:17:30.120
we need to make sure we've
got the people and capability

00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:31.570
to actually deliver the plan.

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:34.650
- Yeah, okay.

00:17:34.650 --> 00:17:36.030
Jules, anything you'd like to add?

00:17:36.030 --> 00:17:36.863
- No.
- Okay.

00:17:38.970 --> 00:17:40.743
How frequently are you meeting?

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:44.520
Is it every two?

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:48.570
- We've probably every sort
of seven or eight weeks.

00:17:48.570 --> 00:17:49.770
- Yeah, okay.

00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:50.603
'Cause it feels like,

00:17:50.603 --> 00:17:53.700
you know, maintaining the momentum,

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:55.020
and really pushing on all the things

00:17:55.020 --> 00:17:58.260
that you're talking about feels
absolutely critical, yeah.

00:17:58.260 --> 00:18:00.060
- I think so, now we've got a clearer view

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:01.377
of what we need to do.

00:18:01.377 --> 00:18:03.390
- And is there anything else you need

00:18:03.390 --> 00:18:05.643
from us people around the room?

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:09.420
- I think the first point about

00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:13.680
that really business aligned view

00:18:13.680 --> 00:18:15.700
of the transformation strategy

00:18:17.190 --> 00:18:19.314
is probably the most important thing.

00:18:19.314 --> 00:18:20.147
- Okay, Jim.
- Yeah,

00:18:20.147 --> 00:18:20.980
and just to add to that,

00:18:20.980 --> 00:18:22.140
Mark and I spoke the other day,

00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:24.630
and Jules and I yesterday
as well, we've got...

00:18:24.630 --> 00:18:27.240
It feels like we're
trying to do an awful lot

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:28.803
with some huge investment.

00:18:29.730 --> 00:18:31.740
Some of the things we've
done over recent years

00:18:31.740 --> 00:18:33.900
have kind of pulled against each other

00:18:33.900 --> 00:18:35.160
but also are struggling a bit

00:18:35.160 --> 00:18:38.490
from the value for money
productivity impact perspective.

00:18:38.490 --> 00:18:39.840
I think the thing we've got to do

00:18:39.840 --> 00:18:42.240
is help Mark, Jules, our the colleagues,

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:43.770
prioritise appropriately

00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:45.000
and make sure we're fully aligned

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:48.270
with the operational
imperatives that we've got.

00:18:48.270 --> 00:18:50.040
And that feels really hard

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:51.367
'cause it's very hard to
say we'll not do that,

00:18:51.367 --> 00:18:54.060
we'll not do this, this will go later.

00:18:54.060 --> 00:18:56.130
But I think we are going to
have to get into that territory

00:18:56.130 --> 00:18:59.400
'cause it's stretched very thin,

00:18:59.400 --> 00:19:02.640
and it feels very disparate at times.

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:04.920
- Yeah, Paul?

00:19:04.920 --> 00:19:07.680
- Yeah, just I think important to say,

00:19:07.680 --> 00:19:09.600
the operating model as
it evolves will help,

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:12.429
but I think it's important
that we don't wait for that.

00:19:12.429 --> 00:19:14.457
And I think in the short
term what that means is,

00:19:14.457 --> 00:19:17.310
and this is a part that
we actually had last week,

00:19:17.310 --> 00:19:20.490
as we start doing more strategy work,

00:19:20.490 --> 00:19:22.290
led out of my directorate

00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:24.150
rather than completing that work

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:25.800
and then handing it over to colleagues

00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:27.607
in the transformation director and saying,

00:19:27.607 --> 00:19:28.710
"Where's the tech bit of this?"

00:19:28.710 --> 00:19:30.900
We're going to start putting
more integrated teams

00:19:30.900 --> 00:19:32.670
from the start so that people that join up

00:19:32.670 --> 00:19:33.773
built in from the beginning.

00:19:33.773 --> 00:19:34.702
- Okay, Elizabeth.

00:19:34.702 --> 00:19:36.000
- I would just echo that point.

00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:38.640
- Yeah!
- So working with Jules

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:42.090
and the team around,
actually, and Meghana,

00:19:42.090 --> 00:19:43.890
on the Modern Service Framework.

00:19:43.890 --> 00:19:44.970
So how do we build a tech

00:19:44.970 --> 00:19:48.660
into the service transformation up front

00:19:48.660 --> 00:19:51.720
and make sure we've got
the incentives aligned.

00:19:51.720 --> 00:19:54.420
But it does mean it's
reliant on individuals

00:19:54.420 --> 00:19:57.140
rather than a systematic way of doing it.

00:19:57.140 --> 00:19:58.590
And I think we need to think

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:01.080
about how we do that a bit more,

00:20:01.080 --> 00:20:04.170
especially given the fact that

00:20:04.170 --> 00:20:07.200
so many people are dispersed
across NHS England,

00:20:07.200 --> 00:20:09.390
they might actually not
know what's going on.

00:20:09.390 --> 00:20:11.460
And then the other thing for me is,

00:20:11.460 --> 00:20:12.810
when I look in the tech space

00:20:12.810 --> 00:20:14.880
and I know we'll be talking
about this afternoon,

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:15.900
there are obviously,

00:20:15.900 --> 00:20:17.310
and there's some in
the productivity paper,

00:20:17.310 --> 00:20:19.350
so no regrets moves.

00:20:19.350 --> 00:20:22.380
So let's just pick those up and prioritise

00:20:22.380 --> 00:20:25.140
and really support the team to do that.

00:20:25.140 --> 00:20:28.290
- So again, I think it,
on base of transparency,

00:20:28.290 --> 00:20:29.670
it'd be good to have probably,

00:20:29.670 --> 00:20:32.760
because if we look at
particularly quality,

00:20:32.760 --> 00:20:34.200
finance, and performance,

00:20:34.200 --> 00:20:35.463
we pick those up now when we come

00:20:35.463 --> 00:20:36.447
into the performance report

00:20:36.447 --> 00:20:38.103
and we have some data on them.

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:41.160
I think in this space,
it'd be good to have

00:20:41.160 --> 00:20:44.340
a sort of high level
overview as to where we are

00:20:44.340 --> 00:20:46.920
and bring it to this
Board very explicitly.

00:20:46.920 --> 00:20:48.630
You know, roughly here's
how we're managing

00:20:48.630 --> 00:20:51.540
to progress the big pieces of work,

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:53.730
I think, would be helpful to make sure,

00:20:53.730 --> 00:20:56.490
'cause it is about joining
up across everybody.

00:20:56.490 --> 00:20:58.290
So to have everybody see that

00:20:58.290 --> 00:21:00.090
and make it a bit more visible,

00:21:00.090 --> 00:21:02.033
I think the rest of the country as well.

00:21:03.240 --> 00:21:06.910
Okay, anything else anyone wants to raise?

00:21:06.910 --> 00:21:08.520
Okay, good, thank you all very much.

00:21:08.520 --> 00:21:11.250
Thank you everyone for the
work on those committees.

00:21:11.250 --> 00:21:12.240
Jim

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:13.073
- Yeah, thanks Penny.

00:21:13.073 --> 00:21:14.730
So it feels like a lot
to talk about today.

00:21:14.730 --> 00:21:16.350
I'll go as quickly as I can

00:21:16.350 --> 00:21:17.610
'cause there is a lot to digest.

00:21:17.610 --> 00:21:21.183
First point I would make
is when we met last time,

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:23.310
colleagues, remember we're looking

00:21:23.310 --> 00:21:26.190
at a very vertical rise in flu

00:21:26.190 --> 00:21:29.310
looking forward in the
winter industrial action.

00:21:29.310 --> 00:21:31.470
Everyone was very anxious
about what that meant,

00:21:31.470 --> 00:21:35.460
and I think we saw a very,
very positive public,

00:21:35.460 --> 00:21:36.990
and staff response on flu

00:21:36.990 --> 00:21:40.020
that really adjusted
the trajectory of flu,

00:21:40.020 --> 00:21:42.360
and made everybody's lives better

00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:44.460
for the weeks that followed the service

00:21:44.460 --> 00:21:47.100
did its usual management
of industrial action

00:21:47.100 --> 00:21:48.180
very, very well.

00:21:48.180 --> 00:21:52.320
Again maintaining very
high levels of performance

00:21:52.320 --> 00:21:54.240
through that process.

00:21:54.240 --> 00:21:57.570
Which also, I think,
give us a decent chance

00:21:57.570 --> 00:22:00.780
of achieving our occupancy objective

00:22:00.780 --> 00:22:01.830
in the run of Christmas,

00:22:01.830 --> 00:22:05.730
which again led by
Sarah-Jane and colleagues,

00:22:05.730 --> 00:22:09.390
has given us a remarkably
better performance

00:22:09.390 --> 00:22:11.490
on things like ambulance handovers

00:22:11.490 --> 00:22:14.520
and category 2 response times.

00:22:14.520 --> 00:22:15.480
Alongside that,

00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:20.190
there's an awful lot of
subjective anecdotal reports

00:22:20.190 --> 00:22:22.920
of how much better it felt in that period

00:22:22.920 --> 00:22:25.050
in between Christmas and New Year,

00:22:25.050 --> 00:22:27.780
and few of us have spoken
to clinical colleagues

00:22:27.780 --> 00:22:29.580
and operational colleagues
over recent weeks,

00:22:29.580 --> 00:22:32.280
which is all kind of reinforced that.

00:22:32.280 --> 00:22:35.010
And I think hopefully
will give everybody heart

00:22:35.010 --> 00:22:37.590
of what can be achieved going forward.

00:22:37.590 --> 00:22:39.613
We're still clearly not at
the levels of performance

00:22:39.613 --> 00:22:41.610
we want to be just yet,

00:22:41.610 --> 00:22:44.880
but you know, there should
be confidence in that.

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:48.630
In that, we still do have
an unacceptable position

00:22:48.630 --> 00:22:50.730
on corridor care in some places.

00:22:50.730 --> 00:22:52.890
So again, a few of us met

00:22:52.890 --> 00:22:55.170
with a Corridor Care Coalition last week

00:22:55.170 --> 00:22:57.390
and we've agreed to set or in the process

00:22:57.390 --> 00:22:58.620
of agreeing a set of actions

00:22:58.620 --> 00:23:01.410
that will allow us to change gear

00:23:01.410 --> 00:23:05.040
and try to really motor
around eradicate in something

00:23:05.040 --> 00:23:08.250
that should never have
been allowed to take place.

00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:09.930
And doing that with colleagues

00:23:09.930 --> 00:23:11.520
who've managed to do that

00:23:11.520 --> 00:23:13.320
in this last year or so as well.

00:23:13.320 --> 00:23:16.050
So I think a good example
of sort of mixed picture,

00:23:16.050 --> 00:23:18.300
some really big strides forward,

00:23:18.300 --> 00:23:19.800
but still not where we want to be,

00:23:19.800 --> 00:23:23.280
but hopefully people can
take confidence from that.

00:23:23.280 --> 00:23:25.560
You'll see through the
day progress as well

00:23:25.560 --> 00:23:28.290
on an elective performance,
GP satisfaction,

00:23:28.290 --> 00:23:29.550
you know, those sorts of things as well.

00:23:29.550 --> 00:23:30.690
So lots of good things.

00:23:30.690 --> 00:23:31.890
It does feel at times

00:23:31.890 --> 00:23:36.030
when the NHS manages to achieve something,

00:23:36.030 --> 00:23:40.110
we then have an argument about
how and whether it's real,

00:23:40.110 --> 00:23:43.110
and you know, it's very draining

00:23:43.110 --> 00:23:44.700
for colleagues on the ground,

00:23:44.700 --> 00:23:46.410
and it's understandable,

00:23:46.410 --> 00:23:48.420
we all accept the challenge and stuff,

00:23:48.420 --> 00:23:50.940
but it would be really nice
to be able to celebrate

00:23:50.940 --> 00:23:54.180
fantastic effort in the
service when it's justified,

00:23:54.180 --> 00:23:55.517
and we will continue to try and do that.

00:23:55.517 --> 00:23:57.960
'Cause I think especially
this last few months

00:23:57.960 --> 00:23:59.580
you've really seen people digging in

00:23:59.580 --> 00:24:01.980
and pulling together and
pulling out the stops

00:24:01.980 --> 00:24:03.780
and making some really big leaps

00:24:03.780 --> 00:24:07.380
as we discussed in the chief
execs event last week as well.

00:24:07.380 --> 00:24:09.030
Not accepting we're not
where we want to be,

00:24:09.030 --> 00:24:10.890
but really great progress.

00:24:10.890 --> 00:24:14.670
Also, well done Mark and the
team on the cancer strategy.

00:24:14.670 --> 00:24:16.480
So again, I think a good example of,

00:24:16.480 --> 00:24:18.090
we're not where we want to be,

00:24:18.090 --> 00:24:19.530
we want to be very ambitious,

00:24:19.530 --> 00:24:21.510
but it has to be realistic,

00:24:21.510 --> 00:24:23.970
and very strong buy-in
in the process as well.

00:24:23.970 --> 00:24:25.950
So I think that's very difficult balance

00:24:25.950 --> 00:24:28.530
to strike in these sorts of processes.

00:24:28.530 --> 00:24:32.610
But well done, it looks like
that's been struck well.

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:34.620
We've now got to deliver it.

00:24:34.620 --> 00:24:37.347
So the hard bit ahead.

00:24:37.347 --> 00:24:39.870
And then really good chief
execs event last week

00:24:39.870 --> 00:24:41.640
where we're talking a
bit about ending the year

00:24:41.640 --> 00:24:43.200
well, starting next year,

00:24:43.200 --> 00:24:45.960
remember we're in a
multi-year plan process,

00:24:45.960 --> 00:24:47.850
but also we're not going to get through

00:24:47.850 --> 00:24:48.840
what we want to get through

00:24:48.840 --> 00:24:51.060
with minor incremental changes.

00:24:51.060 --> 00:24:52.440
This is about finding ways

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:56.340
of making some big leaps
in this next period.

00:24:56.340 --> 00:24:58.590
Some justify challenges in that.

00:24:58.590 --> 00:24:59.670
It's already been mentioned

00:24:59.670 --> 00:25:01.170
about we've got to broaden our sites

00:25:01.170 --> 00:25:03.900
beyond the hospital
walls and in our metrics,

00:25:03.900 --> 00:25:04.980
our discussion of quality,

00:25:04.980 --> 00:25:06.510
our discussion of performance, et cetera,

00:25:06.510 --> 00:25:08.670
and mental health and community services

00:25:08.670 --> 00:25:10.170
and various other things,

00:25:10.170 --> 00:25:13.200
but really great progress.

00:25:13.200 --> 00:25:16.050
Nearly finally, we have
made an appointment

00:25:16.050 --> 00:25:19.680
for the leader of mental
health services nationally.

00:25:19.680 --> 00:25:23.160
We're still going through the
usual processes of approval,

00:25:23.160 --> 00:25:26.040
but hope to be able to
announce that very soon,

00:25:26.040 --> 00:25:27.480
hopefully next week.

00:25:27.480 --> 00:25:30.030
And also we're in the process

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:32.130
of agreeing with a colleague
to come and help us

00:25:32.130 --> 00:25:34.560
with the new hospitals programme

00:25:34.560 --> 00:25:37.320
that there's need, we do need help with

00:25:37.320 --> 00:25:38.760
in terms of driving forward

00:25:38.760 --> 00:25:42.780
some of the recommendations
in the NEO report

00:25:42.780 --> 00:25:45.150
with various other concerns
we've got from the service.

00:25:45.150 --> 00:25:46.350
So very important and again,

00:25:46.350 --> 00:25:47.970
hopefully we'll be able
to say more about that

00:25:47.970 --> 00:25:49.710
in the next couple of weeks.

00:25:49.710 --> 00:25:51.180
Very, very finally then,

00:25:51.180 --> 00:25:54.870
so our staff started the day
in Southampton Hospital today

00:25:54.870 --> 00:25:58.050
who, if you colleagues are
hopefully aware of this,

00:25:58.050 --> 00:26:01.080
had a massive fire at the weekend.

00:26:01.080 --> 00:26:05.970
They evacuated around 500
patients in 40 minutes.

00:26:05.970 --> 00:26:10.970
So that's more than a normal
DGH evacuated in 40 minutes,

00:26:11.250 --> 00:26:13.140
with a really terrible fire

00:26:13.140 --> 00:26:14.370
that ripped through the place.

00:26:14.370 --> 00:26:18.390
Took an awful lot of
stabilising on the day.

00:26:18.390 --> 00:26:21.300
Really, really fantastic response

00:26:21.300 --> 00:26:22.893
within the organisation.

00:26:23.790 --> 00:26:25.140
I've seen colleagues today

00:26:25.140 --> 00:26:29.490
who are still very much
processing what happened,

00:26:29.490 --> 00:26:33.960
different stages of
emotional response going on,

00:26:33.960 --> 00:26:36.030
completely understandably.

00:26:36.030 --> 00:26:39.003
What was very palpable
was the sense of spirit,

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:41.880
the pulling together, the purpose,

00:26:41.880 --> 00:26:45.330
single purpose, care for the patients.

00:26:45.330 --> 00:26:46.163
I've heard stories

00:26:46.163 --> 00:26:49.260
that they have colleagues
running into the flames

00:26:49.260 --> 00:26:51.420
and into the smoke to rescue patients.

00:26:51.420 --> 00:26:54.510
It's really, really a humbling experience.

00:26:54.510 --> 00:26:57.060
We've got some things we
need to help them with today

00:26:57.060 --> 00:26:58.680
in terms of system response,

00:26:58.680 --> 00:27:01.440
they've still got a lot of
patients in their system

00:27:01.440 --> 00:27:04.800
who can't be found beds within
the damage that's being left.

00:27:04.800 --> 00:27:07.380
So we're going to need a
bit of a push to get those,

00:27:07.380 --> 00:27:08.850
especially those that require care

00:27:08.850 --> 00:27:10.020
in a social care setting

00:27:10.020 --> 00:27:11.610
to get where they need to be.

00:27:11.610 --> 00:27:12.900
So that'll be acted upon,

00:27:12.900 --> 00:27:14.700
and it will take time for them to recover,

00:27:14.700 --> 00:27:17.760
not just operationally, but emotionally,

00:27:17.760 --> 00:27:20.580
psychologically, you know,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:27:20.580 --> 00:27:23.340
It is an organisation that we met recently

00:27:23.340 --> 00:27:25.050
because of its financial challenges

00:27:25.050 --> 00:27:26.310
and various other things.

00:27:26.310 --> 00:27:30.780
So I think today, really,
really timely reminder

00:27:30.780 --> 00:27:32.460
that even when you've
got these challenges,

00:27:32.460 --> 00:27:35.970
the NHS is at its best at these times

00:27:35.970 --> 00:27:37.710
and a really fantastic example

00:27:37.710 --> 00:27:40.740
of what the NHS can do
at these difficult times.

00:27:40.740 --> 00:27:42.930
We should never take
that here for granted.

00:27:42.930 --> 00:27:46.860
So just want to place on
record acknowledgement of that

00:27:46.860 --> 00:27:49.067
and thanks to the
colleagues I've met today

00:27:49.067 --> 00:27:50.640
and others involved in that,

00:27:50.640 --> 00:27:53.280
it is really, really impressive.

00:27:53.280 --> 00:27:56.160
But they won't recover
quickly without our help

00:27:56.160 --> 00:27:58.470
and we've got a lot to do to support that.

00:27:58.470 --> 00:27:59.370
So I'll stop there, Penny,

00:27:59.370 --> 00:28:00.776
I'm happy to take any questions

00:28:00.776 --> 00:28:01.860
if there are any.
- Okay.

00:28:01.860 --> 00:28:02.693
Thank you Jim.

00:28:02.693 --> 00:28:05.340
So I think first off, yes,
we should all acknowledge

00:28:05.340 --> 00:28:07.230
and thank that work in Southampton.

00:28:07.230 --> 00:28:10.920
I think certainly when you
were telling me about it

00:28:10.920 --> 00:28:13.740
on Monday, it was worse
than I think came over

00:28:13.740 --> 00:28:15.660
in the news on Sunday night.

00:28:15.660 --> 00:28:18.750
And as you say, very, very impressive

00:28:18.750 --> 00:28:20.850
local response to that.

00:28:20.850 --> 00:28:22.320
I think also thanks to you

00:28:22.320 --> 00:28:24.120
and all of our exec colleagues

00:28:24.120 --> 00:28:26.433
for the work over the
last couple of months.

00:28:27.480 --> 00:28:30.000
Certainly, we're clearly
not out of winter yet,

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:32.010
it's still only first week of February,

00:28:32.010 --> 00:28:34.950
but it feels more positive

00:28:34.950 --> 00:28:37.050
than I think we were concerned about

00:28:37.050 --> 00:28:38.160
at the beginning of December.

00:28:38.160 --> 00:28:40.710
So thank you all, I know
that was a lot of work,

00:28:40.710 --> 00:28:42.000
again, particularly preparing

00:28:42.000 --> 00:28:44.700
for the industrial action and so on.

00:28:44.700 --> 00:28:48.750
And a final point would
be, I think your balance

00:28:48.750 --> 00:28:50.670
between saying we are making progress

00:28:50.670 --> 00:28:52.213
but we've still got a way to go,

00:28:52.213 --> 00:28:55.440
is absolutely right
and we should flow that

00:28:55.440 --> 00:28:57.240
through really most of our conversations.

00:28:57.240 --> 00:28:59.160
We're definitely going
in the right direction

00:28:59.160 --> 00:29:02.923
but we are by no means
where we want to be.

00:29:04.470 --> 00:29:08.220
Any comments, questions for Jim? Andrew.

00:29:08.220 --> 00:29:11.340
- So Jim, what's been the
success around the reduction

00:29:11.340 --> 00:29:13.290
or improvement in ambulance handovers?

00:29:13.290 --> 00:29:16.863
What do you think has been
the real thing that's turned?

00:29:17.790 --> 00:29:20.187
'Cause we've struggled with
this for several years now,

00:29:20.187 --> 00:29:22.950
and we've seen more
improvement this winter

00:29:22.950 --> 00:29:26.940
than ever before,
certainly in recent times.

00:29:26.940 --> 00:29:28.741
- Sarah-Jane.
- I'm happy to go, yeah.

00:29:28.741 --> 00:29:33.000
Just a real sense of
ownership in the Trusts

00:29:33.000 --> 00:29:35.730
that if we don't offload
people from ambulances,

00:29:35.730 --> 00:29:37.560
get them handed over that
we are not going to be able

00:29:37.560 --> 00:29:39.480
to get the ambulance back
out into the community

00:29:39.480 --> 00:29:40.920
to help someone else.

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:42.600
And almost all of the strategies

00:29:42.600 --> 00:29:44.130
that have worked really well

00:29:44.130 --> 00:29:46.680
have been where it's
been really personalised

00:29:46.680 --> 00:29:50.430
to these are our patients,
this is our community.

00:29:50.430 --> 00:29:53.220
Imagine if that is your auntie, brother,

00:29:53.220 --> 00:29:55.200
you know, et cetera,
that needs the ambulance,

00:29:55.200 --> 00:29:57.180
and we can't because they're stuck.

00:29:57.180 --> 00:29:59.190
So almost all is that,

00:29:59.190 --> 00:30:02.010
but there are is the sort
of leadership momentum.

00:30:02.010 --> 00:30:03.987
But sat behind that,
we are starting to see

00:30:03.987 --> 00:30:05.850
and I fully accept everything

00:30:05.850 --> 00:30:08.100
that everybody said
about general progress.

00:30:08.100 --> 00:30:10.710
But we are starting to
see the return, I think,

00:30:10.710 --> 00:30:13.440
of more the sort of general

00:30:13.440 --> 00:30:17.820
clinical operational management
skill of improving flow.

00:30:17.820 --> 00:30:19.740
So we are seeing organisations

00:30:19.740 --> 00:30:21.060
that are genuinely doing it

00:30:21.060 --> 00:30:23.820
through the pull out of the department,

00:30:23.820 --> 00:30:26.730
through into the wards,
creating the space,

00:30:26.730 --> 00:30:28.410
some of these continuous flow models

00:30:28.410 --> 00:30:30.750
that we know work quite well.

00:30:30.750 --> 00:30:33.000
So it's underpinned by that,

00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:36.850
but it always starts with
a decision from the Board

00:30:37.872 --> 00:30:39.750
that we are not going
to be an organisation

00:30:39.750 --> 00:30:40.770
that holds ambulances.

00:30:40.770 --> 00:30:44.070
And to go to Jim's point on corridors,

00:30:44.070 --> 00:30:46.530
it's a very similar thing there.

00:30:46.530 --> 00:30:49.620
It's quite easy to say
it's a whole system issue

00:30:49.620 --> 00:30:50.670
and we've got all these things

00:30:50.670 --> 00:30:51.600
that we need to work through,

00:30:51.600 --> 00:30:55.200
but we heard at that three places

00:30:55.200 --> 00:30:57.540
that have eradicated it
over the past few months,

00:30:57.540 --> 00:31:00.810
and it all started with a
Board decision that said

00:31:00.810 --> 00:31:03.810
that is not what we're going
to have for our communities,

00:31:03.810 --> 00:31:05.970
and it's not going to be
easy and you know, et cetera.

00:31:05.970 --> 00:31:06.803
But it's something

00:31:06.803 --> 00:31:09.630
that we are absolutely going
to focus our attention on.

00:31:09.630 --> 00:31:10.920
Just finally, the other thing

00:31:10.920 --> 00:31:12.570
that's been different
this winter, I think,

00:31:12.570 --> 00:31:15.990
is the general level of
resilience has been stronger.

00:31:15.990 --> 00:31:17.280
So we have had bad days,

00:31:17.280 --> 00:31:18.810
there's no doubt about it.

00:31:18.810 --> 00:31:20.640
But in the past, if we've had a bad day

00:31:20.640 --> 00:31:23.490
it's tended to rumble
on to become a bad week

00:31:23.490 --> 00:31:25.020
and then it, you know, cascades,

00:31:25.020 --> 00:31:28.140
and so even where we've had a really,

00:31:28.140 --> 00:31:32.640
a day where we've struggled,
we've come back quickly.

00:31:32.640 --> 00:31:35.880
And on the extremes,

00:31:35.880 --> 00:31:38.430
the longest day we've had full stop

00:31:38.430 --> 00:31:39.840
for a category 2 response time

00:31:39.840 --> 00:31:41.700
has been just over 50 minutes,

00:31:41.700 --> 00:31:43.020
whereas in previous years,

00:31:43.020 --> 00:31:44.910
our bad days have looked like approaching

00:31:44.910 --> 00:31:46.830
an hour and a half as you know.

00:31:46.830 --> 00:31:49.110
So it's not just the
averages that has improved,

00:31:49.110 --> 00:31:51.450
but we've really trimmed
off on the extreme.

00:31:51.450 --> 00:31:53.280
But it is a huge leadership effort

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:55.083
backed up by that process,

00:31:56.070 --> 00:31:57.633
sort of redesigned behind it.

00:31:58.520 --> 00:32:00.120
- Okay, I'll take two questions

00:32:00.120 --> 00:32:02.220
from by David and Elizabeth.
- Yeah, it was more

00:32:02.220 --> 00:32:04.680
of a response, and I was going to say,

00:32:04.680 --> 00:32:05.850
we're sitting in the South West,

00:32:05.850 --> 00:32:07.650
and actually the performance of SWAST

00:32:07.650 --> 00:32:09.570
shouldn't go unnoted.

00:32:09.570 --> 00:32:11.910
I dunno if you want to say something, Sue.

00:32:11.910 --> 00:32:15.270
And actually, I live in the South West,

00:32:15.270 --> 00:32:18.030
as many of you know.
- So just a bit of bias.

00:32:18.030 --> 00:32:20.940
- No, look at the data,
Penny, look at the data.

00:32:20.940 --> 00:32:23.820
But what I would say also is,

00:32:23.820 --> 00:32:25.740
so I wasn't on call over the weekend,

00:32:25.740 --> 00:32:29.040
but it just reminded me
actually the whole NHS

00:32:29.040 --> 00:32:31.440
and how we've got porous
boundaries of regions.

00:32:31.440 --> 00:32:34.440
So recognising the work
that Dorset did and Sue

00:32:34.440 --> 00:32:38.160
and Trevor in the regional
team over the weekend,

00:32:38.160 --> 00:32:41.400
helping in of diverts and
just managing that flow.

00:32:41.400 --> 00:32:42.870
It's really impressive to see.

00:32:42.870 --> 00:32:44.910
But I dunno if you want to
say something about the work

00:32:44.910 --> 00:32:46.710
that SWAST has been doing Sue.

00:32:46.710 --> 00:32:48.210
- Yes, certainly happy to,

00:32:48.210 --> 00:32:50.400
and I'd agree with Sarah-Jane.

00:32:50.400 --> 00:32:54.810
It's a Board decision that it
becomes a zero-tolerance too,

00:32:54.810 --> 00:32:57.480
but it's also looking at that dynamic risk

00:32:57.480 --> 00:33:00.270
and realising that you
may be able to mitigate

00:33:00.270 --> 00:33:02.820
the risk in your particular part of it

00:33:02.820 --> 00:33:04.860
but somebody else then has a bigger risk

00:33:04.860 --> 00:33:06.210
as a result of that.

00:33:06.210 --> 00:33:08.370
So looking at it as a dynamic risk

00:33:08.370 --> 00:33:11.100
as opposed to an organisational risk.

00:33:11.100 --> 00:33:14.520
I think it's also then about
getting clinical buy-in,

00:33:14.520 --> 00:33:16.950
and I think we've eradicated corridor care

00:33:16.950 --> 00:33:19.290
in one or two of our hospitals,

00:33:19.290 --> 00:33:21.240
and that's been a commitment to staff

00:33:21.240 --> 00:33:22.620
who've said that's the worst.

00:33:22.620 --> 00:33:24.570
So it's again, it's about staff experience

00:33:24.570 --> 00:33:27.510
as well as patient experience
and listening to that,

00:33:27.510 --> 00:33:30.180
and making sure that that
then gets fed through

00:33:30.180 --> 00:33:32.464
from a Board level as a commitment.

00:33:32.464 --> 00:33:34.170
- Okay, David.

00:33:34.170 --> 00:33:36.870
- It sounds incredibly
pre-planned but it isn't.

00:33:36.870 --> 00:33:38.670
But just to support the
last three comments,

00:33:38.670 --> 00:33:40.290
when we've been doing the last round

00:33:40.290 --> 00:33:42.870
of particular regional
performance reviews,

00:33:42.870 --> 00:33:46.230
regions really do feel like
they have a fantastic grip

00:33:46.230 --> 00:33:48.420
of supporting their ambulance colleagues,

00:33:48.420 --> 00:33:50.760
and it is at least the
two or three regions

00:33:50.760 --> 00:33:53.070
we've seen dramatic
turnaround and real pride

00:33:53.070 --> 00:33:54.690
in the performance of
their ambulance services.

00:33:54.690 --> 00:33:56.760
So great credit to the regions as well

00:33:56.760 --> 00:33:58.907
and their leadership.
- Okay, so great.

00:33:59.870 --> 00:34:01.740
So to continue the progress,

00:34:01.740 --> 00:34:02.700
as opposed to make the link up

00:34:02.700 --> 00:34:04.740
with where we were before,

00:34:04.740 --> 00:34:07.143
talking about the technology committee,

00:34:08.160 --> 00:34:12.570
when do we think we will
get the tech that we need

00:34:12.570 --> 00:34:15.690
into the emergency care pathway,

00:34:15.690 --> 00:34:17.910
so that we don't have
what currently happens

00:34:17.910 --> 00:34:19.350
of ambulance staff turn up

00:34:19.350 --> 00:34:22.950
and have an A&amp;E nurse
typing with one finger the,

00:34:22.950 --> 00:34:25.890
you know, the notes in
terms of what's happened

00:34:25.890 --> 00:34:27.210
to the patient,

00:34:27.210 --> 00:34:28.770
build on the great work that we've had

00:34:28.770 --> 00:34:30.897
with things like EECGs and so on.

00:34:30.897 --> 00:34:34.560
And then take that tech the
whole way through the pathway.

00:34:34.560 --> 00:34:36.000
I mean, that's the join up

00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:37.322
that we're all looking to see.

00:34:39.780 --> 00:34:41.910
- We saw some brilliance for the FDP

00:34:41.910 --> 00:34:45.120
where they were using,
you know, AI and stuff

00:34:45.120 --> 00:34:48.750
to predict when the
ambulance was coming in,

00:34:48.750 --> 00:34:51.060
send the messages
through into the pathway.

00:34:51.060 --> 00:34:52.920
So it would seem like
it'd be a really good tool

00:34:52.920 --> 00:34:55.830
for the hospital to use
in terms of triaging

00:34:55.830 --> 00:34:56.663
ahead of time.

00:34:56.663 --> 00:34:58.590
- And how many trusts is that in?

00:34:58.590 --> 00:35:00.005
- I don't know this was an idea.

00:35:00.005 --> 00:35:02.738
So it's just-
- Oh okay, none, okay.

00:35:06.157 --> 00:35:09.060
- Just to pick up on
Sarah-Jane's point just to flag,

00:35:09.060 --> 00:35:11.253
one of the highest priority pieces of work

00:35:11.253 --> 00:35:13.110
that we've got progressing at the moment

00:35:13.110 --> 00:35:15.660
is the medium term UEC strategy,

00:35:15.660 --> 00:35:18.450
and that will be the place
where we're making sure

00:35:18.450 --> 00:35:20.340
that how do we get beyond

00:35:20.340 --> 00:35:22.200
really good operational discipline

00:35:22.200 --> 00:35:23.670
and performance sprints

00:35:23.670 --> 00:35:26.430
and into genuine technology
enabled transformation.

00:35:26.430 --> 00:35:29.700
And so I think we should
see that coming together,

00:35:29.700 --> 00:35:31.950
at least as a plan in
the next couple of months

00:35:31.950 --> 00:35:34.380
and then delivery.

00:35:34.380 --> 00:35:37.277
- I think our next big leap,
Penny, in this space is,

00:35:37.277 --> 00:35:39.330
and it'll be a big part
of the corridor care plan,

00:35:39.330 --> 00:35:42.690
when we see it, is that real concentration

00:35:42.690 --> 00:35:45.810
on frail older people
that we are still bringing

00:35:45.810 --> 00:35:47.980
in too many numbers to hospital

00:35:49.170 --> 00:35:52.740
often because there's not been
a care coordination ahead.

00:35:52.740 --> 00:35:54.690
If we could really get into that group,

00:35:54.690 --> 00:35:57.690
it not only helps response
times the handover,

00:35:57.690 --> 00:35:59.670
reduction in corridor
care and everything else,

00:35:59.670 --> 00:36:01.380
but it unlocks the key to so many

00:36:01.380 --> 00:36:03.570
of the efficiency and productivity issues.

00:36:03.570 --> 00:36:05.400
So that's going to be
one of our big focuses

00:36:05.400 --> 00:36:08.190
working with Claire and team.

00:36:08.190 --> 00:36:09.660
