we should come back with
a paper at some point

00:46:24.870 --> 00:46:26.940
about what we're going to do about that.

00:46:26.940 --> 00:46:28.543
And I know lots of
colleagues are working on it,

00:46:28.543 --> 00:46:30.747
but things like the SDEC coding

00:46:30.747 --> 00:46:32.100
and you know various other things

00:46:32.100 --> 00:46:34.110
do cause some wrinkles in our data.

00:46:34.110 --> 00:46:36.750
Second point, there is a
lot more discussion now

00:46:36.750 --> 00:46:39.180
going on about quality,
broadening the metrics,

00:46:39.180 --> 00:46:40.830
as well alongside the performance stuff.

00:46:40.830 --> 00:46:42.630
So does feel like we've opened up

00:46:42.630 --> 00:46:43.950
an appetite a little bit here

00:46:43.950 --> 00:46:46.100
that it's going to
helpfully, it's going to take

00:46:46.100 --> 00:46:47.820
a bit to match,

00:46:47.820 --> 00:46:51.390
but the main point was
that, at a trust level,

00:46:51.390 --> 00:46:53.160
when you're do having
this kind of discussion,

00:46:53.160 --> 00:46:55.920
it's easier to connect the curiosity

00:46:55.920 --> 00:46:57.390
and getting behind the numbers

00:46:57.390 --> 00:46:59.520
'cause you can go and meet with staff,

00:46:59.520 --> 00:47:02.430
you can sit in complaints discussions,

00:47:02.430 --> 00:47:05.130
you can do walkabouts, you
know those sorts of things.

00:47:05.130 --> 00:47:07.080
Not necessarily for today,

00:47:07.080 --> 00:47:09.090
but we need to think
about how we as a Board,

00:47:09.090 --> 00:47:11.220
as a National Board, become more curious

00:47:11.220 --> 00:47:13.890
and try and get behind the data a bit more

00:47:13.890 --> 00:47:15.570
because you do really feel

00:47:15.570 --> 00:47:17.097
quite a long way from it, yeah.

00:47:17.097 --> 00:47:18.214
- Are you sure?

00:47:18.214 --> 00:47:21.529
- Yeah, absolutely.
- Okay, all right.

00:47:21.529 --> 00:47:22.362
You heard it here

00:47:22.362 --> 00:47:24.221
Obviously when you were on holiday, Penny.

00:47:24.221 --> 00:47:26.094
But yeah.

00:47:26.094 --> 00:47:28.623
- So I've got Claire and then Duncan.

00:47:29.820 --> 00:47:30.690
- I want to make a comment

00:47:30.690 --> 00:47:31.700
but I can do you want to
pick up the diabetes bit

00:47:31.700 --> 00:47:34.170
at the end, Penny, I'll
do that as we go through.

00:47:34.170 --> 00:47:36.533
- Maybe pick it up at the end, David.

00:47:36.533 --> 00:47:37.520
- That was what I was going
to make a comment about.

00:47:37.520 --> 00:47:39.510
So it was just to explain
that the importance

00:47:39.510 --> 00:47:42.960
of the reduction in the
antibiotic use in children.

00:47:42.960 --> 00:47:44.760
So at the moment, that is our only metric

00:47:44.760 --> 00:47:46.530
that we've got that is a
proxy for how we're doing

00:47:46.530 --> 00:47:48.900
against improving
antimicrobial resistance,

00:47:48.900 --> 00:47:51.093
which is a massive threat globally.

00:47:53.490 --> 00:47:54.420
It's really positive,

00:47:54.420 --> 00:47:56.160
we've been stuck for a very long time,

00:47:56.160 --> 00:47:58.350
and actually over the
last sort of few months,

00:47:58.350 --> 00:48:01.380
we've finally started to make progress.

00:48:01.380 --> 00:48:03.810
It is part of a much bigger picture

00:48:03.810 --> 00:48:06.240
about how we tackle infections.

00:48:06.240 --> 00:48:08.370
We had the CMO report recently,

00:48:08.370 --> 00:48:09.930
and I think it ties in particularly

00:48:09.930 --> 00:48:12.960
with some of the other
bits that are going on.

00:48:12.960 --> 00:48:15.360
So the CMO highlighted that
we are not treating infections

00:48:15.360 --> 00:48:17.070
as well in older people

00:48:17.070 --> 00:48:18.930
and we're underplaying the significance

00:48:18.930 --> 00:48:20.820
of older people when they get infections.

00:48:20.820 --> 00:48:25.020
So it was just we've got
the MSF sepsis coming out.

00:48:25.020 --> 00:48:27.930
I think it's actually how
we start to view infections

00:48:27.930 --> 00:48:31.290
as a more important mark

00:48:31.290 --> 00:48:32.730
of how we are doing collectively

00:48:32.730 --> 00:48:33.960
as part of the quality of report,

00:48:33.960 --> 00:48:35.670
and it's putting the
vaccines and prevention bit

00:48:35.670 --> 00:48:37.320
in with the prescribing,

00:48:37.320 --> 00:48:39.060
and we need to think more
about hospital prescribing

00:48:39.060 --> 00:48:40.950
'cause that's now got stuck.

00:48:40.950 --> 00:48:42.060
- Okay, really good point.

00:48:42.060 --> 00:48:43.410
So let's more just log that

00:48:43.410 --> 00:48:45.030
as something that we come back to.

00:48:45.030 --> 00:48:46.680
Okay, Ravi.

00:48:46.680 --> 00:48:48.180
Oh, sorry I jumped over Duncan.

00:48:48.180 --> 00:48:50.190
So I'll go Duncan then Ravi.

00:48:50.190 --> 00:48:51.300
- It leads in quite nicely actually

00:48:51.300 --> 00:48:52.907
'cause I was going to raise
the issue about vaccines

00:48:52.907 --> 00:48:54.480
'cause it's terrible

00:48:54.480 --> 00:48:57.730
that we've lost our
measles status on this.

00:48:57.730 --> 00:48:59.790
And I did want to just
put a plug in for the fact

00:48:59.790 --> 00:49:03.640
that we've now got the
pathfinders of health visiting

00:49:04.530 --> 00:49:05.790
with vaccines going on,

00:49:05.790 --> 00:49:06.660
and perhaps at some point,

00:49:06.660 --> 00:49:08.730
we should bring that back to
the Board for some visibility

00:49:08.730 --> 00:49:11.400
and perhaps about what more
we are doing on vaccines

00:49:11.400 --> 00:49:12.960
because I think it's so critical.

00:49:12.960 --> 00:49:14.670
And then the second point,

00:49:14.670 --> 00:49:16.720
and I guess in the vein of being curious,

00:49:17.610 --> 00:49:20.550
adult inpatients with
a learning disability

00:49:20.550 --> 00:49:21.480
has been quite static,

00:49:21.480 --> 00:49:23.010
and it's starting to come down now.

00:49:23.010 --> 00:49:24.870
What are we doing differently?

00:49:24.870 --> 00:49:28.380
And I guess because this
has been so problematic

00:49:28.380 --> 00:49:30.120
for so long, what else do we need to do

00:49:30.120 --> 00:49:33.248
to really drive this down even further?

00:49:33.248 --> 00:49:36.300
- Okay, great, Ravi and then Amanda.

00:49:36.300 --> 00:49:38.910
- There are some welcome
improvements in elective care.

00:49:38.910 --> 00:49:41.010
I'm just wondering whether it's your view

00:49:41.010 --> 00:49:43.830
that we just have to accept
that it will be incremental,

00:49:43.830 --> 00:49:45.360
and we will get progress,

00:49:45.360 --> 00:49:47.070
or whether there's a line of sight

00:49:47.070 --> 00:49:49.863
to sort of increasing
the rate of improvement.

00:49:51.300 --> 00:49:52.413
- Okay, that's good.

00:49:54.420 --> 00:49:55.470
Amanda.

00:49:55.470 --> 00:49:58.020
- Just to come back on
a couple of the points.

00:49:58.020 --> 00:50:00.243
We have taken action on some of the things

00:50:00.243 --> 00:50:01.680
that are flagging here.

00:50:01.680 --> 00:50:03.720
So particularly children's
vaccination rates

00:50:03.720 --> 00:50:06.600
but also the all eight diabetes processes

00:50:06.600 --> 00:50:10.950
to strengthen the focus on
that within the GP contract.

00:50:10.950 --> 00:50:12.417
I can't talk about detail

00:50:12.417 --> 00:50:13.620
'cause it's not in the public domain

00:50:13.620 --> 00:50:15.540
for another couple of weeks,

00:50:15.540 --> 00:50:17.343
but we've noted it and acted.

00:50:18.360 --> 00:50:20.740
- Okay, did you have a question Andrew?

00:50:20.740 --> 00:50:22.590
- Yeah, just one point just to build

00:50:22.590 --> 00:50:27.480
on Louise's point around life expectancy.

00:50:27.480 --> 00:50:30.810
I mean it's disappointing to
see it reducing the way it has.

00:50:30.810 --> 00:50:34.590
But have we plotted the key
elements of the 10-year plan

00:50:34.590 --> 00:50:38.280
and what that's going to do
in terms of life expectancy,

00:50:38.280 --> 00:50:41.070
because this should hit the bottom

00:50:41.070 --> 00:50:43.320
and then move back up again, I hope,

00:50:43.320 --> 00:50:44.880
into a better space.

00:50:44.880 --> 00:50:47.302
Because we have lost nearly
two years of life expectancy,

00:50:47.302 --> 00:50:51.120
which is a hell of a lot,
a hell of a lot really,

00:50:51.120 --> 00:50:52.918
especially for people getting quite close.

00:50:53.984 --> 00:50:55.990
- You're doing well to still be here.

00:50:57.986 --> 00:50:58.993
- There'll be cohorts that are even worse.

00:50:58.993 --> 00:51:01.560
- There are, and that that's the concern,

00:51:01.560 --> 00:51:03.270
is the inequalities as well.

00:51:03.270 --> 00:51:07.083
Okay, so let me try and summarise.

00:51:09.060 --> 00:51:12.210
So first off, which is you
don't need to respond to,

00:51:12.210 --> 00:51:14.793
but I think we do need
to put on record is,

00:51:15.690 --> 00:51:17.610
when you look through
the breadth of this data,

00:51:17.610 --> 00:51:21.030
the timing of many of the
metrics is very different,

00:51:21.030 --> 00:51:22.830
because things like life expectancy,

00:51:22.830 --> 00:51:24.930
you've got to look at over 10 years,

00:51:24.930 --> 00:51:27.210
other things we're looking
at over the last few weeks

00:51:27.210 --> 00:51:28.380
and last few months.

00:51:28.380 --> 00:51:29.213
And I think we just,

00:51:29.213 --> 00:51:31.140
we perhaps just need to acknowledge that

00:51:31.140 --> 00:51:35.610
a little bit more
upfront, is the first one.

00:51:35.610 --> 00:51:38.700
Secondly is, and we have
all been speaking about this

00:51:38.700 --> 00:51:41.490
for quite a while, we have loads of data,

00:51:41.490 --> 00:51:43.710
which is fantastic,
and we do probably need

00:51:43.710 --> 00:51:45.810
to be using things like FDPA bit more

00:51:45.810 --> 00:51:47.190
to do the summarising up,

00:51:47.190 --> 00:51:49.140
going down into the more detail,

00:51:49.140 --> 00:51:51.270
and just think through
how we bring that back

00:51:51.270 --> 00:51:54.930
to different Board
committees and to this Board.

00:51:54.930 --> 00:51:57.720
And then the third bit is your point Jim,

00:51:57.720 --> 00:51:59.340
that in some areas I think,

00:51:59.340 --> 00:52:02.400
we do have some nervousness
about data quality

00:52:02.400 --> 00:52:04.710
and that needs to be an action really

00:52:04.710 --> 00:52:05.989
to get better and better.

00:52:05.989 --> 00:52:08.100
And I think particularly as
the technology gets better

00:52:08.100 --> 00:52:11.550
than the accuracy of the data,

00:52:11.550 --> 00:52:13.260
should get better as well.

00:52:13.260 --> 00:52:14.943
So those are more observations.

00:52:16.380 --> 00:52:19.500
I think, you know, great
conversation today,

00:52:19.500 --> 00:52:21.360
which is really your system bit

00:52:21.360 --> 00:52:23.700
and the curiosity bit at a system level,

00:52:23.700 --> 00:52:25.620
which is absolutely
what we should be doing,

00:52:25.620 --> 00:52:27.630
and of course ICBs should be doing.

00:52:27.630 --> 00:52:30.210
So we also need to see a
lot of this data flowing

00:52:30.210 --> 00:52:35.070
through the ICBs, is really summarised

00:52:35.070 --> 00:52:36.437
by that discussion about life expectancy.

00:52:36.437 --> 00:52:38.460
'Cause that's ultimately
what we're here for

00:52:38.460 --> 00:52:42.180
is to improve health and
wellbeing and quality of life.

00:52:42.180 --> 00:52:44.070
So we need quality of life measures

00:52:44.070 --> 00:52:45.570
alongside life expectancy measures.

00:52:45.570 --> 00:52:47.970
So that years of life
lived in good health,

00:52:47.970 --> 00:52:49.080
I think is a really good measure,

00:52:49.080 --> 00:52:50.250
which we should also be looking at,

00:52:50.250 --> 00:52:52.140
but would be going down as well,

00:52:52.140 --> 00:52:53.850
but we should be looking at that.

00:52:53.850 --> 00:52:56.160
And then really as you said Andrew,

00:52:56.160 --> 00:52:58.230
I think thinking through
what are the drivers of that,

00:52:58.230 --> 00:53:00.930
so primary prevention, absolutely.

00:53:00.930 --> 00:53:05.010
And while a lot of that is
out with our direct remit,

00:53:05.010 --> 00:53:07.920
we don't have much in here
on obesity rates for example.

00:53:07.920 --> 00:53:09.450
So maybe we should be looking at that,

00:53:09.450 --> 00:53:11.760
even though we recognise we
need broader system partners

00:53:11.760 --> 00:53:12.960
to work on that.

00:53:12.960 --> 00:53:16.110
But absolutely vaccination
and screening we need,

00:53:16.110 --> 00:53:17.520
and people have said we should do

00:53:17.520 --> 00:53:20.280
a bit more focus on that, absolutely.

00:53:20.280 --> 00:53:22.110
I'm with, as others have said,

00:53:22.110 --> 00:53:23.460
the most concerning one for me

00:53:23.460 --> 00:53:25.170
continues to be secondary prevention.

00:53:25.170 --> 00:53:27.000
It's completely within our gift,

00:53:27.000 --> 00:53:29.550
we hold the contracts with the GPs,

00:53:29.550 --> 00:53:32.370
and I don't think that
we can say it's good

00:53:32.370 --> 00:53:36.780
to have less than 40% of
people with type 1 diabetes

00:53:36.780 --> 00:53:37.950
having regular checks.

00:53:37.950 --> 00:53:41.670
I mean that's after we
take out exceptions,

00:53:41.670 --> 00:53:43.440
after we take out the exceptions

00:53:43.440 --> 00:53:45.270
of the hard-to-reach people.

00:53:45.270 --> 00:53:46.470
I mean that really is,

00:53:46.470 --> 00:53:49.890
that's stacking up a future of ill health

00:53:49.890 --> 00:53:54.600
and early mortality, so not good.

00:53:54.600 --> 00:53:57.360
And then also, I still
think we need a bit more

00:53:57.360 --> 00:53:59.520
on effectiveness throughout the system.

00:53:59.520 --> 00:54:00.510
I know we've got some measure,

00:54:00.510 --> 00:54:02.700
but you know in hospitals
we've got nothing in here.

00:54:02.700 --> 00:54:04.800
All the things that were
in the Darzi investigation

00:54:04.800 --> 00:54:07.170
on door to needle times,

00:54:07.170 --> 00:54:08.910
outcomes from stroke care, and so on,

00:54:08.910 --> 00:54:12.030
I think we as a Board need to
be seeing a bit more of that,

00:54:12.030 --> 00:54:13.560
because ultimately those three

00:54:13.560 --> 00:54:15.990
is what will contribute
to life expectancy.

00:54:15.990 --> 00:54:19.350
So we produce Andrew, we
need to be able to see that.

00:54:19.350 --> 00:54:21.180
So I think if you could
talk a bit about that David,

00:54:21.180 --> 00:54:22.620
that would be good.

00:54:22.620 --> 00:54:23.880
If you could talk about the adults

00:54:23.880 --> 00:54:26.280
