00:57:01.050 --> 00:57:04.937
about some of the transformation efforts

00:57:06.150 --> 00:57:07.170
that are going in,

00:57:07.170 --> 00:57:09.510
notably that we'll be starting next year.

00:57:09.510 --> 00:57:12.780
So big thing that we're focused
on is about outpatients.

00:57:12.780 --> 00:57:15.600
We know with, we're already seeing

00:57:15.600 --> 00:57:19.770
the significant impact of
things like advice and guidance

00:57:19.770 --> 00:57:22.050
where we can get patients

00:57:22.050 --> 00:57:25.410
having a diagnosis much more quickly

00:57:25.410 --> 00:57:26.427
than being added to the waiting list

00:57:26.427 --> 00:57:28.530
and waiting for a period
of time at the moment,

00:57:28.530 --> 00:57:30.930
a diagnosis and having
their treatment started,

00:57:30.930 --> 00:57:33.360
and having clinicians in primary care,

00:57:33.360 --> 00:57:35.250
clinicians in hospitals,

00:57:35.250 --> 00:57:37.350
and sometimes involving the patients,

00:57:37.350 --> 00:57:40.140
enabling a much quicker consultation,

00:57:40.140 --> 00:57:43.110
much quicker diagnosis to
be achieved for patients.

00:57:43.110 --> 00:57:44.610
So we know that's one key element.

00:57:44.610 --> 00:57:45.780
The second is about some

00:57:45.780 --> 00:57:47.370
of the digital transformation
that we're going to see.

00:57:47.370 --> 00:57:51.330
We had a conversation before about how AVT

00:57:51.330 --> 00:57:53.250
can be used in different settings.

00:57:53.250 --> 00:57:54.960
We talk a lot about it in outpatients,

00:57:54.960 --> 00:57:58.350
we think it's probably about
10% productivity opportunity

00:57:58.350 --> 00:57:59.183
in outpatient.

00:57:59.183 --> 00:58:01.080
If you think the breadth of
outpatient activity we do,

00:58:01.080 --> 00:58:03.240
the number of clinics we run all the time,

00:58:03.240 --> 00:58:05.703
it's a significant benefit
that we could realise.

00:58:06.870 --> 00:58:09.420
That's just one element of the
transformation with digital.

00:58:09.420 --> 00:58:11.280
And then the massive
shift with neighbourhoods,

00:58:11.280 --> 00:58:13.170
I think, gives us an opportunity

00:58:13.170 --> 00:58:16.860
both to avoid patients
being referred into hospital

00:58:16.860 --> 00:58:19.920
in the first instance for outpatients,

00:58:19.920 --> 00:58:21.270
still getting the treatment they need,

00:58:21.270 --> 00:58:23.190
the diagnosis that's really pivotal

00:58:23.190 --> 00:58:24.960
to their course treatment.

00:58:24.960 --> 00:58:27.600
But doing that in a setting
that's close to the home.

00:58:27.600 --> 00:58:29.763
And we also, final, final bit,

00:58:30.750 --> 00:58:33.240
we talk a lot about how we're going to,

00:58:33.240 --> 00:58:34.350
we've done for a number of years,

00:58:34.350 --> 00:58:35.550
how we're going to reduce

00:58:35.550 --> 00:58:39.900
the number of patients that
we have coming to hospitals

00:58:39.900 --> 00:58:41.310
and follow-up appointments.

00:58:41.310 --> 00:58:42.960
It doesn't mean that
we don't have oversight

00:58:42.960 --> 00:58:45.540
and visibility of patient's condition,

00:58:45.540 --> 00:58:48.990
but we want to involve patients
more closely in the care,

00:58:48.990 --> 00:58:51.900
and have a more neighborhood-led model

00:58:51.900 --> 00:58:53.640
to continue the follow up care

00:58:53.640 --> 00:58:55.380
for patients when it's warranted.

00:58:55.380 --> 00:58:56.580
- I just add to that as well.

00:58:56.580 --> 00:58:58.380
So Mark's team, Ravi are doing

00:58:58.380 --> 00:59:01.260
a bit of analysis this
week on the organisations

00:59:01.260 --> 00:59:04.680
that have made big, like
double-digit percentage movements

00:59:04.680 --> 00:59:07.020
on RTT all this size.

00:59:07.020 --> 00:59:08.820
And we think we understand broadly

00:59:08.820 --> 00:59:10.950
what's going on but don't
fully understand them.

00:59:10.950 --> 00:59:13.530
As soon as we can understand that better,

00:59:13.530 --> 00:59:15.870
we'll get a better chance
of industrialising it.

00:59:15.870 --> 00:59:17.910
'Cause it is there's
a good bunch isn't it?

00:59:17.910 --> 00:59:19.530
We shared them with chief execs last week,

00:59:19.530 --> 00:59:23.010
20 odd organisations that
have really broken the whole,

00:59:23.010 --> 00:59:27.930
this is 1%, 2%, 20, 30%
movements on some of the metrics,

00:59:27.930 --> 00:59:29.793
which I think is where the game is.

00:59:31.050 --> 00:59:34.350
- So I think we'll come back
to that key point, okay?

00:59:34.350 --> 00:59:36.780
- I think there's just
two final areas, Penny.

00:59:36.780 --> 00:59:39.930
First is Andrew's comment
around life expectancy

00:59:39.930 --> 00:59:41.220
matched against the 10-year plan,

00:59:41.220 --> 00:59:43.680
I think is a really important one.

00:59:43.680 --> 00:59:46.440
I don't believe that we
have that information,

00:59:46.440 --> 00:59:47.670
but I'm looking slightly at Paul,

00:59:47.670 --> 00:59:49.770
sorry, from a strategic perspective

00:59:49.770 --> 00:59:51.570
whether that's something we would want to,

00:59:51.570 --> 00:59:52.947
I mean, with public health colleagues

00:59:52.947 --> 00:59:53.780
and with broader colleagues,

00:59:53.780 --> 00:59:56.130
it would be an important
exercise, I think,

00:59:56.130 --> 00:59:58.137
and something that will
bind to motivate people

00:59:58.137 --> 00:59:59.760
and the power of the plan.

00:59:59.760 --> 01:00:01.810
And then finally just on community waits.

01:00:02.976 --> 01:00:05.520
And this is an area we
focus on increasingly

01:00:05.520 --> 01:00:07.950
at the performance
reviews with regions now.

01:00:07.950 --> 01:00:09.420
So it isn't something that's hidden,

01:00:09.420 --> 01:00:11.517
it's something that we
see and we challenge on

01:00:11.517 --> 01:00:12.960
and there has been some good progress.

01:00:12.960 --> 01:00:15.510
But Amanda, I dunno if
you wanted maybe summarise

01:00:15.510 --> 01:00:18.060
a bit some of the broader
progress that you're seeing.

01:00:18.060 --> 01:00:19.237
- In GP waits.
- Community waits.

01:00:19.237 --> 01:00:20.550
- Community waits.
- Yeah.

01:00:20.550 --> 01:00:22.350
- So the issue with community waits,

01:00:22.350 --> 01:00:25.230
it's never before been a target.

01:00:25.230 --> 01:00:27.600
And so it hasn't had the focus

01:00:27.600 --> 01:00:29.550
that some of the longer waits have had.

01:00:30.507 --> 01:00:33.630
It has continued to go up
when you break it down.

01:00:33.630 --> 01:00:38.070
There's an issue that systems
have the capacity to tackle

01:00:38.070 --> 01:00:40.560
and has been put into the
medium term planning guidance

01:00:40.560 --> 01:00:42.660
this year around tackling

01:00:42.660 --> 01:00:45.600
musculoskeletal service community waits.

01:00:45.600 --> 01:00:47.970
But there's a much, much
more challenging issue

01:00:47.970 --> 01:00:50.220
around community paediatrics

01:00:50.220 --> 01:00:52.380
and neurodiversity
assessments for children.

01:00:52.380 --> 01:00:55.320
And that's where the bulk of
the extremely long waiters

01:00:55.320 --> 01:00:57.180
but also the rise.

01:00:57.180 --> 01:00:58.890
We did ask systems last year

01:00:58.890 --> 01:01:02.230
for plans to take the longest waiters out

01:01:03.420 --> 01:01:05.040
with the odd exception people

01:01:05.040 --> 01:01:06.780
were universally unsuccessful.

01:01:06.780 --> 01:01:08.760
In that we've put a tighter ask

01:01:08.760 --> 01:01:11.130
in the planning guidance this year.

01:01:11.130 --> 01:01:13.710
But it is a challenge
that stretches across,

01:01:13.710 --> 01:01:15.630
it's not just a money challenge,

01:01:15.630 --> 01:01:18.480
it's a workforce and a
pathway redesign challenge.

01:01:18.480 --> 01:01:21.480
- Okay, so we've got work underway

01:01:21.480 --> 01:01:26.453
on neurodiversity autism, ADHD,

01:01:28.320 --> 01:01:29.520
but it would be good to see,

01:01:29.520 --> 01:01:33.090
we haven't had a discussion
at Board level on the data.

01:01:33.090 --> 01:01:34.773
So how many people are waiting,

01:01:35.670 --> 01:01:37.680
what are the conditions
they're waiting for,

01:01:37.680 --> 01:01:39.180
how many staff are there?

01:01:39.180 --> 01:01:41.018
What's the productivity

01:01:41.018 --> 01:01:42.990
and all of that within community?

01:01:42.990 --> 01:01:45.870
I think we need to get
into that and look at that

01:01:45.870 --> 01:01:48.120
so we can bring that
back to a future meeting.

01:01:48.120 --> 01:01:51.244
And then sickness absence
was the other one.

01:01:51.244 --> 01:01:52.786
- Yes.
- Can I just...

01:01:52.786 --> 01:01:55.140
Jo, I dunno whether you'd like to give

01:01:55.140 --> 01:01:56.850
any thoughts on sickness absence.

01:01:56.850 --> 01:01:57.933
- Yes, the data has slightly risen.

01:01:57.933 --> 01:02:00.180
I mean in the 10-year workforce plan,

01:02:00.180 --> 01:02:03.840
we said we'd reduce it over
the next 10 years down to 4.1%.

01:02:03.840 --> 01:02:05.280
So we're looking for 1% reduction.

01:02:05.280 --> 01:02:07.560
So think your point about
tracking it against,

01:02:07.560 --> 01:02:10.650
so on trend, it is not that big a change,

01:02:10.650 --> 01:02:12.476
but actually we do need
a directional change.

01:02:12.476 --> 01:02:14.580
There's a lot of effort
we're putting into that.

01:02:14.580 --> 01:02:17.370
It was a key target in
the planning guidance

01:02:17.370 --> 01:02:19.590
to sort of make sure that people
were given that attention.

01:02:19.590 --> 01:02:20.850
'cause poor sickness absence

01:02:20.850 --> 01:02:22.590
as well has been bad for staff

01:02:22.590 --> 01:02:24.150
that drives bank and agency,

01:02:24.150 --> 01:02:25.680
which you're also trying to reduce.

01:02:25.680 --> 01:02:27.870
So it's connected as Andrew
knows, lots of other things.

01:02:27.870 --> 01:02:29.700
So I think we just need to look

01:02:29.700 --> 01:02:31.770
at whether that is
actually a seasonal blip.

01:02:31.770 --> 01:02:33.239
'Cause it comes down,

01:02:33.239 --> 01:02:36.600
it's not that helps a
month by month basis,

01:02:36.600 --> 01:02:38.480
but if that continues we need to-

01:02:38.480 --> 01:02:40.410
- It was September '25

01:02:40.410 --> 01:02:42.180
was higher than August '24.

01:02:42.180 --> 01:02:44.404
- Yeah, I know, so I was trying, but,

01:02:44.404 --> 01:02:46.020
and there might be variations as well,

01:02:46.020 --> 01:02:47.520
I need to see what's beneath that

01:02:47.520 --> 01:02:49.793
as geographically as well.
- Okay.

01:02:51.183 --> 01:02:53.070
- Through our EPEG,

01:02:53.070 --> 01:02:55.200
which is a efficiency
and productivity group,

01:02:55.200 --> 01:02:56.580
we are doing with Jo's team,

01:02:56.580 --> 01:02:59.400
a deep dive into staffing to be honest.

01:02:59.400 --> 01:03:01.500
So that it is temporary staffing,

01:03:01.500 --> 01:03:03.840
substantive staffing sickness.

01:03:03.840 --> 01:03:05.520
We're also looking at accrued annual leave

01:03:05.520 --> 01:03:07.140
that we can see that's
been going up as well,

01:03:07.140 --> 01:03:09.153
probably related to industrial action.

01:03:10.050 --> 01:03:12.900
So just looking at the kind
of those underlying factors

01:03:12.900 --> 01:03:15.450
that might have impact on
this trend of sickness.

01:03:15.450 --> 01:03:17.040
- Okay, and now we're
going to come back to that

01:03:17.040 --> 01:03:18.990
in finance and productivity in a minute.

01:03:18.990 --> 01:03:19.952
Meghana.
- Thank you.

01:03:19.952 --> 01:03:22.130
I think it would be great to see it split

01:03:22.130 --> 01:03:24.270
by short-term sickness
and long-term sickness

01:03:24.270 --> 01:03:27.450
because you know, the conditions
causing those are different

01:03:27.450 --> 01:03:29.040
and therefore the management strategies

01:03:29.040 --> 01:03:30.900
are different for
different workforce groups

01:03:30.900 --> 01:03:32.040
in the workforce.

01:03:32.040 --> 01:03:34.200
I think we'd be able to tackle
it better if we did that.

01:03:34.200 --> 01:03:35.790
- Yeah, and we could look at pressures,

01:03:35.790 --> 01:03:36.777
should we bring a deeper dive on this?

01:03:36.777 --> 01:03:40.170
I think it's so crucial
to, you know, from...

01:03:40.170 --> 01:03:42.300
So for nursing, even though
if we had a 1% reduction

01:03:42.300 --> 01:03:43.133
in sickness absence,

01:03:43.133 --> 01:03:45.270
we'd get three and a half
thousand extra nurses a year,

01:03:45.270 --> 01:03:46.550
says it's connected to
so much of our work,

01:03:46.550 --> 01:03:48.900
it's one of those key indicators.

01:03:48.900 --> 01:03:50.340
- Yeah.
- And it would look against

01:03:50.340 --> 01:03:51.506
the 10-year workforce plan

01:03:51.506 --> 01:03:54.000
as how we're doing go against trend,

01:03:54.000 --> 01:03:55.272
that'd be really good.

01:03:55.272 --> 01:03:56.790
- Okay, good.

01:03:56.790 --> 01:03:58.440
So thank you David, and
I think that gives us

01:03:58.440 --> 01:04:00.000
some key areas for focus

01:04:00.000 --> 01:04:01.890
over the next couple of months.

01:04:01.890 --> 01:04:02.970
Elizabeth. Finance.

01:04:02.970 --> 01:04:03.840
- Yeah, thank you.

01:04:03.840 --> 01:04:06.360
So at month nine it's a short paper.

01:04:06.360 --> 01:04:08.190
It's a bit of a snapshot to be honest

01:04:08.190 --> 01:04:10.560
because the month nine work continues,

01:04:10.560 --> 01:04:12.783
but we wanted to bring
it to you real time.

01:04:13.680 --> 01:04:16.080
We, as an NHS, you'll see in the paper,

01:04:16.080 --> 01:04:18.690
we financially, we broadly balanced

01:04:18.690 --> 01:04:21.600
both year to date and forecast outturn.

01:04:21.600 --> 01:04:26.160
So a small overspend year
to date of 14 million,

01:04:26.160 --> 01:04:30.960
it's about 0.03% actually of
our year to date allocation

01:04:30.960 --> 01:04:35.190
and then a small underspend
actually on a forecast basis.

01:04:35.190 --> 01:04:37.140
So a very strong position

01:04:37.140 --> 01:04:38.890
to be in at this stage of the year.

01:04:40.050 --> 01:04:42.570
I have to be honest, it's
been a bumpy month nine.

01:04:42.570 --> 01:04:43.770
We always said at month nine,

01:04:43.770 --> 01:04:45.120
we'd have some bumps in the road,

01:04:45.120 --> 01:04:47.430
and we've definitely had those.

01:04:47.430 --> 01:04:52.430
But today, we have 17
systems and 61% of providers

01:04:52.770 --> 01:04:55.323
that are delivering in
line with their plans.

01:04:56.910 --> 01:04:58.710
If you compare that to
this time last year,

01:04:58.710 --> 01:05:00.450
that would've only been four systems.

01:05:00.450 --> 01:05:04.050
So feels like we've still got a grip.

01:05:04.050 --> 01:05:05.250
System level overspends,

01:05:05.250 --> 01:05:06.150
you'll see in the paper,

01:05:06.150 --> 01:05:10.260
account for about 445 million.

01:05:10.260 --> 01:05:13.140
And mainly this is reflective of slippage

01:05:13.140 --> 01:05:15.240
against efficiency plans

01:05:15.240 --> 01:05:18.330
and increased workforce costs
in a very unplanned way,

01:05:18.330 --> 01:05:20.280
I'll come back to that.

01:05:20.280 --> 01:05:24.330
At month nine, we did signal to the sector

01:05:24.330 --> 01:05:27.360
that we were distributing

01:05:27.360 --> 01:05:30.450
some industrial action funding for both...

01:05:30.450 --> 01:05:33.750
This is a contribution towards
the cost of industrial action

01:05:33.750 --> 01:05:36.000
for November and December strikes,

01:05:36.000 --> 01:05:38.640
so we wouldn't cover it in full.

01:05:38.640 --> 01:05:41.790
We used the SitRep data
that was provided to us

01:05:41.790 --> 01:05:44.160
as a basis of distribution,

01:05:44.160 --> 01:05:46.830
and effectively that funding was generated

01:05:46.830 --> 01:05:49.380
through a moratorium of spend within

01:05:49.380 --> 01:05:51.630
both NHS England and the
Department of Health.

01:05:51.630 --> 01:05:53.850
So it wasn't a reserve or anything,

01:05:53.850 --> 01:05:55.290
it was funding that we generated

01:05:55.290 --> 01:05:57.210
by stopping some things

01:05:57.210 --> 01:06:00.273
to ensure that we could support
the service more broadly.

01:06:01.470 --> 01:06:02.790
And then at month nine,

01:06:02.790 --> 01:06:04.710
in terms of the forecast position,

01:06:04.710 --> 01:06:06.120
which is the topic

01:06:06.120 --> 01:06:09.180
that we are particularly
looking at the moment.

01:06:09.180 --> 01:06:12.960
You'll see that 13 systems
are currently not on track

01:06:12.960 --> 01:06:17.130
to receive DSF in line with
the final quarter of the year.

01:06:17.130 --> 01:06:18.690
The way that the DSF actually works

01:06:18.690 --> 01:06:22.800
is we have our reported
numbers come through to us

01:06:22.800 --> 01:06:24.810
and then working very closely
with the region and teams,

01:06:24.810 --> 01:06:26.250
we look at the confidence

01:06:26.250 --> 01:06:28.800
as to whether or not those
systems would recover

01:06:28.800 --> 01:06:30.000
between now and the end of the year.

01:06:30.000 --> 01:06:31.320
And obviously with one quarter left,

01:06:31.320 --> 01:06:33.000
there's less time to recover.

01:06:33.000 --> 01:06:36.252
And what I can say is we've had two ICBs

01:06:36.252 --> 01:06:39.240
and 21 trusts at month nine
that have actually signalled

01:06:39.240 --> 01:06:42.540
that they now formally
through Board-reported process

01:06:42.540 --> 01:06:45.213
want to change their
forecasts to a deficit.

01:06:46.200 --> 01:06:49.590
Those revised deficits,
when they've come in,

01:06:49.590 --> 01:06:52.410
we're working very closely
with our regional teams

01:06:52.410 --> 01:06:54.480
to identify further actions

01:06:54.480 --> 01:06:55.770
between now and the end of the year

01:06:55.770 --> 01:06:57.660
to see if we can reduce that run rate,

01:06:57.660 --> 01:07:00.810
really important to manage
the run rate into next year,

01:07:00.810 --> 01:07:03.753
even if we can't recover
the full costs collectively.

01:07:05.010 --> 01:07:07.710
I've engaged with all of the Boards,

01:07:07.710 --> 01:07:10.230
and I've used our productivity packs

01:07:10.230 --> 01:07:11.850
and our improvement packs

01:07:11.850 --> 01:07:15.180
to signal where I think
there could be areas

01:07:15.180 --> 01:07:17.040
of opportunity in the short term,

01:07:17.040 --> 01:07:19.680
and just reemphasizing,

01:07:19.680 --> 01:07:22.290
there's quite still quite a lot to go at.

01:07:22.290 --> 01:07:25.020
What we are not going to
do, because it's February,

01:07:25.020 --> 01:07:26.607
is we are not going to sit back

01:07:26.607 --> 01:07:28.200
and just wait for this to happen.

01:07:28.200 --> 01:07:30.360
This is all about now
making sure we get a grip

01:07:30.360 --> 01:07:33.060
of the run rate in those organisations.

01:07:33.060 --> 01:07:35.970
So we'll continue to work
closely with those systems.

01:07:35.970 --> 01:07:39.570
But needless to say,
other than one system,

01:07:39.570 --> 01:07:42.420
the forecasts that came in,

01:07:42.420 --> 01:07:45.330
they were something that we
were working very closely

01:07:45.330 --> 01:07:48.750
with system partners and trusts on.

01:07:48.750 --> 01:07:51.480
So they weren't a surprise to us as such.

01:07:51.480 --> 01:07:54.270
One systems forecast has
come in at a higher level

01:07:54.270 --> 01:07:56.550
than we anticipated, and
the team are in there today,

01:07:56.550 --> 01:07:59.010
actually I'm getting a regular update.

01:07:59.010 --> 01:08:02.430
That particular system
has had a 3,000-person

01:08:02.430 --> 01:08:05.340
headcount increase in the last three years

01:08:05.340 --> 01:08:07.020
in the acute sector.

01:08:07.020 --> 01:08:08.070
And needless to say,

01:08:08.070 --> 01:08:09.596
the letters that they wrote to me

01:08:09.596 --> 01:08:11.220
- [Penny] And with activity?

01:08:11.220 --> 01:08:12.420
- And the activity is...

01:08:12.420 --> 01:08:13.650
The cost weighted activity,

01:08:13.650 --> 01:08:15.930
I'm trying to work it out as
we sit at the moment, Penny,

01:08:15.930 --> 01:08:18.000
but it's nothing like that.

01:08:18.000 --> 01:08:19.920
So there's quite a lot to go at.

01:08:19.920 --> 01:08:21.540
Our benchmarking pack suggests

01:08:21.540 --> 01:08:24.240
they've got 380 million
pounds worth of opportunities,

01:08:24.240 --> 01:08:25.650
just the things that we are looking at

01:08:25.650 --> 01:08:27.120
in that single system.

01:08:27.120 --> 01:08:30.690
So we are looking forward
to working with them closely

01:08:30.690 --> 01:08:35.640
and helping them with a smile of course.

01:08:35.640 --> 01:08:38.400
But you know, no Board, no system

01:08:38.400 --> 01:08:40.140
wants to find themselves in this place

01:08:40.140 --> 01:08:41.070
at this stage of the year.

01:08:41.070 --> 01:08:43.713
So we've got to respect that as well.

01:08:45.270 --> 01:08:47.910
But what I can say is,
because of the work we've done

01:08:47.910 --> 01:08:49.950
around tighter financial controls

01:08:49.950 --> 01:08:52.230
within NHS England and
the Department of Health

01:08:52.230 --> 01:08:54.120
and the moratorium of spend,

01:08:54.120 --> 01:08:55.650
we've got some underspends

01:08:55.650 --> 01:08:59.430
in non-delegated specialist commissioning.

01:08:59.430 --> 01:09:02.190
So all of these things are not budgets

01:09:02.190 --> 01:09:04.980
that would've impacted on
the service in aggregate.

01:09:04.980 --> 01:09:06.600
We are still on track to deliver

01:09:06.600 --> 01:09:07.920
a balance plan for this year.

01:09:07.920 --> 01:09:11.070
So we feel reasonably confident,

01:09:11.070 --> 01:09:13.110
and as I say, even those systems

01:09:13.110 --> 01:09:14.610
that have gone off plan month nine,

01:09:14.610 --> 01:09:16.440
they're engaging really proactively.

01:09:16.440 --> 01:09:17.490
So I'm pleased to see that.

01:09:17.490 --> 01:09:19.707
- And the only bit there Elizabeth

01:09:19.707 --> 01:09:21.030
and what you say is, of course,

01:09:21.030 --> 01:09:22.980
that's after deficit support funding.

01:09:22.980 --> 01:09:24.450
- Yeah
- So we are propping up

01:09:24.450 --> 01:09:27.000
a number of systems which Jim says

01:09:27.000 --> 01:09:29.340
they are spending other people's money

01:09:29.340 --> 01:09:32.340
to some quite high degree in some areas.

01:09:32.340 --> 01:09:34.110
- Yeah,
- I'm sure Rob is delighted

01:09:34.110 --> 01:09:36.000
that Dorset is at the
bottom of this table.

01:09:36.000 --> 01:09:37.350
So well done, Dorset.

01:09:37.350 --> 01:09:39.330
- Yeah, Dorset have
done exceptionally well,

01:09:39.330 --> 01:09:41.973
actually, really, really well.

01:09:42.982 --> 01:09:45.690
- Thank you.
- Okay, so I've got Andrew,

01:09:45.690 --> 01:09:47.910
Jeremy, and Louise to come in.

01:09:47.910 --> 01:09:50.130
- So Elizabeth, what happens if this year

01:09:50.130 --> 01:09:52.050
you withdraw deficit support?

01:09:52.050 --> 01:09:54.780
You mentioned there were
13 systems affected,

01:09:54.780 --> 01:09:59.070
that makes next year's kind
of starting position worse.

01:09:59.070 --> 01:10:03.933
So are you expecting to see
a full recovery next year

01:10:05.070 --> 01:10:08.010
where you've got systems
in significant deficit

01:10:08.010 --> 01:10:10.441
as a result of not hitting their target?

01:10:10.441 --> 01:10:11.274
- Can I build on that?

01:10:11.274 --> 01:10:12.107
I've got similar question

01:10:12.107 --> 01:10:13.440
which is given the way you're managing it,

01:10:13.440 --> 01:10:15.510
and well done for managing it so tightly,

01:10:15.510 --> 01:10:16.740
are any things you're doing yet

01:10:16.740 --> 01:10:19.020
for managing this year
mortgaging us into next year

01:10:19.020 --> 01:10:19.853
in terms of risk?

01:10:19.853 --> 01:10:20.820
So it's similar question.

01:10:20.820 --> 01:10:23.850
- Yeah, yeah, so-
- Well, let's take,

01:10:23.850 --> 01:10:25.830
'cause I've got one question

01:10:25.830 --> 01:10:26.820
that builds on that as well.

01:10:26.820 --> 01:10:29.970
So I've heard a bit of
a rumour from the system

01:10:29.970 --> 01:10:34.970
that a lot of the savings to
date have been non-recurrent.

01:10:34.980 --> 01:10:37.140
So they're not really
productivity improvements,

01:10:37.140 --> 01:10:39.360
they're more cost cuts.

01:10:39.360 --> 01:10:41.610
So what's your sense of the balance

01:10:41.610 --> 01:10:44.220
between non recurrent
and recurrent savings?

01:10:44.220 --> 01:10:45.330
Louise.

01:10:45.330 --> 01:10:48.030
- So I think you're doing
a fantastic job, Elizabeth,

01:10:48.030 --> 01:10:49.320
in supporting the systems.

01:10:49.320 --> 01:10:52.620
But I'm also a bit worried
about some of the choices

01:10:52.620 --> 01:10:57.620
that they're making in
terms of patient care.

01:10:58.110 --> 01:10:58.943
So it'd be good,

01:10:58.943 --> 01:11:01.530
if there is some kind of
transparency to be given

01:11:01.530 --> 01:11:05.520
on any choices that are being
made across all systems,

01:11:05.520 --> 01:11:09.180
on changes in services
that are being offered

01:11:09.180 --> 01:11:11.520
rather than the kind of
longer term productivity

01:11:11.520 --> 01:11:13.230
and efficiency gains, that would be good.

01:11:13.230 --> 01:11:15.480
And then on the point in paragraph seven

01:11:15.480 --> 01:11:20.130
about non-delegated specialised
commissioning NHS England,

01:11:20.130 --> 01:11:23.181
what is that, and is that having
any impact on patient care?

01:11:23.181 --> 01:11:24.563
- Okay, okay.

01:11:26.640 --> 01:11:31.000
So coming to F&amp;P committee

01:11:32.640 --> 01:11:37.640
pretty soon, will be a conversation
about the unearned DSF.

01:11:37.680 --> 01:11:39.630
Clearly there's some choices to make.

01:11:39.630 --> 01:11:41.070
In one of the organisations

01:11:41.070 --> 01:11:45.630
that actually has gone off
forecast at the year end

01:11:45.630 --> 01:11:47.670
actually wrote to me and said the reason

01:11:47.670 --> 01:11:51.600
that they'd gone off was because of DSF.

01:11:51.600 --> 01:11:53.700
And I said we've always
said it was conditional on

01:11:53.700 --> 01:11:56.280
you deliver your plan and you earn it.

01:11:56.280 --> 01:11:57.690
There's quite a number of systems

01:11:57.690 --> 01:12:01.710
that are delivering this
year without any DSF.

01:12:01.710 --> 01:12:03.330
So we are trying to make sure

01:12:03.330 --> 01:12:08.130
that whatever our next step
is feels fair and balanced.

01:12:08.130 --> 01:12:10.410
And one of the conversations
that we've been having

01:12:10.410 --> 01:12:14.100
with our CFO advisory group
is would we redistribute

01:12:14.100 --> 01:12:18.360
some of the DSF to those
systems that are delivering,

01:12:18.360 --> 01:12:20.370
recognising they'd only
get a cash benefit,

01:12:20.370 --> 01:12:22.350
'cause they'd have to put
it onto their bottom lines,

01:12:22.350 --> 01:12:24.000
that's one of the things.

01:12:24.000 --> 01:12:25.290
The other thing that we've engaged

01:12:25.290 --> 01:12:30.090
with the systems on that have
gone off at month nine is,

01:12:30.090 --> 01:12:32.820
going back to Boards to say in the event

01:12:32.820 --> 01:12:35.910
that you can show a step change

01:12:35.910 --> 01:12:38.520
in this last quarter, you
could earn a proportion

01:12:38.520 --> 01:12:39.753
of your DSF back.

01:12:40.710 --> 01:12:42.210
But ultimately, what we don't want

01:12:42.210 --> 01:12:45.060
is to be sitting with an
underspend in NHS England,

01:12:45.060 --> 01:12:48.390
and then the bit, Penny,
that you made point about,

01:12:48.390 --> 01:12:50.880
you know, there's support
going into systems,

01:12:50.880 --> 01:12:52.050
it's quite complicated,

01:12:52.050 --> 01:12:54.690
and we can see it in allocations.

01:12:54.690 --> 01:12:58.530
So some of these systems
in effect are below target,

01:12:58.530 --> 01:13:00.870
so they would've got some of this money.

01:13:00.870 --> 01:13:03.000
So what we are doing
over the next three years

01:13:03.000 --> 01:13:04.800
is effectively unwinding the DSF

01:13:04.800 --> 01:13:06.360
and putting it into the allocations,

01:13:06.360 --> 01:13:08.433
so moving everybody closer to target.

01:13:09.390 --> 01:13:10.830
And I described, I think,

01:13:10.830 --> 01:13:15.060
in CFOs and chief execs last week,

01:13:15.060 --> 01:13:17.400
we agreed the principle
of the pace of change

01:13:17.400 --> 01:13:19.590
with the CFO community in the summer

01:13:19.590 --> 01:13:21.540
when it was a number free zone.

01:13:21.540 --> 01:13:22.830
I think that was really important,

01:13:22.830 --> 01:13:24.660
'cause as soon as
everybody's seen the numbers,

01:13:24.660 --> 01:13:25.740
they've all got a different opinion

01:13:25.740 --> 01:13:27.840
about how fast we should unwind it.

01:13:27.840 --> 01:13:29.520
So we've taken that approach,

01:13:29.520 --> 01:13:32.227
but there is quite a lot
of noise of people saying,

01:13:32.227 --> 01:13:35.700
"Well, now that it's so transparent,

01:13:35.700 --> 01:13:38.850
if I had my full allocation and I moved,"

01:13:38.850 --> 01:13:40.080
which we know never happens

01:13:40.080 --> 01:13:41.257
and it's never happened in history,

01:13:41.257 --> 01:13:44.250
"then I wouldn't have to
do this piece of recovery."

01:13:44.250 --> 01:13:47.310
So that's a tension.

01:13:47.310 --> 01:13:49.500
On Louise's point about choices.

01:13:49.500 --> 01:13:53.850
So all of the choices

01:13:53.850 --> 01:13:56.313
and the SIP Plans that come in,

01:13:57.270 --> 01:14:00.150
both at plan stage and
then during the year,

01:14:00.150 --> 01:14:02.670
working really closely
through regional teams,

01:14:02.670 --> 01:14:03.900
their quality impact assess,

01:14:03.900 --> 01:14:06.540
so we are constant
constantly looking at that.

01:14:06.540 --> 01:14:09.270
For example, I won't name the system

01:14:09.270 --> 01:14:11.370
but quite recently,

01:14:11.370 --> 01:14:14.970
we knew and it was
alerted by Amanda Doyle,

01:14:14.970 --> 01:14:17.250
and she doesn't know I've acted on it,

01:14:17.250 --> 01:14:21.570
that a system was
withdrawing hospice funding,

01:14:21.570 --> 01:14:24.120
which didn't feel like
the right thing to do,

01:14:24.120 --> 01:14:26.130
and felt like a very knee-jerk reaction.

01:14:26.130 --> 01:14:28.380
So we've gone back to help our system

01:14:28.380 --> 01:14:30.060
find an alternative solution

01:14:30.060 --> 01:14:32.670
to withdraw in the
hospice funding in year,

01:14:32.670 --> 01:14:34.650
recognising, you know, there needs

01:14:34.650 --> 01:14:36.630
to be local commissioning
for all of these services.

01:14:36.630 --> 01:14:39.450
If we see something that
we know is inappropriate,

01:14:39.450 --> 01:14:44.140
we will step in and help
people find alternatives.

01:14:44.140 --> 01:14:45.206
- On SpeCom.

01:14:45.206 --> 01:14:47.820
- Oh, in SpeCom, on SpeCom.

01:14:47.820 --> 01:14:52.380
The way that specialist
drugs and devices work

01:14:52.380 --> 01:14:56.010
is as the costs are
incurred in the provider,

01:14:56.010 --> 01:15:00.210
we pay directly for those
costs and we currently,

01:15:00.210 --> 01:15:02.580
the current costs of
specialist drugs and devices

01:15:02.580 --> 01:15:04.290
is actually lower than we forecast

01:15:04.290 --> 01:15:05.370
at the beginning of the year,

01:15:05.370 --> 01:15:06.393
so it's come down.

01:15:08.490 --> 01:15:10.950
- So Paul wants to come in just for that.

01:15:10.950 --> 01:15:15.950
I think, and you know this
would be a big exercise to do,

01:15:16.980 --> 01:15:19.080
but it feels like if we
link these conversations

01:15:19.080 --> 01:15:21.030
so back to where we
were on life expectancy

01:15:21.030 --> 01:15:22.149
and quality of care and so on.

01:15:22.149 --> 01:15:26.220
It would be nice to see, at some stage,

01:15:26.220 --> 01:15:28.710
what money do we think
we should be spending,

01:15:28.710 --> 01:15:30.090
and on what services

01:15:30.090 --> 01:15:32.820
if we were to maximise life expectancy

01:15:32.820 --> 01:15:33.963
and quality of life.

01:15:34.800 --> 01:15:37.290
And that would take into account

01:15:37.290 --> 01:15:38.730
the impact of those services

01:15:38.730 --> 01:15:41.190
on life expectancy and quality of life.

01:15:41.190 --> 01:15:42.720
But it would also take into account

01:15:42.720 --> 01:15:45.120
what we think the should costs should be

01:15:45.120 --> 01:15:47.580
if people are working
efficiently and effectively.

01:15:47.580 --> 01:15:49.140
And that would then give us something

01:15:49.140 --> 01:15:51.600
that we could actually be monitoring

01:15:51.600 --> 01:15:53.250
all of our finances against,

01:15:53.250 --> 01:15:54.660
you know, how close are people

01:15:54.660 --> 01:15:58.140
to actually delivering a high quality

01:15:58.140 --> 01:15:59.850
and efficient service.

01:15:59.850 --> 01:16:01.920
And I think at the moment
we don't quite have that,

01:16:01.920 --> 01:16:03.240
so it's quite hard-

01:16:03.240 --> 01:16:05.370
- I think, you know-
- So things bubble up, like.

01:16:05.370 --> 01:16:07.135
- It also helps you narrative, I think,

01:16:07.135 --> 01:16:08.603
business case in the country

01:16:08.603 --> 01:16:10.410
for spending on the economy, et cetera.

01:16:10.410 --> 01:16:11.243
- So yeah.

01:16:11.243 --> 01:16:13.020
- Well yes, that's the other
bit is where we investing.

01:16:13.020 --> 01:16:15.390
- I think we've got pieces of
the jigsaw coming together.

01:16:15.390 --> 01:16:16.784
- Yes.
- And Paul and I

01:16:16.784 --> 01:16:18.387
have been talking about it.
- Yeah, I was about to say,

01:16:18.387 --> 01:16:19.600
I think over the next month,

01:16:19.600 --> 01:16:21.630
we're going to be putting
exactly that together.

01:16:21.630 --> 01:16:22.710
- Okay, great.
- I think we will need,

01:16:22.710 --> 01:16:25.110
this goes back to not just year on year,

01:16:25.110 --> 01:16:26.820
but 10-year plan delivery,

01:16:26.820 --> 01:16:29.160
a view on actually how
do we expect a pattern

01:16:29.160 --> 01:16:30.510
of activity and cost of change

01:16:30.510 --> 01:16:33.150
in line with proposed
service model changes,

01:16:33.150 --> 01:16:34.467
left shift productivity,

01:16:34.467 --> 01:16:35.760
and can we see that in the round

01:16:35.760 --> 01:16:36.990
and in year on year say

01:16:36.990 --> 01:16:38.940
is that where we're
headed or we adjusting?

01:16:38.940 --> 01:16:39.773
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

01:16:39.773 --> 01:16:42.060
- I think we need to be
realistic about the timeframes

01:16:42.060 --> 01:16:43.380
in which we'd be able to do it

01:16:43.380 --> 01:16:44.617
for that whole service.

01:16:44.617 --> 01:16:47.335
- Yes, no, I recognise that.

01:16:47.335 --> 01:16:48.168
- Paul.

01:16:48.168 --> 01:16:52.867
- I want to make a sort of
NHSE comment about grip,

01:16:54.210 --> 01:16:56.700
because I think what you're
describing isn't grip,

01:16:56.700 --> 01:16:57.903
it's relationships.

01:16:59.010 --> 01:17:01.440
And if you think about
people coming to you

01:17:01.440 --> 01:17:03.810
and you going to them, it isn't grip.

01:17:04.737 --> 01:17:07.470
And what you've done is
construct a set of relationships

01:17:07.470 --> 01:17:10.020
and they have a set of
relationships with you back.

01:17:10.020 --> 01:17:11.490
And the way in which historically

01:17:11.490 --> 01:17:14.040
NHSE has talked about
that, I think is wrong.

01:17:14.040 --> 01:17:17.040
I don't think it's grip, I
think it's a relationship,

01:17:17.040 --> 01:17:18.990
and relationships is not that.

01:17:18.990 --> 01:17:20.970
Relationships is both ways,

01:17:20.970 --> 01:17:23.730
and that's what I think
is being constructed.

01:17:23.730 --> 01:17:26.430
And what that means is once
you have a relationship,

01:17:26.430 --> 01:17:27.990
you can then start talking about,

01:17:27.990 --> 01:17:30.090
I'm going to CFOs and talking about,

01:17:30.090 --> 01:17:32.610
let's think about life expectancy.

01:17:32.610 --> 01:17:34.320
It's quite a difficult conversation,

01:17:34.320 --> 01:17:35.820
but now you've got a relationship,

01:17:35.820 --> 01:17:37.953
you can start to have those discussions.

01:17:39.000 --> 01:17:43.500
Whereas actually, if we just
telling them all the time,

01:17:43.500 --> 01:17:44.700
it's not a relationship.

01:17:45.870 --> 01:17:46.703
- Okay-
- So I think...

01:17:46.703 --> 01:17:50.040
Yeah, so it's a bit about
us having the assurance

01:17:50.040 --> 01:17:51.810
that we are spending the
money on the right things

01:17:51.810 --> 01:17:54.300
and in the most efficient way,

01:17:54.300 --> 01:17:56.190
and as you say, having a dialogue

01:17:56.190 --> 01:17:57.660
that enables those conversations.

01:17:57.660 --> 01:17:59.850
And I hope that we'll start to see,

01:17:59.850 --> 01:18:02.190
again, early days recognise that

01:18:02.190 --> 01:18:05.730
when we come onto the medium
term planning submissions,

01:18:05.730 --> 01:18:07.590
one would hope that the ICBs

01:18:07.590 --> 01:18:09.510
are starting to think in that way,

01:18:09.510 --> 01:18:13.023
where should they be spending
their money to improve health.

01:18:13.023 --> 01:18:14.967
- Yeah.
- Okay.

01:18:14.967 --> 01:18:16.920
And then, I mean, this leads nicely

01:18:16.920 --> 01:18:19.230
onto the productivity
and improvement, but-

01:18:19.230 --> 01:18:21.090
- I was just going to mention something,

01:18:21.090 --> 01:18:22.380
we've talked about quite a lot

01:18:22.380 --> 01:18:24.360
in the development of
the quality strategy,

01:18:24.360 --> 01:18:25.710
which is it all starts

01:18:25.710 --> 01:18:27.279
from value-based commissioning.

01:18:27.279 --> 01:18:28.590
- Absolutely.
- How do we determine

01:18:28.590 --> 01:18:30.090
what value is at that point,

01:18:30.090 --> 01:18:32.400
and it's going to be different
for different systems.

01:18:32.400 --> 01:18:33.750
And if we get that right,

01:18:33.750 --> 01:18:35.130
everything that follows on from there

01:18:35.130 --> 01:18:36.360
is actually going to be better.

01:18:36.360 --> 01:18:38.160
- Exactly, so the quality strategy

01:18:38.160 --> 01:18:40.400
will almost set that out hopefully,

01:18:40.400 --> 01:18:43.680
or some of it sets us
in the right direction.

01:18:43.680 --> 01:18:44.679
Good, okay.

01:18:44.679 --> 01:18:46.846
- And we've got an old CFO

01:18:48.360 --> 01:18:50.910
gathering again coming up in February,

01:18:50.910 --> 01:18:54.090
and Jeremy and Mark are coming along.

01:18:54.090 --> 01:18:57.090
So a number of CFOs have
asked me about roles,

01:18:57.090 --> 01:18:58.170
the roles of the Board,

01:18:58.170 --> 01:19:00.690
and the type of relationship
you'd want with a NED

01:19:00.690 --> 01:19:02.010
and what could be offered.

01:19:02.010 --> 01:19:04.110
Mark keeps asking me at
one of these meetings,

01:19:04.110 --> 01:19:05.220
so how is it happening?

01:19:05.220 --> 01:19:06.750
So I thought he could come to the troops

01:19:06.750 --> 01:19:08.460
and ask them.

01:19:08.460 --> 01:19:09.371
- Take your laptop.

01:19:12.720 --> 01:19:14.070
- Very good.

01:19:14.070 --> 01:19:15.030
Productivity?

01:19:15.030 --> 01:19:20.030
- Yeah, so I've had more
feedback on this paper

01:19:20.400 --> 01:19:22.240
than I did on the finance one.

01:19:22.240 --> 01:19:24.420
So clearly, we need to think about

01:19:24.420 --> 01:19:26.820
how we write our papers as well.

01:19:26.820 --> 01:19:28.800
But headlines to draw out I suppose.

01:19:28.800 --> 01:19:32.460
So what would I say?

01:19:32.460 --> 01:19:36.030
So productivity, performance,
really encouraging,

01:19:36.030 --> 01:19:38.170
not complacent in the acute sector,

01:19:38.170 --> 01:19:41.920
2.7% improvement for '24-'25 as a whole,

01:19:43.009 --> 01:19:47.874
and then in the first half
of '25-'26, similar levels,

01:19:47.874 --> 01:19:50.880
and further improvements
within the non-acute sector.

01:19:50.880 --> 01:19:52.560
So we are well placed, I guess,

01:19:52.560 --> 01:19:55.740
from a treasury target
perspective in our SR,

01:19:55.740 --> 01:19:59.910
where we are asked to do
2% productivity per annum,

01:19:59.910 --> 01:20:00.843
three-year period.

01:20:01.680 --> 01:20:04.260
Critically, this trajectory
would actually get us

01:20:04.260 --> 01:20:07.773
to a place where we close
the COVID productivity gap.

01:20:08.790 --> 01:20:10.953
So we return to the an NHS,

01:20:11.820 --> 01:20:15.243
which would be pre-pandemic
product productivity levels.

01:20:16.110 --> 01:20:18.300
Penny's going to say in a minute-
- What in the next,

01:20:18.300 --> 01:20:19.590
At what time?
- By the end of the SR.

01:20:19.590 --> 01:20:21.510
- Okay.
- Penny's now going to say

01:20:21.510 --> 01:20:24.723
that's now fast enough, and we know that.

01:20:25.560 --> 01:20:27.540
But what we are doing is
we are looking at this

01:20:27.540 --> 01:20:29.250
through three phases of productivity,

01:20:29.250 --> 01:20:32.070
and a lot of conversation
that we have this afternoon.

01:20:32.070 --> 01:20:35.550
We are phase one, which we're almost in.

01:20:35.550 --> 01:20:39.030
So for '26-'27 is quite operational

01:20:39.030 --> 01:20:40.287
and clinical improvement

01:20:40.287 --> 01:20:41.640
and the work that we are doing

01:20:41.640 --> 01:20:43.263
with Sarah-Jane and Mark.

01:20:44.250 --> 01:20:47.340
So we are looking at
it on the time horizon.

01:20:47.340 --> 01:20:49.806
What I did want to note
is through the SR period,

01:20:49.806 --> 01:20:52.080
the NHS had quite a lot of challenge

01:20:52.080 --> 01:20:56.310
as to whether or not 2%
was it far high enough,

01:20:56.310 --> 01:20:58.890
and we had a lot of engagement

01:20:58.890 --> 01:21:00.600
through the summer months to demonstrate

01:21:00.600 --> 01:21:03.153
why this did feel like a shift.

01:21:04.200 --> 01:21:07.740
And what I would say is if you compare us

01:21:07.740 --> 01:21:09.090
to what's happening

01:21:09.090 --> 01:21:11.370
and what's happening
across the wider economy,

01:21:11.370 --> 01:21:14.100
at no 0.8% over this period,

01:21:14.100 --> 01:21:16.710
it looks like we are
challenging ourselves.

01:21:16.710 --> 01:21:18.210
Now, we will always challenge ourselves

01:21:18.210 --> 01:21:19.890
to go further and faster of course,

01:21:19.890 --> 01:21:22.200
but good progress there.

01:21:22.200 --> 01:21:23.760
And then within the report,

01:21:23.760 --> 01:21:25.020
we've mentioned, you know,

01:21:25.020 --> 01:21:27.510
the Wayfinder and the Dora AI examples

01:21:27.510 --> 01:21:29.050
which just bring this to life

01:21:29.910 --> 01:21:31.110
through the EPEG group,

01:21:31.110 --> 01:21:33.180
which is an efficiency
and productivity group,

01:21:33.180 --> 01:21:36.750
which is multidisciplinary
across NHS England.

01:21:36.750 --> 01:21:38.670
Brilliant conversations from people

01:21:38.670 --> 01:21:40.770
in many of the execs teams,

01:21:40.770 --> 01:21:43.020
the execs come along as well.

01:21:43.020 --> 01:21:45.750
But when the conversation
gets really heated

01:21:45.750 --> 01:21:48.750
and it feels to me it's
whenever we are talking

01:21:48.750 --> 01:21:50.040
about the patient journey.

01:21:50.040 --> 01:21:51.360
So we are trying to bring that

01:21:51.360 --> 01:21:53.250
through a lot more in our conversations.

01:21:53.250 --> 01:21:56.831
But what you can see is across '25-'26,

01:21:56.831 --> 01:22:01.770
length of stay is falling
across our bed base.

01:22:01.770 --> 01:22:04.710
You can see we are doing
more same day care,

01:22:04.710 --> 01:22:06.510
avoiding night overnight stays,

01:22:06.510 --> 01:22:07.508
which is a lot of the work

01:22:07.508 --> 01:22:10.290
that Sarah-Jane has been pushing us on.

01:22:10.290 --> 01:22:13.650
And then very large reductions
in temporary staffing.

01:22:13.650 --> 01:22:16.200
And at the chief execs event last week,

01:22:16.200 --> 01:22:19.050
I put up a slide, and it
was fantastic actually

01:22:19.050 --> 01:22:20.790
to see some of those numbers.

01:22:20.790 --> 01:22:22.237
Quite often people say,

01:22:22.237 --> 01:22:24.210
"Oh, we are taking out the cost,"

01:22:24.210 --> 01:22:27.570
but does that mean it
impacts on performance,

01:22:27.570 --> 01:22:30.600
and increasingly we can
point to the examples

01:22:30.600 --> 01:22:33.030
where there are some organisations

01:22:33.030 --> 01:22:34.740
where they've been challenged
for quite a long time

01:22:34.740 --> 01:22:36.120
and they are really are doing it all.

01:22:36.120 --> 01:22:37.740
So it's an improvement trajectory

01:22:37.740 --> 01:22:38.640
and they're not quite there,

01:22:38.640 --> 01:22:40.320
but really something to celebrate.

01:22:40.320 --> 01:22:42.750
And we've had a lot of
feedback about that,

01:22:42.750 --> 01:22:45.000
didn't we Jim, in terms of the
way the presentations went.

01:22:45.000 --> 01:22:47.433
So not complacent as I say.

01:22:48.420 --> 01:22:51.030
And then I guess the large,

01:22:51.030 --> 01:22:53.490
the biggest sources of
productivity opportunity

01:22:53.490 --> 01:22:55.980
for us are technology and left shift.

01:22:55.980 --> 01:22:58.740
So we might want to have
a conversation about that.

01:22:58.740 --> 01:23:00.120
And then just to highlight,

01:23:00.120 --> 01:23:03.060
so this Board has really
challenged NHS England

01:23:03.060 --> 01:23:06.240
and me quite hard about transparency,

01:23:06.240 --> 01:23:08.340
so there is a detailed guide

01:23:08.340 --> 01:23:10.680
on how we calculate productivity,

01:23:10.680 --> 01:23:12.677
quite meaty guide that's going to go out

01:23:12.677 --> 01:23:15.750
on the 12th of February along with,

01:23:15.750 --> 01:23:19.560
we'll start publishing monthly
productivity information

01:23:19.560 --> 01:23:20.910
at a trust level.

01:23:20.910 --> 01:23:23.100
I've been told this doesn't
happen anywhere else

01:23:23.100 --> 01:23:24.090
in the world apparently.

01:23:24.090 --> 01:23:27.810
So that's a first for
us and our little team.

01:23:27.810 --> 01:23:29.850
But this will give patients and clinicians

01:23:29.850 --> 01:23:33.090
and leaders a much clearer
progress of what's going on.

01:23:33.090 --> 01:23:35.703
Recognising the point
that Jim made about data,

01:23:36.600 --> 01:23:39.360
in the past, we would've
tried to polish the data,

01:23:39.360 --> 01:23:40.827
and then we would've resisted sharing it,

01:23:40.827 --> 01:23:44.280
and I think we doing quite
the reverse this time.

01:23:44.280 --> 01:23:46.227
And then with thanks to Penny as well,

01:23:46.227 --> 01:23:48.570
and a number of her contacts,

01:23:48.570 --> 01:23:51.900
tomorrow actually we've got a
number of the think tanks in

01:23:51.900 --> 01:23:53.370
and some really key stakeholders

01:23:53.370 --> 01:23:55.200
to take them through our methodology,

01:23:55.200 --> 01:23:58.830
and the feedback to date's
been incredibly positive,

01:23:58.830 --> 01:24:02.010
so much so I think others are looking

01:24:02.010 --> 01:24:03.944
at joining forces with us,

01:24:03.944 --> 01:24:05.520
and changing some of the ways

01:24:05.520 --> 01:24:08.880
that they look at their metrics as well.

01:24:08.880 --> 01:24:11.010
But yeah, so we rise into
the challenge I would say,

01:24:11.010 --> 01:24:12.660
but there's always so much more we can do.

01:24:12.660 --> 01:24:15.240
- Okay, good, comments, questions?

01:24:15.240 --> 01:24:19.413
I've got Mark, I've got Ravi,
Robert, Jeremy, and Andrew.

01:24:21.030 --> 01:24:21.863
- So-

01:24:21.863 --> 01:24:23.850
- I'll take them all together.

01:24:23.850 --> 01:24:25.140
- One, thank you,

01:24:25.140 --> 01:24:26.940
I really enjoyed reading this paper.

01:24:29.490 --> 01:24:31.140
So when you think of productivity,

01:24:31.140 --> 01:24:32.970
there's always two elements to it.

01:24:32.970 --> 01:24:37.970
One, there's operational
day-to-day incremental improvement,

01:24:38.070 --> 01:24:39.720
which is clearly flowing
through the system,

01:24:39.720 --> 01:24:41.550
and that I don't think there's any denial

01:24:41.550 --> 01:24:43.410
that the numbers you're showing

01:24:43.410 --> 01:24:46.210
over the last two years looking
forward are really good.

01:24:48.060 --> 01:24:51.480
There's a brilliant
sentence in paragraph 17

01:24:51.480 --> 01:24:53.493
about pushing the frontier,

01:24:54.630 --> 01:24:57.450
and I wondered, is
there a way of capturing

01:24:57.450 --> 01:24:59.130
what pushing the frontier means?

01:24:59.130 --> 01:25:01.170
In actual numbers, we could understand

01:25:01.170 --> 01:25:03.693
whether it's treatments per doctor,

01:25:05.700 --> 01:25:07.230
which we could keep coming back to,

01:25:07.230 --> 01:25:08.940
because at the moment I'm not sure,

01:25:08.940 --> 01:25:11.370
we can quite imagine the scale

01:25:11.370 --> 01:25:13.320
of what pushing the frontier means,

01:25:13.320 --> 01:25:15.660
and without that sort
of leap in imagination,

01:25:15.660 --> 01:25:19.290
it's then quite hard to
push the reorganisation

01:25:19.290 --> 01:25:21.630
of the system to get there.

01:25:21.630 --> 01:25:23.733
- Yeah, great point, Ravi.

01:25:24.870 --> 01:25:26.790
- Sort of follows on from
that in paragraph 20,

01:25:26.790 --> 01:25:29.670
you talk about Wayfinder
being particularly significant

01:25:29.670 --> 01:25:31.740
in terms of how effective it is,

01:25:31.740 --> 01:25:33.870
and also just getting people connected

01:25:33.870 --> 01:25:36.570
to the NHS app sees a
2 1/2 to 3% improvement

01:25:36.570 --> 01:25:37.560
in waiting list.

01:25:37.560 --> 01:25:39.000
But my question on both those is,

01:25:39.000 --> 01:25:42.090
what is our approach to driving adoption

01:25:42.090 --> 01:25:46.713
on the things that we are very,
very convinced are scalable,

01:25:48.000 --> 01:25:51.330
and how can we just go
gangbusters on that,

01:25:51.330 --> 01:25:54.390
because that's the fastest
route to proving this year?

01:25:54.390 --> 01:25:57.183
And then similarly, on
pushing the frontier,

01:25:58.653 --> 01:26:00.660
what is our way of managing the portfolio

01:26:00.660 --> 01:26:04.233
of bets on frontier innovations?

01:26:06.660 --> 01:26:07.833
- Okay, Robert?

01:26:08.730 --> 01:26:11.130
- Yeah, I think it's a great paper too,

01:26:11.130 --> 01:26:12.810
and it's encouraging.

01:26:12.810 --> 01:26:14.940
I just wanted to emphasise

01:26:14.940 --> 01:26:16.470
what you say in paragraph 19,

01:26:16.470 --> 01:26:18.270
start of the technology section,

01:26:18.270 --> 01:26:19.950
and it's picking up something

01:26:19.950 --> 01:26:23.100
that Mark reported back
from the data committee.

01:26:23.100 --> 01:26:25.080
I think there's a real opportunity

01:26:25.080 --> 01:26:29.070
for non-incremental gain
if we pull off the trick

01:26:29.070 --> 01:26:31.620
of integrating technology
with pathway redesign.

01:26:31.620 --> 01:26:34.080
At the moment, they're
not working together

01:26:34.080 --> 01:26:35.070
in the way they should.

01:26:35.070 --> 01:26:38.190
So I think at one of the
earlier data committees,

01:26:38.190 --> 01:26:41.460
someone was there who said
there are 3,700 individuals

01:26:41.460 --> 01:26:44.550
whose job is transformation
across this system.

01:26:44.550 --> 01:26:46.260
I just dunno who they
are, what they're doing,

01:26:46.260 --> 01:26:47.910
and have they got the capacity?

01:26:47.910 --> 01:26:50.490
Because if we are going to
get non-incremental change,

01:26:50.490 --> 01:26:53.790
it totally depends on
radical pathway redesign

01:26:53.790 --> 01:26:56.520
with the technology in the
conversation in the room.

01:26:56.520 --> 01:26:58.140
And I'm not convinced yet

01:26:58.140 --> 01:26:59.483
that it's actually happening.

01:27:00.900 --> 01:27:02.730
- Jeremy.
- Yeah, so it's a great paper.

01:27:02.730 --> 01:27:04.380
Inevitably, I think
efficient we tend to look

01:27:04.380 --> 01:27:07.260
at left to right, what's
in year, what's short term.

01:27:07.260 --> 01:27:09.810
And based on the conversations
a lot we've had today,

01:27:09.810 --> 01:27:11.970
I'd be really interested
in a three-year, five-year,

01:27:11.970 --> 01:27:14.340
10-year view as to where are we spending,

01:27:14.340 --> 01:27:15.720
what the prior is, how we linking it

01:27:15.720 --> 01:27:17.460
with the IT transformation,

01:27:17.460 --> 01:27:19.320
where are we placing the bets?

01:27:19.320 --> 01:27:22.440
Where are the biggest low, easiest-to-hit,

01:27:22.440 --> 01:27:25.170
biggest impact areas so that
we can get a better view

01:27:25.170 --> 01:27:26.670
of what that future looks like,

01:27:26.670 --> 01:27:29.130
'cause otherwise we're going
to be doing it week by week

01:27:29.130 --> 01:27:31.020
as opposed to "Here's the game plan,

01:27:31.020 --> 01:27:32.700
how are we going to execute it?"

01:27:32.700 --> 01:27:34.860
- Andrew.
- So my point's around

01:27:34.860 --> 01:27:36.480
creating the right incentives

01:27:36.480 --> 01:27:39.810
for follow up appointments going forward,

01:27:39.810 --> 01:27:43.590
and how are we going to kind
of cover the fixed costs

01:27:43.590 --> 01:27:45.270
that providers are stranded with.

01:27:45.270 --> 01:27:50.250
So from memory, 1/3 of a typical
DGH income is outpatients.

01:27:50.250 --> 01:27:52.170
So it's a big chunk of cash,

01:27:52.170 --> 01:27:55.020
and I think creating the right incentives

01:27:55.020 --> 01:27:57.930
so that organisations
don't feel disadvantaged

01:27:57.930 --> 01:28:01.170
if they run with this
at pace with clinicians

01:28:01.170 --> 01:28:02.550
is really, really important.

01:28:02.550 --> 01:28:05.280
And striking that balance
around what you're going to do

01:28:05.280 --> 01:28:08.103
with the fixed costs element
that are stranded there,

01:28:09.510 --> 01:28:12.030
I think is a really,
really important dimension

01:28:12.030 --> 01:28:14.280
to the potential success of this.

01:28:14.280 --> 01:28:15.750
- Yeah.
- Because it's big money.

01:28:15.750 --> 01:28:18.150
- So I think well, let me put these out,

01:28:18.150 --> 01:28:20.220
then because I think it's
actually for two of you

01:28:20.220 --> 01:28:22.020
to respond to it drawing

01:28:22.020 --> 01:28:23.820
on lots of friends around the room.

01:28:23.820 --> 01:28:27.333
But sorry, the first big
question is the what.

01:28:28.530 --> 01:28:30.090
What is it that we think is actually

01:28:30.090 --> 01:28:31.017
going to drive the productivity?

01:28:31.017 --> 01:28:33.450
And we have been asking
that in different ways

01:28:33.450 --> 01:28:36.050
for a while and linking
it to the 10-year plan.

01:28:36.050 --> 01:28:39.000
And I think we really do need to see that,

01:28:39.000 --> 01:28:40.110
because otherwise it's quite hard

01:28:40.110 --> 01:28:41.640
to interpret the data really.

01:28:41.640 --> 01:28:43.160
So in some ways a 7% improvement

01:28:43.160 --> 01:28:44.880
in mental health looks good,

01:28:44.880 --> 01:28:47.280
but if we think the opportunity is 20%

01:28:47.280 --> 01:28:49.290
within the current operating model,

01:28:49.290 --> 01:28:51.210
then we've still got a way to go.

01:28:51.210 --> 01:28:54.030
If we think it's 30% by using tech,

01:28:54.030 --> 01:28:56.280
then, you know, that starts to tell us

01:28:56.280 --> 01:28:58.110
where we should be focusing.

01:28:58.110 --> 01:28:59.130
And I think on all of these,

01:28:59.130 --> 01:29:02.310
it's a mixture of ops plus tech.

01:29:02.310 --> 01:29:04.690
So starting to sort of set that out

01:29:05.820 --> 01:29:07.470
in all the different areas would be good

01:29:07.470 --> 01:29:10.740
'cause then we know
where we need to focus,

01:29:10.740 --> 01:29:12.960
and the bit about Ravi's point,

01:29:12.960 --> 01:29:15.090
what's scalable, what
can we do quite quickly,

01:29:15.090 --> 01:29:17.280
what will take us three years, five years,

01:29:17.280 --> 01:29:19.070
10 years as Jeremy said.

01:29:19.070 --> 01:29:20.370
So that's all on the what.

01:29:20.370 --> 01:29:22.800
And then the second bit is on the how.

01:29:22.800 --> 01:29:24.000
And I think this is the bit

01:29:24.000 --> 01:29:26.250
where I think we don't see the link

01:29:26.250 --> 01:29:27.877
to the next paper on
the improvement paper.

01:29:27.877 --> 01:29:31.890
You know, what is our
improvement approach,

01:29:31.890 --> 01:29:34.020
both in terms of the
operational improvement

01:29:34.020 --> 01:29:38.490
but the tech transformation
that sits alongside that.

01:29:38.490 --> 01:29:39.840
And then as part of that,

01:29:39.840 --> 01:29:42.180
what are the incentives for change,

01:29:42.180 --> 01:29:45.090
and where we know we'll
have specific challenges

01:29:45.090 --> 01:29:46.890
such as around the fixed costs,

01:29:46.890 --> 01:29:48.916
what are very specific
things that we will do

01:29:48.916 --> 01:29:50.760
to support that?

01:29:50.760 --> 01:29:52.830
So maybe you and Paul, can-

01:29:52.830 --> 01:29:54.930
- Yeah, should I start?

01:29:54.930 --> 01:29:57.030
So there is a...

01:29:57.030 --> 01:29:59.640
We've got, which we can
share with the Board,

01:29:59.640 --> 01:30:01.740
I went through again last night,

01:30:01.740 --> 01:30:05.550
quite a meaty pack around a piece of paper

01:30:05.550 --> 01:30:07.710
that kind of the three phases
of the productivity plan.

01:30:07.710 --> 01:30:09.150
I know we've talked to Penny about it,

01:30:09.150 --> 01:30:12.390
but effectively is showing
like years one and two,

01:30:12.390 --> 01:30:15.907
and the things in year one and two

01:30:15.907 --> 01:30:18.720
are things you'd all recognise actually.

01:30:18.720 --> 01:30:21.120
And there's no big shakes or surprises,

01:30:21.120 --> 01:30:22.320
but there's quite a lot of opportunity

01:30:22.320 --> 01:30:24.150
to reduce variation in there,

01:30:24.150 --> 01:30:26.340
lots of workforce initiatives,

01:30:26.340 --> 01:30:28.080
more banking agency, job planning,

01:30:28.080 --> 01:30:29.820
all of the things that we've talked about,

01:30:29.820 --> 01:30:32.703
which we haven't done to
date very successfully,

01:30:33.900 --> 01:30:35.880
and quite a bit as well
on medicine's value

01:30:35.880 --> 01:30:37.170
and commercial opportunities.

01:30:37.170 --> 01:30:38.340
So that's kind of the year one.

01:30:38.340 --> 01:30:40.980
And then as we move on,

01:30:40.980 --> 01:30:44.160
then we do get into the new
tech and the left shift,

01:30:44.160 --> 01:30:45.510
which is why I referenced earlier

01:30:45.510 --> 01:30:50.430
about the kind of wayfarers,
and Wayfinders, sorry,

01:30:50.430 --> 01:30:55.430
and Dora, those are the
kind of the no-regret moves

01:30:56.070 --> 01:30:57.210
that you mentioned, Ravi,

01:30:57.210 --> 01:30:58.410
that we know that they work.

01:30:58.410 --> 01:31:00.110
So working with Jules and the team

01:31:01.020 --> 01:31:04.530
in a confined, limited amount of resource,

01:31:04.530 --> 01:31:07.770
how do we prioritise the
things that we know work,

01:31:07.770 --> 01:31:10.413
but it's not for us to
do it in NHS England,

01:31:10.413 --> 01:31:12.300
it's for us to enable
it out in the service.

01:31:12.300 --> 01:31:14.880
And I think that's the
challenge at the minute.

01:31:14.880 --> 01:31:16.860
So I think there's quite a bit there.

01:31:16.860 --> 01:31:18.030
On the frontier shifts then,

01:31:18.030 --> 01:31:19.110
the way I think about it,

01:31:19.110 --> 01:31:21.780
but we haven't had this
broader conversation.

01:31:21.780 --> 01:31:25.110
For me, it's when I'm
working with Joe and Penny

01:31:25.110 --> 01:31:26.970
on the 10-year workforce plan

01:31:26.970 --> 01:31:29.940
is the new workforce models
that we are describing.

01:31:29.940 --> 01:31:32.130
When we are working on
neighbourhood health

01:31:32.130 --> 01:31:34.680
and some of the archetypes,

01:31:34.680 --> 01:31:38.520
it's the moving care from
hospital and redesigning services,

01:31:38.520 --> 01:31:39.750
and then it's the tech work

01:31:39.750 --> 01:31:42.600
which I do feel like it
feels a bit separate to me

01:31:42.600 --> 01:31:46.560
and I'm trying to, maybe it's
just not my comfort area.

01:31:46.560 --> 01:31:49.680
So I'm trying to lean in and
appreciate that a bit more.

01:31:49.680 --> 01:31:50.940
But I do know there's bits of tech

01:31:50.940 --> 01:31:52.290
where I can see that it really works,

01:31:52.290 --> 01:31:54.330
and I want to garner it.

01:31:54.330 --> 01:31:55.830
And then through the finance team,

01:31:55.830 --> 01:31:57.900
what we are trying to do is link

01:31:57.900 --> 01:32:00.720
into all of these strategies
and pieces of work

01:32:00.720 --> 01:32:03.570
to create effectively the payment models

01:32:03.570 --> 01:32:05.970
as Andrew's described, and the incentives

01:32:05.970 --> 01:32:10.500
so that if there's a
service model in future,

01:32:10.500 --> 01:32:13.710
you pay for that pathway
almost like a should be cost,

01:32:13.710 --> 01:32:16.350
we talk about it 'cause it's
better than best practise,

01:32:16.350 --> 01:32:18.510
and people will have to
change their approach.

01:32:18.510 --> 01:32:20.760
But we need to make sure
they've got the tech,

01:32:20.760 --> 01:32:23.790
they've got the support to
change the way they're doing it,

01:32:23.790 --> 01:32:26.337
and financially, the flows work for them.

01:32:26.337 --> 01:32:28.620
And I think for me, that's the start

01:32:28.620 --> 01:32:30.270
of the bit of the model of change,

01:32:30.270 --> 01:32:32.760
as well as going back to what Paul said.

01:32:32.760 --> 01:32:35.160
So I think the success
of the finance community,

01:32:35.160 --> 01:32:38.580
and actually what this
broader team has done

01:32:38.580 --> 01:32:42.240
in the last nine months has
been about the relationships.

01:32:42.240 --> 01:32:43.230
I don't think I've ever gone

01:32:43.230 --> 01:32:44.880
and told somebody what to do,

01:32:44.880 --> 01:32:48.570
but I've expressed a view
and said more importantly,

01:32:48.570 --> 01:32:50.610
I can see somebody over
there that's doing it

01:32:50.610 --> 01:32:52.800
and they might have been further back

01:32:52.800 --> 01:32:55.710
than that individual or our system was.

01:32:55.710 --> 01:32:58.290
And that seems to get a bit of momentum.

01:32:58.290 --> 01:32:59.595
So, some on there.

01:32:59.595 --> 01:33:01.440
- Okay, and Paul, do you want to come in

01:33:01.440 --> 01:33:03.840
and also pick up the fixed cost please?

01:33:03.840 --> 01:33:04.673
- Oh yeah.

01:33:06.570 --> 01:33:07.403
- We're asking you a lot.

01:33:07.403 --> 01:33:08.563
On the fixed costs.

01:33:08.563 --> 01:33:11.130
- I'll do the fixed cost, yeah.

01:33:11.130 --> 01:33:12.666
- We can come back to fixed cost.

01:33:12.666 --> 01:33:15.240
- I think a lot of the same themes

01:33:15.240 --> 01:33:17.520
through various discussions
this morning, right,

01:33:17.520 --> 01:33:19.050
and the ones that we've had in indeed

01:33:19.050 --> 01:33:20.970
that as well in particular.

01:33:20.970 --> 01:33:23.670
So the work that we are
doing , and I say we,

01:33:23.670 --> 01:33:26.520
I mean, we collectively
across various members

01:33:26.520 --> 01:33:28.450
of the exec team and our colleagues

01:33:29.400 --> 01:33:30.900
I think is going to be
critical to pull this together,

01:33:30.900 --> 01:33:33.960
and what that will look
like is anchoring ourselves

01:33:33.960 --> 01:33:36.300
on what are the outcomes
that we're trying to achieve

01:33:36.300 --> 01:33:38.820
over the next one, two,
three years, in particular,

01:33:38.820 --> 01:33:41.280
around sort of performance
commitments and productivity?

01:33:41.280 --> 01:33:43.440
What are the longer
term outcome commitments

01:33:43.440 --> 01:33:44.820
that we're trying to get to

01:33:44.820 --> 01:33:47.880
in terms of genuine
increases and reductions

01:33:47.880 --> 01:33:50.613
in inequality around
healthy life expectancy?

01:33:51.690 --> 01:33:53.730
We then, as we've all
been saying, I think,

01:33:53.730 --> 01:33:55.593
several of us today, need to make sure

01:33:55.593 --> 01:33:59.610
that we've got genuinely
integrated approaches

01:33:59.610 --> 01:34:01.620
to the things that we've identified

01:34:01.620 --> 01:34:02.453
that are going to get us there.

01:34:02.453 --> 01:34:05.793
So all of the pathway
redesign work, the MSFs,

01:34:07.950 --> 01:34:10.740
and the various other
strategies that link to them,

01:34:10.740 --> 01:34:12.060
need to be developed jointly,

01:34:12.060 --> 01:34:14.940
which is what we're kicking
off in some fundamental areas.

01:34:14.940 --> 01:34:17.910
And we need to be able to
describe through that work,

01:34:17.910 --> 01:34:21.870
what the combination of
left shift and productivity,

01:34:21.870 --> 01:34:24.000
releasing benefits we're going to achieve.

01:34:24.000 --> 01:34:25.740
Some of which in the near term

01:34:25.740 --> 01:34:29.550
will be fairly incremental as
we get further out should be,

01:34:29.550 --> 01:34:31.590
I think, genuinely accelerated.

01:34:31.590 --> 01:34:34.500
And all of that, I think the ingredients

01:34:34.500 --> 01:34:36.747
for all of that work
are in the 10-year plan,

01:34:36.747 --> 01:34:38.820
and I think our job is
to pull that together

01:34:38.820 --> 01:34:40.260
in a structured way

01:34:40.260 --> 01:34:42.780
with some proper analytical
frameworks around it as well,

01:34:42.780 --> 01:34:45.480
such that the Board are able collectively

01:34:45.480 --> 01:34:47.610
to assure yourselves

01:34:47.610 --> 01:34:49.830
that actually we're on
track and delivering it.

01:34:49.830 --> 01:34:50.663
- Okay.
- I would just add to that

01:34:50.663 --> 01:34:53.250
'cause I appreciate, Paul
and I haven't caught up

01:34:53.250 --> 01:34:55.950
about the productivity plan

01:34:55.950 --> 01:34:58.950
that's gone through EPEG as such.

01:34:58.950 --> 01:35:00.570
So there's the makings of that,

01:35:00.570 --> 01:35:03.060
the analytical information depend,

01:35:03.060 --> 01:35:04.770
year one and two is quite robust

01:35:04.770 --> 01:35:06.210
and then I guess a bit flaky

01:35:06.210 --> 01:35:07.770
to be honest as it progresses,

01:35:07.770 --> 01:35:09.390
but all the ideas are there,

01:35:09.390 --> 01:35:11.943
and importantly a lot of clinical buy-in.

01:35:12.870 --> 01:35:16.053
The other thing I would
say about is, so Jim,

01:35:17.070 --> 01:35:19.140
a long time ago, and I think
you've talked about this

01:35:19.140 --> 01:35:23.130
for years actually,
about us putting in EPRs

01:35:23.130 --> 01:35:24.780
before the clinical strategies are ready,

01:35:24.780 --> 01:35:26.670
so it goes back to Robert's point.

01:35:26.670 --> 01:35:29.757
So now we've got a different
process for putting in EPRs

01:35:29.757 --> 01:35:33.000
and actually our business case
approvals process as well.

01:35:33.000 --> 01:35:34.770
So we are looking at
the behavioural change

01:35:34.770 --> 01:35:35.850
that we are expecting,

01:35:35.850 --> 01:35:36.683
and we are actually looking

01:35:36.683 --> 01:35:38.610
at the clinical strategies as well

01:35:38.610 --> 01:35:41.100
rather than here's a dollop of money,

01:35:41.100 --> 01:35:42.930
and we'll see you in five years time

01:35:42.930 --> 01:35:44.070
and see if it worked.

01:35:44.070 --> 01:35:46.140
- Okay.
- On the fixed costs,

01:35:46.140 --> 01:35:47.817
I'd love to say there
was an answer to this,

01:35:47.817 --> 01:35:51.780
and I think Andrew, in effect,

01:35:51.780 --> 01:35:53.220
what I'm always pushing through

01:35:53.220 --> 01:35:55.350
the neighbourhood health conversation is

01:35:55.350 --> 01:35:58.230
that there's skin in the
game for all partners

01:35:58.230 --> 01:36:00.300
because I don't think...

01:36:00.300 --> 01:36:01.980
I think we need, you know,

01:36:01.980 --> 01:36:03.120
if it's going to be out of hospital,

01:36:03.120 --> 01:36:05.130
it's got to be pushed
and it'll be held onto

01:36:05.130 --> 01:36:08.643
unless there is sufficient
skin in the game for everyone.

01:36:09.510 --> 01:36:11.670
But also, I would just
say a bit of a shout out

01:36:11.670 --> 01:36:14.640
to the CFO community, you know,

01:36:14.640 --> 01:36:17.310
we have created headroom
collectively this year

01:36:17.310 --> 01:36:20.970
in order to fund industrial
action and other things.

01:36:20.970 --> 01:36:22.500
I'd love to be able to do that again,

01:36:22.500 --> 01:36:23.880
but not to fund deficits.

01:36:23.880 --> 01:36:27.420
So you know, people submit
your plans in February,

01:36:27.420 --> 01:36:29.700
balanced plans that stack up.

01:36:29.700 --> 01:36:31.560
We've been doing planning since June,

01:36:31.560 --> 01:36:34.113
so there's no reason why
that can't be a reality.

01:36:35.220 --> 01:36:37.410
Get the balance plans in,
get on to deliver them.

01:36:37.410 --> 01:36:40.503
I've already seen that
cost improvement plans,

01:36:41.730 --> 01:36:42.930
they're not fully baked in

01:36:42.930 --> 01:36:44.340
but they're much more advanced

01:36:44.340 --> 01:36:46.260
than we've seen in previous years.

01:36:46.260 --> 01:36:48.090
And then if there's headroom next year,

01:36:48.090 --> 01:36:49.380
there's headroom, we will share,

01:36:49.380 --> 01:36:52.560
we will absolutely work on
how can we make that change.

01:36:52.560 --> 01:36:55.920
But currently, I would
be anxious about that

01:36:55.920 --> 01:36:56.760
if I'm honest, Penny,

01:36:56.760 --> 01:36:58.350
'cause I wouldn't want another month nine

01:36:58.350 --> 01:36:59.790
where we are looking around

01:36:59.790 --> 01:37:02.280
to see if there's underspends to overspend

01:37:02.280 --> 01:37:04.290
because Boards have overspent.

01:37:04.290 --> 01:37:05.130
- Yeah, okay.

01:37:05.130 --> 01:37:07.367
So I think we've made a
lot of progress on this

01:37:07.367 --> 01:37:10.290
in the last 10 months, which is great,

01:37:10.290 --> 01:37:12.390
I think we've got a lot more clarity.

01:37:12.390 --> 01:37:15.870
But I think the strong
message from the Board

01:37:15.870 --> 01:37:18.663
is we need more clarity on
exactly what we're going to do,

01:37:19.590 --> 01:37:21.270
and crucially how we're going to do it,

01:37:21.270 --> 01:37:23.373
which is the next item.

01:37:24.720 --> 01:37:26.610
I do wonder on cost improvement plans

01:37:26.610 --> 01:37:28.350
whether that's the right language,

01:37:28.350 --> 01:37:30.183
and actually I was going to say

01:37:30.183 --> 01:37:31.166
productivity improvement plans,

01:37:31.166 --> 01:37:32.673
but then that's PIPs and that sits

01:37:32.673 --> 01:37:34.170
in a whole nother bit of government,

01:37:34.170 --> 01:37:35.400
so we don't want to go there.

01:37:35.400 --> 01:37:36.630
But I think there is something

01:37:36.630 --> 01:37:38.070
about being clear on that

01:37:38.070 --> 01:37:40.318
'cause it's different-
- Just on that,

01:37:40.318 --> 01:37:43.680
I think you can unpack your productivity

01:37:43.680 --> 01:37:44.513
either through doing more activity

01:37:44.513 --> 01:37:45.346
either through doing more activity

01:37:45.346 --> 01:37:46.800
or optimising your cost base.
- Yep.

01:37:46.800 --> 01:37:47.970
- And so I think it's important

01:37:47.970 --> 01:37:49.830
that we separate out those two things

01:37:49.830 --> 01:37:51.000
and the individual providers

01:37:51.000 --> 01:37:53.490
as we share their
productivity data with them

01:37:53.490 --> 01:37:55.710
are able to understand that cascades

01:37:55.710 --> 01:37:56.850
through their organisations

01:37:56.850 --> 01:37:59.640
and is consistent with
whatever contracts, et cetera.

01:37:59.640 --> 01:38:01.560
So I think actually it's
an important distinction.

01:38:01.560 --> 01:38:03.560
- Yeah, okay, right, we need to move on.

01:38:04.470 --> 01:38:07.500
So I propose no more than
10 minutes on this item,

01:38:07.500 --> 01:38:09.000
keep us back on track,

01:38:09.000 --> 01:38:11.940
well, partly because we
have already discussed it.

01:38:11.940 --> 01:38:15.660
I think the crucial bit is
we've had the conversation

01:38:15.660 --> 01:38:18.393
about quality and life expectancy,

01:38:19.350 --> 01:38:21.270
focusing on secondary
prevention effectiveness.

01:38:21.270 --> 01:38:23.310
We've had the conversation
about productivity,

01:38:23.310 --> 01:38:27.360
and obviously those two sit
very closely intertwined.

01:38:27.360 --> 01:38:29.340
What we need assurance on now

01:38:29.340 --> 01:38:32.730
is that we have a mechanism for the system

01:38:32.730 --> 01:38:35.760
to essentially be self-improving,

01:38:35.760 --> 01:38:37.800
and what are we doing to support that.

01:38:37.800 --> 01:38:39.000
- Yeah, thanks Penny.

01:38:39.000 --> 01:38:40.890
Yeah, I was just going to
say that in the opening,

01:38:40.890 --> 01:38:43.200
just to reminder that this
is really about the how,

01:38:43.200 --> 01:38:45.210
and there is a job
obviously following this

01:38:45.210 --> 01:38:50.210
to connect it to what we want
to actually focus attention on

01:38:50.250 --> 01:38:51.900
as we move forwards.

01:38:51.900 --> 01:38:54.360
Just to say that this
paper, to remind colleagues,

01:38:54.360 --> 01:38:56.250
has been built through a collaboration

01:38:56.250 --> 01:38:59.913
between colleagues, both
nationally and regionally,

01:39:00.780 --> 01:39:02.340
those that are focused on improvement,

01:39:02.340 --> 01:39:04.620
but also the operational
and performance teams.

01:39:04.620 --> 01:39:06.390
So this is the people that are sort of

01:39:06.390 --> 01:39:09.330
at the sharp end of delivery,

01:39:09.330 --> 01:39:10.500
and obviously crucially,

01:39:10.500 --> 01:39:13.170
with chief exec, chief operating officer,

01:39:13.170 --> 01:39:14.940
and other clinical leaders
through the system.

01:39:14.940 --> 01:39:16.620
So it's a result of, I think,

01:39:16.620 --> 01:39:18.570
that was one of the challenges
the Board gave last time,

01:39:18.570 --> 01:39:22.530
is this really given the
NHS what it would want.

01:39:22.530 --> 01:39:24.870
So we've set out, in paragraph six,

01:39:24.870 --> 01:39:26.790
you can see the design principles

01:39:26.790 --> 01:39:28.980
of what we are building on.

01:39:28.980 --> 01:39:31.800
So I'll just pick out a few of those.

01:39:31.800 --> 01:39:33.750
It is for providers themselves

01:39:33.750 --> 01:39:35.790
to have suitable improvement programmes

01:39:35.790 --> 01:39:38.760
aligned to the objectives that
they are trying to deliver,

01:39:38.760 --> 01:39:41.880
and for those to be focused
across whole pathways

01:39:41.880 --> 01:39:43.710
and be sort of embedded

01:39:43.710 --> 01:39:46.620
in staff and patient
and public involvement.

01:39:46.620 --> 01:39:49.183
We are not nationally
trying to design a system

01:39:49.183 --> 01:39:52.320
to ensure that that improvement
approach is in every trust.

01:39:52.320 --> 01:39:55.200
And we know by and large that is the case.

01:39:55.200 --> 01:39:58.200
But as we think about NHS England's role,

01:39:58.200 --> 01:40:03.060
we really feel strongly that
this is about the improvement,

01:40:03.060 --> 01:40:06.300
support, and approach for all providers,

01:40:06.300 --> 01:40:09.450
including those that are
in the middle grouping.

01:40:09.450 --> 01:40:11.040
Because I think over the last few years,

01:40:11.040 --> 01:40:12.600
a lot of our improvement offers

01:40:12.600 --> 01:40:15.180
have been highlighting the outstanding

01:40:15.180 --> 01:40:16.560
and trying to scoop up those

01:40:16.560 --> 01:40:18.840
that are at the tail of performance.

01:40:18.840 --> 01:40:21.363
This is very much
focused on a whole offer.

01:40:22.380 --> 01:40:26.280
It has to be now across
pathways and sectors,

01:40:26.280 --> 01:40:28.560
not organisations.

01:40:28.560 --> 01:40:30.975
Having organisational
methodologies won't work

01:40:30.975 --> 01:40:34.290
for some of the shifts
that we need to make,

01:40:34.290 --> 01:40:37.770
and obviously it needs to
be aligned to 10-year plan,

01:40:37.770 --> 01:40:39.570
and other things that are really important

01:40:39.570 --> 01:40:41.133
in terms of assessing where providers are,

01:40:41.133 --> 01:40:43.680
like the National Oversight
Framework, et cetera.

01:40:43.680 --> 01:40:45.870
So it's built on those
following principles,

01:40:45.870 --> 01:40:47.700
and I think that the NHS community

01:40:47.700 --> 01:40:48.990
felt quite strongly about that.

01:40:48.990 --> 01:40:50.160
It's not for NHS England

01:40:50.160 --> 01:40:52.680
to decide how all improvement works.

01:40:52.680 --> 01:40:56.310
But when comes to the
distinctive role of the centre,

01:40:56.310 --> 01:40:58.380
then we've got four areas.

01:40:58.380 --> 01:41:01.254
So the first is that we
set strategy priorities,

01:41:01.254 --> 01:41:03.810
codify practise, and identify

01:41:03.810 --> 01:41:05.310
the opportunities for improvement.

01:41:05.310 --> 01:41:07.800
We've talked about quite a lot
of those here already today,

01:41:07.800 --> 01:41:09.690
modern surface frameworks,

01:41:09.690 --> 01:41:10.920
that's a lot of what our GIRFT

01:41:10.920 --> 01:41:14.460
Getting It Right First
Time teams do already.

01:41:14.460 --> 01:41:16.740
We've got the emergence
of the model series,

01:41:16.740 --> 01:41:19.200
so things like the model
emergency department,

01:41:19.200 --> 01:41:20.673
which will emerge soon,

01:41:21.720 --> 01:41:25.680
and various other blueprints and guides.

01:41:25.680 --> 01:41:27.903
I think on the technology side,

01:41:29.040 --> 01:41:30.780
we need to push in the same way.

01:41:30.780 --> 01:41:33.480
So we need to be absolutely codifying

01:41:33.480 --> 01:41:34.590
what the best practise

01:41:34.590 --> 01:41:37.140
in terms of some of these
technologies and rollouts

01:41:37.140 --> 01:41:39.480
and opportunities for productivity are.

01:41:39.480 --> 01:41:41.910
But our job is to codify that.

01:41:41.910 --> 01:41:45.570
And for many organisations,
that will be enough.

01:41:45.570 --> 01:41:48.600
They'll be able to take
that guide, that blueprint.

01:41:48.600 --> 01:41:51.990
It'll be describing the
best of the NHS anyway,

01:41:51.990 --> 01:41:53.490
and there'll be a big group of people

01:41:53.490 --> 01:41:55.140
that will run and embrace that.

01:41:55.140 --> 01:41:56.400
Obviously not everybody will.

01:41:56.400 --> 01:41:58.320
So we need to think about
how we support those

01:41:58.320 --> 01:42:00.170
that will need more help and support.

01:42:01.350 --> 01:42:04.410
The other thing that we
think we bring value to

01:42:04.410 --> 01:42:06.690
as NHS England is the support

01:42:06.690 --> 01:42:08.220
for the strengthening of leadership

01:42:08.220 --> 01:42:10.440
and management around improvement

01:42:10.440 --> 01:42:11.520
right across the Board.

01:42:11.520 --> 01:42:14.010
We know that many of our clinical

01:42:14.010 --> 01:42:17.040
and operational teams have
lost the improvement skill.

01:42:17.040 --> 01:42:18.510
We've taken a step into this

01:42:18.510 --> 01:42:21.090
with clinical operational
training this year.

01:42:21.090 --> 01:42:23.560
But we found that, as
we meet certain groups

01:42:24.690 --> 01:42:25.800
within organisations

01:42:25.800 --> 01:42:27.360
and start to skill them up,

01:42:27.360 --> 01:42:28.740
they're trying to work with other people

01:42:28.740 --> 01:42:30.300
that don't have the skill.

01:42:30.300 --> 01:42:33.840
So expanding that into our
more senior management cohorts

01:42:33.840 --> 01:42:37.510
and out into Board
development is a crucial role

01:42:38.430 --> 01:42:39.570
that we play in that as well.

01:42:39.570 --> 01:42:42.240
And then that also
stretches out into support

01:42:42.240 --> 01:42:44.670
around strategic commissioning
of value-based commission

01:42:44.670 --> 01:42:46.950
that we've also talked about.

01:42:46.950 --> 01:42:49.410
We do then think, and
again I would stress,

01:42:49.410 --> 01:42:52.650
this came very strongly from
the chief exec community

01:42:52.650 --> 01:42:56.700
that there is power intake
in a small number of things,

01:42:56.700 --> 01:42:58.200
probably two or three a year,

01:42:58.200 --> 01:43:00.450
although I'm sure colleagues
may challenge on that.

01:43:00.450 --> 01:43:03.090
But taking two or three things a year

01:43:03.090 --> 01:43:06.300
where we have a really big
strategic push on them,

01:43:06.300 --> 01:43:09.330
they're an all in endeavour
and everybody has to buy in

01:43:09.330 --> 01:43:11.490
and do them so they don't become optional,

01:43:11.490 --> 01:43:15.000
they don't become for
every Board to negotiate

01:43:15.000 --> 01:43:18.120
whether that's the right thing
in their patch, et cetera.

01:43:18.120 --> 01:43:19.290
We've been trialling,

01:43:19.290 --> 01:43:22.080
learning and improvement
networks this year,

01:43:22.080 --> 01:43:23.400
we've got 21 of them,

01:43:23.400 --> 01:43:25.740
they're led by some of
our best chief execs.

01:43:25.740 --> 01:43:27.990
There's a sense that they're
really starting to work

01:43:27.990 --> 01:43:29.560
as a mechanism to do that

01:43:30.840 --> 01:43:32.580
and they are starting to be a lot more,

01:43:32.580 --> 01:43:36.780
all embracing again in terms
of pathways and engagement.

01:43:36.780 --> 01:43:40.410
So there is a sort of a sense

01:43:40.410 --> 01:43:42.360
that we could use that as a vehicle

01:43:42.360 --> 01:43:46.410
so the best in the NHS
can lead the rest through,

01:43:46.410 --> 01:43:49.560
but obviously choosing
those areas is critical.

01:43:49.560 --> 01:43:53.250
And then the final part
then the improvement support

01:43:53.250 --> 01:43:56.910
that we provide to the most challenged.

01:43:56.910 --> 01:43:59.370
We do obviously continue
to need to do that

01:43:59.370 --> 01:44:01.500
aligned to the national
oversight framework.

01:44:01.500 --> 01:44:03.600
It is predominantly
supporting those trusts

01:44:03.600 --> 01:44:07.140
in segments four and
five with some in three.

01:44:07.140 --> 01:44:09.870
We've aligned everything
around get the GIRFT programme

01:44:09.870 --> 01:44:11.100
as you know this year

01:44:11.100 --> 01:44:14.230
both in terms of systematic support

01:44:15.930 --> 01:44:18.780
led and working in
conjunction with the regions,

01:44:18.780 --> 01:44:21.510
but also being able to put in much more

01:44:21.510 --> 01:44:23.310
real-time responsive support

01:44:23.310 --> 01:44:25.290
where that's important
for improvement as well.

01:44:25.290 --> 01:44:27.600
So I call out GIRFT colleagues

01:44:27.600 --> 01:44:28.890
that have done an awful lot of work

01:44:28.890 --> 01:44:31.920
to support on eradication
of corridor care in trust

01:44:31.920 --> 01:44:33.750
that's just been at really short notice,

01:44:33.750 --> 01:44:34.620
so it's not just based

01:44:34.620 --> 01:44:38.220
on sort of longer term
metrics and indicators.

01:44:38.220 --> 01:44:39.210
And then we've also got

01:44:39.210 --> 01:44:41.310
the National Provider
Improvement programme as well,

01:44:41.310 --> 01:44:43.650
which is just about to take off,

01:44:43.650 --> 01:44:45.750
take from the Recovery Support Programme

01:44:45.750 --> 01:44:48.900
for those that are most
challenged in NOF five.

01:44:48.900 --> 01:44:50.520
So we think this is the right balance

01:44:50.520 --> 01:44:53.160
between just letting
people get on locally,

01:44:53.160 --> 01:44:55.590
but having the sort of the infrastructure,

01:44:55.590 --> 01:44:57.480
the leadership through the regions

01:44:57.480 --> 01:44:59.040
and nationally to be able to support

01:44:59.040 --> 01:45:00.630
on improvements moving forwards.

01:45:00.630 --> 01:45:02.430
And our next step would be to link it

01:45:02.430 --> 01:45:06.090
to what are the big shifts
that we really want to make

01:45:06.090 --> 01:45:08.910
and what would we expect to get from it

01:45:08.910 --> 01:45:11.850
so that we can then move forward

01:45:11.850 --> 01:45:14.820
with our operational plan
for the next three years.

01:45:14.820 --> 01:45:15.653
- Okay, thank you.

01:45:15.653 --> 01:45:18.600
So I think to a degree the
proof will be in the pudding.

01:45:18.600 --> 01:45:23.580
Will we see performance
looking significantly better

01:45:23.580 --> 01:45:25.260
in six months and in a year,

01:45:25.260 --> 01:45:27.480
and how quickly we're
going to revisit this

01:45:27.480 --> 01:45:29.100
and say what is working, what isn't?

01:45:29.100 --> 01:45:30.330
I mean, I'd suggest we need

01:45:30.330 --> 01:45:32.220
to be revisiting it pretty frequently

01:45:32.220 --> 01:45:35.520
'cause we don't have the luxury of time.

01:45:35.520 --> 01:45:37.830
Okay, I'll take a few questions together.

01:45:37.830 --> 01:45:40.050
So I've got Louise and Robert

01:45:40.050 --> 01:45:42.483
and Duncan and Claire and Paul,

01:45:43.800 --> 01:45:45.660
and we are rapidly running out of time.

01:45:45.660 --> 01:45:46.890
So what I'm going to do,

01:45:46.890 --> 01:45:48.390
so to not have you come back on these,

01:45:48.390 --> 01:45:49.560
we'll take these as comments,

01:45:49.560 --> 01:45:53.343
and we'll ask you to take
them away and them in, Robert.

01:45:54.270 --> 01:45:57.090
- Oh, so I think most of us believe

01:45:57.090 --> 01:45:59.550
that non-incremental progress is possible

01:45:59.550 --> 01:46:00.990
and we discussed that earlier.

01:46:00.990 --> 01:46:04.290
I really liked your reference to big leaps

01:46:04.290 --> 01:46:06.434
twice in the paper, indeed

01:46:06.434 --> 01:46:10.010
- An obligation my come with big leaps.

01:46:10.010 --> 01:46:10.935
So my question,

01:46:10.935 --> 01:46:12.060
and you're not going to
be able to answer it,

01:46:12.060 --> 01:46:14.220
is have you seen any big leaps yet,

01:46:14.220 --> 01:46:15.540
and are we going to make sure

01:46:15.540 --> 01:46:17.610
that the big leaps are
then well publicised

01:46:17.610 --> 01:46:20.235
so others can jump too?

01:46:20.235 --> 01:46:21.068
- Very good, Louise.

01:46:21.068 --> 01:46:22.410
- So I suppose I'm just looking forward

01:46:22.410 --> 01:46:24.925
to the next iteration where we can see

01:46:24.925 --> 01:46:28.650
what the methodologies actually
are against productivity,

01:46:28.650 --> 01:46:31.923
against safety, against,
you know, admin issues.

01:46:33.120 --> 01:46:35.550
And I know we launched
GIRFT which is great,

01:46:35.550 --> 01:46:36.383
and it does work,

01:46:36.383 --> 01:46:40.260
but then if GIRFT did work
consistently everywhere,

01:46:40.260 --> 01:46:43.560
then we wouldn't have to have
Jess's Rule or Martha's Rule

01:46:43.560 --> 01:46:45.390
or you know, these kind of waves

01:46:45.390 --> 01:46:49.230
of kind of additional
interventions that have to come.

01:46:49.230 --> 01:46:51.093
So we look forward to that I suppose.

01:46:52.020 --> 01:46:53.760
- Yeah, I agree, Duncan.

01:46:53.760 --> 01:46:56.340
- So really supportive, I guess,

01:46:56.340 --> 01:46:57.600
and just reflecting on a couple

01:46:57.600 --> 01:46:58.830
of conversations we've been in,

01:46:58.830 --> 01:47:01.050
and Jim and I were with my
strategic advisory group

01:47:01.050 --> 01:47:02.700
of chief nurses yesterday

01:47:02.700 --> 01:47:04.050
and in the corridor conversation,

01:47:04.050 --> 01:47:07.020
there are certain groups
of staff in the NHS

01:47:07.020 --> 01:47:10.650
that will have a bigger impact
than others in this space.

01:47:10.650 --> 01:47:12.720
And I'm thinking ward
sisters, charge nurses

01:47:12.720 --> 01:47:14.280
and how they see themselves in this,

01:47:14.280 --> 01:47:16.350
'cause those are the people
that have the biggest reach

01:47:16.350 --> 01:47:19.500
in terms of not just nursing staff

01:47:19.500 --> 01:47:21.420
but across the multidisciplinary team,

01:47:21.420 --> 01:47:24.210
and will impact quality flow safety.

01:47:24.210 --> 01:47:25.410
So I just think there's something about

01:47:25.410 --> 01:47:27.750
how do we make sure it really lands well

01:47:27.750 --> 01:47:29.070
with certain staff groups

01:47:29.070 --> 01:47:31.293
and they are prioritised in this space.

01:47:32.550 --> 01:47:33.383
- Claire.

01:47:33.383 --> 01:47:34.216
- I had the joy yesterday

01:47:34.216 --> 01:47:35.490
of doing the health select committee

01:47:35.490 --> 01:47:38.220
talking about NHS pilots,

01:47:38.220 --> 01:47:40.680
and a lot of the criticism came

01:47:40.680 --> 01:47:43.830
around really the NHS has
relied really heavily on pilots

01:47:43.830 --> 01:47:45.300
to initiate change.

01:47:45.300 --> 01:47:47.400
And so I say one of the
ways that we will know

01:47:47.400 --> 01:47:49.710
that this is working is we've
got a real cultural shift

01:47:49.710 --> 01:47:52.650
across the whole system that's
driving the improvement,

01:47:52.650 --> 01:47:54.420
and we should stop seeing this reliance

01:47:54.420 --> 01:47:55.440
on small pots of money

01:47:55.440 --> 01:47:57.963
that get handed to people
to do small projects.

01:47:59.370 --> 01:48:00.203
- Paul.

01:48:00.203 --> 01:48:03.270
- Maybe just to build on Duncan's point,

01:48:03.270 --> 01:48:05.517
I think the other role that we've got,

01:48:05.517 --> 01:48:06.780
and we've talked about this a bit,

01:48:06.780 --> 01:48:09.390
is actually data and data infrastructure,

01:48:09.390 --> 01:48:10.710
and using the data infrastructure

01:48:10.710 --> 01:48:12.930
that we have to start putting good data

01:48:12.930 --> 01:48:16.290
and insights into the hands
of people inside organisations

01:48:16.290 --> 01:48:17.700
that are responsible
for driving that change,

01:48:17.700 --> 01:48:19.320
and I think that that
connects to your point.

01:48:19.320 --> 01:48:20.787
- Yeah, well, that's what
we're going to come on to.

01:48:20.787 --> 01:48:23.550
And the last chart of the
papers shows us very nicely

01:48:23.550 --> 01:48:25.890
how we could be monitoring
some improvements.

01:48:25.890 --> 01:48:28.080
So I think what would be great

01:48:28.080 --> 01:48:29.580
for us to be able to see more clearly,

01:48:29.580 --> 01:48:32.310
and you know, I recognise we
might not have this for March,

01:48:32.310 --> 01:48:33.720
but I think we should aspire to have it

01:48:33.720 --> 01:48:36.360
for whenever the following
Board meeting is,

01:48:36.360 --> 01:48:38.520
is much greater clarity on the what.

01:48:38.520 --> 01:48:40.980
So all the things, Elizabeth,
that you are working on

01:48:40.980 --> 01:48:44.400
and other colleagues are
working on, and then how,

01:48:44.400 --> 01:48:46.410
how we are expecting to see these improve,

01:48:46.410 --> 01:48:48.570
and crucially over what timeframe

01:48:48.570 --> 01:48:49.710
as a number of people have asked for

01:48:49.710 --> 01:48:52.260
what will be different in a year,

01:48:52.260 --> 01:48:53.670
three years, five years, 10 years,

01:48:53.670 --> 01:48:57.150
which all links into the 10-year
plan implementation anyway.

01:48:57.150 --> 01:48:58.410
So recognise it's a big piece,

01:48:58.410 --> 01:49:00.030
you know, it's a big thing to do.

01:49:00.030 --> 01:49:01.573
But I think if we can
start to do some of that,

01:49:01.573 --> 01:49:05.820
that would be good, or at
least some leading indicators.

01:49:05.820 --> 01:49:08.640
- Okay, so talking of which, Jules,

01:49:08.640 --> 01:49:10.770
how are we going to
become more transparent

01:49:10.770 --> 01:49:12.570
and share that data?

01:49:12.570 --> 01:49:13.413
- So, thank you.

01:49:13.413 --> 01:49:15.360
So a short introduction to the paper

01:49:15.360 --> 01:49:18.140
in regular titled "The
New Era of Transparency."

01:49:18.140 --> 01:49:20.730
So the paper provides an
update on the NHS's shift

01:49:20.730 --> 01:49:22.140
to a new era of transparency,

01:49:22.140 --> 01:49:25.260
which is reflecting clear
expectations from this Board

01:49:25.260 --> 01:49:27.750
and which is reinforced
in the 10-year plan

01:49:27.750 --> 01:49:31.170
to become the most transparent
health system in the world.

01:49:31.170 --> 01:49:35.130
So we're moving decisively
to open, by default, data,

01:49:35.130 --> 01:49:37.860
applying governance and legal checks only

01:49:37.860 --> 01:49:39.813
where there is a specific concern.

01:49:41.220 --> 01:49:44.280
Across NHS programme, reporting insights,

01:49:44.280 --> 01:49:47.730
publications, and transparency
has now been established

01:49:47.730 --> 01:49:52.710
to deliver and provide the
required cultural change.

01:49:52.710 --> 01:49:55.530
So in early progress,
we've had public release

01:49:55.530 --> 01:49:57.570
of major transparency assets

01:49:57.570 --> 01:50:00.960
including the NHS Oversight Framework,

01:50:00.960 --> 01:50:03.570
segmentation, league tables,

01:50:03.570 --> 01:50:06.630
and associated acute provider tables.

01:50:06.630 --> 01:50:09.120
The next step is making much more

01:50:09.120 --> 01:50:11.730
of the content available
in the benchmarking tool,

01:50:11.730 --> 01:50:13.320
the model health system,

01:50:13.320 --> 01:50:15.510
which is going to be openly available

01:50:15.510 --> 01:50:17.730
from late February, early March.

01:50:17.730 --> 01:50:20.610
And this would offer
unprecedented visibility

01:50:20.610 --> 01:50:24.120
of variation in both clinical
quality and productivity,

01:50:24.120 --> 01:50:26.610
and therefore aligns with
our 10-year health plan

01:50:26.610 --> 01:50:28.563
and the forthcoming quality strategy.

01:50:30.240 --> 01:50:31.530
- Okay, great, Jim.

01:50:31.530 --> 01:50:32.970
- So this can sound technical,

01:50:32.970 --> 01:50:35.620
but we'll go back to Paul's
challenge a few months ago.

01:50:35.620 --> 01:50:37.710
It's a big psychological shift,

01:50:37.710 --> 01:50:39.840
the behavioural shift for all of us.

01:50:39.840 --> 01:50:40.980
I think we're all signed up

01:50:40.980 --> 01:50:45.000
with a transparency
theme as a general point,

01:50:45.000 --> 01:50:47.310
but it's a big transition
from where we've been,

01:50:47.310 --> 01:50:48.210
you know, so we've got to think

01:50:48.210 --> 01:50:50.640
about how we systematise this.

01:50:50.640 --> 01:50:52.860
And also then, you know,
we've had a bit of this

01:50:52.860 --> 01:50:54.780
in recent weeks when we publish stuff,

01:50:54.780 --> 01:50:55.950
other people will have views

01:50:55.950 --> 01:50:58.800
on what that means and that's fine.

01:50:58.800 --> 01:51:00.210
You know, I think we've
got to stop feeling

01:51:00.210 --> 01:51:03.570
like we own the only view on data.

01:51:03.570 --> 01:51:05.040
The point of this is get it out,

01:51:05.040 --> 01:51:07.705
have the different views,
can it help us improve,

01:51:07.705 --> 01:51:09.480
you know, going back to the last point.

01:51:09.480 --> 01:51:11.593
So I think, we've just got
to hold our nerve on that,

01:51:11.593 --> 01:51:13.500
'cause you can feel the pressure

01:51:13.500 --> 01:51:15.320
when something gets kicked around,

01:51:15.320 --> 01:51:18.510
we get a bit of negativity
on some data or whatever.

01:51:18.510 --> 01:51:19.527
There's learning in it,

01:51:19.527 --> 01:51:22.080
and we've just got to
stick with it, I think.

01:51:22.080 --> 01:51:25.803
- Yeah, so I've got Matthew, Robert.

01:51:26.850 --> 01:51:29.520
- And just to say that I
obviously particularly welcome

01:51:29.520 --> 01:51:31.860
the progress we've made on the publishing,

01:51:31.860 --> 01:51:33.837
the National Oversight Framework data

01:51:33.837 --> 01:51:36.210
and all the underpinning data.

01:51:36.210 --> 01:51:39.120
I think we need to be equally focused

01:51:39.120 --> 01:51:40.770
in terms of the next steps in this

01:51:40.770 --> 01:51:44.430
on applying the same
rigour to transparency

01:51:44.430 --> 01:51:46.860
around our non-acute
services, our acute services.

01:51:46.860 --> 01:51:47.910
And I think particularly

01:51:47.910 --> 01:51:50.220
of the recommendations
in the Kingdom Review

01:51:50.220 --> 01:51:51.900
about the difference that
might have made there.

01:51:51.900 --> 01:51:54.780
So really keeping our focus on, you know,

01:51:54.780 --> 01:51:57.330
building the robustness
of those other datasets,

01:51:57.330 --> 01:51:59.040
as well as making the datasets

01:51:59.040 --> 01:52:00.900
we've already got more available.

01:52:00.900 --> 01:52:04.740
And similarly, the same
point applies to the work

01:52:04.740 --> 01:52:06.180
that I know we've got on the way

01:52:06.180 --> 01:52:09.300
on improving data around
patient experience,

01:52:09.300 --> 01:52:10.680
'cause I think that's another area

01:52:10.680 --> 01:52:12.840
where transparency is going
to be really, really important

01:52:12.840 --> 01:52:15.930
if we're going to get the
power out of that data.

01:52:15.930 --> 01:52:17.403
So thank you, Jules.

01:52:18.240 --> 01:52:19.800
- Robert.
- Two quick comments.

01:52:19.800 --> 01:52:22.137
I mean, it's absolutely
the right thing to do.

01:52:22.137 --> 01:52:24.180
And I don't know, Jules, if you're aware

01:52:24.180 --> 01:52:25.560
of what the surgeons are doing,

01:52:25.560 --> 01:52:27.570
they've got a programme called NCIP,

01:52:27.570 --> 01:52:30.210
which is a great exercise in transparency.

01:52:30.210 --> 01:52:33.750
It's homegrown, but it's
actually very impressive

01:52:33.750 --> 01:52:34.770
and it might be worth looking at.

01:52:34.770 --> 01:52:36.390
- Thank you.
- I've just got one comment,

01:52:36.390 --> 01:52:38.880
and that is, while it's
the right thing to do,

01:52:38.880 --> 01:52:41.670
if I knew that I was living next

01:52:41.670 --> 01:52:43.590
to the worst performing
trust in the country

01:52:43.590 --> 01:52:45.870
and that was where I was
going to get my care,

01:52:45.870 --> 01:52:46.980
I wouldn't feel great.

01:52:46.980 --> 01:52:49.440
But if this is linked
in some way to choice,

01:52:49.440 --> 01:52:51.390
then transparency not only

01:52:51.390 --> 01:52:52.640
is probably going to be a catalyst

01:52:52.640 --> 01:52:54.900
for improvement within the system,

01:52:54.900 --> 01:52:57.300
but actually in terms of the patient,

01:52:57.300 --> 01:52:59.250
and whether NHS Online might have

01:52:59.250 --> 01:53:00.900
some contribution to make to that.

01:53:00.900 --> 01:53:03.000
But I think without choice,

01:53:03.000 --> 01:53:06.093
then there may be some
slightly negative consequence.

01:53:07.110 --> 01:53:08.520
- Louise.

01:53:08.520 --> 01:53:10.830
- Thanks, Robert, that's
such an important point.

01:53:10.830 --> 01:53:11.663
Thanks for raising it.

01:53:11.663 --> 01:53:14.400
And actually I was going to
talk about My Planned Care

01:53:14.400 --> 01:53:18.450
as an example of kind of almost
poor transparency in a way

01:53:18.450 --> 01:53:21.670
because all it does is give averages

01:53:22.560 --> 01:53:24.510
of waiting times rather than

01:53:24.510 --> 01:53:27.510
what is actually in a
more granular way the case

01:53:27.510 --> 01:53:31.080
for your procedure and
your local hospital,

01:53:31.080 --> 01:53:34.020
which would enable
choice to Robert's point.

01:53:34.020 --> 01:53:36.450
So you've co-designed,

01:53:36.450 --> 01:53:38.880
you know, meaningful with
the public with people,

01:53:38.880 --> 01:53:42.213
and it really has to be
in order to enable choice.

01:53:44.940 --> 01:53:49.350
- So I was going to a bit
build on that as well.

01:53:49.350 --> 01:53:51.930
So about 10, 15 years ago,

01:53:51.930 --> 01:53:56.040
you could go on London SHA website,

01:53:56.040 --> 01:53:57.060
and to your point Matthew,

01:53:57.060 --> 01:53:59.130
you could see every single GP practise,

01:53:59.130 --> 01:54:01.110
and you could see detailed quality data

01:54:01.110 --> 01:54:03.030
about, you know, diabetes,

01:54:03.030 --> 01:54:04.800
complication rate from diabetes and so on,

01:54:04.800 --> 01:54:05.910
and that no longer exists.

01:54:05.910 --> 01:54:07.890
So we're going to bring that back.

01:54:07.890 --> 01:54:10.860
And then secondly, how
far are we going to go

01:54:10.860 --> 01:54:14.883
on real clinical metrics to enable choice?

01:54:15.870 --> 01:54:19.500
So we did offer to Norman Williams

01:54:19.500 --> 01:54:21.270
to come and share some of that data here

01:54:21.270 --> 01:54:24.333
on consultant outcomes and
I think we should do that.

01:54:25.410 --> 01:54:30.410
And then thirdly, how do we, almost then,

01:54:30.570 --> 01:54:32.610
how do we make sure that we take action

01:54:32.610 --> 01:54:34.230
as a result of this?

01:54:34.230 --> 01:54:36.300
So even if I look at that

01:54:36.300 --> 01:54:39.177
relatively simplistic chart there,

01:54:39.177 --> 01:54:42.030
it might remind ourselves other
healthcare systems measure

01:54:42.030 --> 01:54:44.490
length of stay for primary hip replacement

01:54:44.490 --> 01:54:46.650
in terms of hours, not days.

01:54:46.650 --> 01:54:48.570
And they see anything over 48 hours

01:54:48.570 --> 01:54:50.760
is a serious untoward incident.

01:54:50.760 --> 01:54:54.720
So what are we doing to
actually say this is not good?

01:54:54.720 --> 01:54:56.700
Have we got the right
improvement approaches,

01:54:56.700 --> 01:54:59.940
and are we tackling it
as quickly as we could?

01:54:59.940 --> 01:55:02.520
So I dunno whether you've
got answers to those now.

01:55:02.520 --> 01:55:04.170
Some of those may be better picked up

01:55:04.170 --> 01:55:05.937
through the Quality Committee

01:55:05.937 --> 01:55:07.740
and the National Quality Board first.

01:55:07.740 --> 01:55:09.540
But are those all...

01:55:09.540 --> 01:55:10.530
I suppose the best thing would be,

01:55:10.530 --> 01:55:12.880
are those all pieces of
work that are underway?

01:55:14.477 --> 01:55:15.693
- I think most of those are,

01:55:15.693 --> 01:55:17.170
it's how it links, Penny.

01:55:17.170 --> 01:55:19.920
So if we can bring that
back, that'd be helpful.

01:55:19.920 --> 01:55:20.753
- I think that would be good.

01:55:20.753 --> 01:55:21.586
But it might be good to take

01:55:21.586 --> 01:55:24.153
to the Quality Committees first,

01:55:25.320 --> 01:55:26.930
and then bring it back here.

01:55:26.930 --> 01:55:27.763
Is that okay?

01:55:29.250 --> 01:55:31.140
Okay, great, really important piece,

01:55:31.140 --> 01:55:33.960
and I think we're all
keen to see more on that.

01:55:33.960 --> 01:55:34.793
Jo.

01:55:34.793 --> 01:55:36.765
- Yeah, been a common
theme to today, isn't it?

01:55:36.765 --> 01:55:38.970
We've all been sort of talking about

01:55:38.970 --> 01:55:40.500
the purpose of what we're doing.

01:55:40.500 --> 01:55:42.150
So quite often the papers are structured

01:55:42.150 --> 01:55:44.280
in terms of the means,
transparency is a means.

01:55:44.280 --> 01:55:45.900
But we're saying actually

01:55:45.900 --> 01:55:48.630
it's the way we're going
to drive improvements.

01:55:48.630 --> 01:55:50.760
It's not about transparency,
it's the way we do it.

01:55:50.760 --> 01:55:52.290
Same, the life expectancy memory

01:55:52.290 --> 01:55:54.060
is a lot better performance metrics,

01:55:54.060 --> 01:55:55.880
so the inputs rather than actually looking

01:55:55.880 --> 01:55:57.480
at the outputs that they should drive.

01:55:57.480 --> 01:55:59.550
So we just need to kind
of reframe our work

01:55:59.550 --> 01:56:02.760
to remind ourselves others
why we're doing this.

01:56:02.760 --> 01:56:05.280
- Yeah, very good point, thank you.

01:56:05.280 --> 01:56:07.530
Okay, thank you very much everyone.

01:56:07.530 --> 01:56:09.510
We have been slightly running over,

01:56:09.510 --> 01:56:11.640
but it felt like we're having good

01:56:11.640 --> 01:56:14.760
and crucially important conversations.

01:56:14.760 --> 01:56:18.060
We have got a few questions
from members of the public.

01:56:18.060 --> 01:56:22.050
So first of all, Sarah-Jane,
questions for you.

01:56:22.050 --> 01:56:24.060
Given pressures currently facing hospitals

01:56:24.060 --> 01:56:26.100
and the role of the 111 triage system,

01:56:26.100 --> 01:56:27.870
what are the considerations or barriers

01:56:27.870 --> 01:56:30.780
for implementing a
comprehensive triage system

01:56:30.780 --> 01:56:34.080
at hospital entrances to
improve patient direction

01:56:34.080 --> 01:56:36.120
and reduce the demand on A&amp;E.

01:56:36.120 --> 01:56:37.230
- And just, I was really pleased

01:56:37.230 --> 01:56:38.820
to see this question when
it came up. I was like,

01:56:38.820 --> 01:56:41.397
"Oh, yes okay, so-
- Did you send it in?

01:56:41.397 --> 01:56:46.397
- No, it's possible.

01:56:46.500 --> 01:56:49.050
So every patient that arrives

01:56:49.050 --> 01:56:50.280
at an emergency department

01:56:50.280 --> 01:56:54.720
should be triaged, streamed, and directed

01:56:54.720 --> 01:56:57.420
to the most appropriate service for them

01:56:57.420 --> 01:56:59.010
as they are checked in

01:56:59.010 --> 01:57:01.380
and that clinical triage takes place.

01:57:01.380 --> 01:57:03.270
We do know that there is variation

01:57:03.270 --> 01:57:05.310
in the way that happens at the moment,

01:57:05.310 --> 01:57:07.800
and we are going to be
publishing next week

01:57:07.800 --> 01:57:10.290
the model emergency department document.

01:57:10.290 --> 01:57:11.700
But our expectation is that

01:57:11.700 --> 01:57:13.530
over the course of the next year,

01:57:13.530 --> 01:57:15.990
ideally sooner in the instance of this,

01:57:15.990 --> 01:57:17.893
we will work to a more
standardised approach,

01:57:17.893 --> 01:57:20.310
so that is the case for all patients.

01:57:20.310 --> 01:57:24.063
There are digital tools that support this,

01:57:24.960 --> 01:57:27.300
and I think it's a really
important direction of travel,

01:57:27.300 --> 01:57:28.650
'cause what we really don't want

01:57:28.650 --> 01:57:30.150
is people to have to arrive

01:57:30.150 --> 01:57:31.980
to be redirected to something else

01:57:31.980 --> 01:57:33.900
that a digital triage
would've allowed them

01:57:33.900 --> 01:57:35.340
to have presented at the right place

01:57:35.340 --> 01:57:37.560
in right time in the first place.

01:57:37.560 --> 01:57:39.960
So with what Paul was describing

01:57:39.960 --> 01:57:43.020
for the sort of medium
term strategy for UEC,

01:57:43.020 --> 01:57:45.840
we would see this one as
one of our cornerstones,

01:57:45.840 --> 01:57:47.340
'cause we want to get to a place

01:57:47.340 --> 01:57:50.610
where an emergency
department is a destination,

01:57:50.610 --> 01:57:52.800
not the place that you go by default.

01:57:52.800 --> 01:57:54.630
But we can't expect that of the public

01:57:54.630 --> 01:57:57.480
until we've got much clearer navigation,

01:57:57.480 --> 01:58:00.510
triage streaming to be
able to support to do that.

01:58:00.510 --> 01:58:03.000
There are some examples
around the country.

01:58:03.000 --> 01:58:05.040
In the meantime, I would say,

01:58:05.040 --> 01:58:07.410
that calling 111 in advance

01:58:07.410 --> 01:58:11.250
is always the most helpful
if people are unsure,

01:58:11.250 --> 01:58:12.810
because the directory of services

01:58:12.810 --> 01:58:16.020
that sits behind that
can support redirection

01:58:16.020 --> 01:58:17.460
so that people can go to a place

01:58:17.460 --> 01:58:19.230
that's often better for them

01:58:19.230 --> 01:58:21.840
than an emergency department
if they don't need one.

01:58:21.840 --> 01:58:22.740
But I really do...

01:58:22.740 --> 01:58:24.240
I completely understand,

01:58:24.240 --> 01:58:26.160
we've all seen it in the
emergency departments

01:58:26.160 --> 01:58:29.370
that we've been in, and
we are going to have to do

01:58:29.370 --> 01:58:31.650
a lot more of this over the
next few years for sure.

01:58:31.650 --> 01:58:32.700
And I'm really glad that, you know,

01:58:32.700 --> 01:58:35.910
the sort of patients can see
what we can see essentially,

01:58:35.910 --> 01:58:37.980
and want to see the same sort of changes.

01:58:37.980 --> 01:58:39.857
- Okay, great, thank you.

01:58:39.857 --> 01:58:40.950
- It definitely wasn't me,

01:58:40.950 --> 01:58:44.073
but I'll think about what
I want to answer next time.

01:58:45.090 --> 01:58:47.400
- Claire, how does NHS England ensure

01:58:47.400 --> 01:58:50.880
public-facing mental health
information on nhs.uk,

01:58:50.880 --> 01:58:52.770
reflects current clinical understanding,

01:58:52.770 --> 01:58:54.510
aligns with mental health standards,

01:58:54.510 --> 01:58:57.600
and avoids stigmatising terminology

01:58:57.600 --> 01:59:01.140
such as habit for
recognised mental disorders?

01:59:01.140 --> 01:59:01.973
- Thanks very much, Penny.

01:59:01.973 --> 01:59:04.020
So the content in the NHS website

01:59:04.020 --> 01:59:05.647
is reviewed systematically
every three years.

01:59:05.647 --> 01:59:07.350
But if we get new evidence

01:59:07.350 --> 01:59:09.720
or if we get feedback
from members of public

01:59:09.720 --> 01:59:12.090
or stakeholders, we can
update it more frequently.

01:59:12.090 --> 01:59:15.600
The current content is based on ICD-10,

01:59:15.600 --> 01:59:17.700
which the International
Classification Diseases,

01:59:17.700 --> 01:59:20.400
which is developed by the WHO,

01:59:20.400 --> 01:59:22.230
the World Health Organisation.

01:59:22.230 --> 01:59:24.837
We are currently working on ICD-10,

01:59:24.837 --> 01:59:28.140
and the particular question
referred to trichotillomania,

01:59:28.140 --> 01:59:30.300
which is described in ICD-10 as a habit

01:59:30.300 --> 01:59:31.710
of an impulse disorder,

01:59:31.710 --> 01:59:34.890
which is an outmoded and
stigmatised language,

01:59:34.890 --> 01:59:37.560
been ICD-11, trichotillomania
is classified

01:59:37.560 --> 01:59:40.230
as body-focused repetitive
behaviour disorder.

01:59:40.230 --> 01:59:41.250
And we're going through a process

01:59:41.250 --> 01:59:43.410
of updating the content appropriately.

01:59:43.410 --> 01:59:44.243
- Okay, great.

01:59:44.243 --> 01:59:45.556
Thank you, I did not know that.

01:59:45.556 --> 01:59:47.790
- There you go.
- Thank you.

01:59:47.790 --> 01:59:51.000
Matthew, question for you
following the Levy Review,

01:59:51.000 --> 01:59:54.570
when will NHSE be commenting
on the failings detailed

01:59:54.570 --> 01:59:56.460
and the recommendations,

01:59:56.460 --> 01:59:58.980
and additionally, when
revising the specifications,

01:59:58.980 --> 02:00:00.360
will NHS England commit

02:00:00.360 --> 02:00:02.883
to using plain language based on biology?

02:00:03.750 --> 02:00:07.260
- Thank you, so the Levy
Review was commissioned

02:00:07.260 --> 02:00:12.260
to look independently at adult
gender dysphoria services,

02:00:12.600 --> 02:00:16.710
including the variation in practise

02:00:16.710 --> 02:00:20.610
between those services and
opportunities for improvement.

02:00:20.610 --> 02:00:22.260
The report was published in December,

02:00:22.260 --> 02:00:23.820
and alongside the report,

02:00:23.820 --> 02:00:26.820
we set out our initial response,

02:00:26.820 --> 02:00:29.190
which committed to do further work

02:00:29.190 --> 02:00:31.530
on precisely the issue raised,

02:00:31.530 --> 02:00:34.890
and that is the variation in data

02:00:34.890 --> 02:00:36.450
around the proportion of patients

02:00:36.450 --> 02:00:39.750
who are discharged from these services

02:00:39.750 --> 02:00:43.470
because of repeated did not attends

02:00:43.470 --> 02:00:46.380
or sort of failure to maintain contact.

02:00:46.380 --> 02:00:50.250
That is an early priority
for the clinical audit work,

02:00:50.250 --> 02:00:51.930
not least because of the variation

02:00:51.930 --> 02:00:53.460
between services in that data

02:00:53.460 --> 02:00:56.280
as well as the overall picture.

02:00:56.280 --> 02:00:58.290
David Levy will be
chairing an oversight Board

02:00:58.290 --> 02:01:00.270
that will report on a regular basis

02:01:00.270 --> 02:01:02.790
on the progress of that work.

02:01:02.790 --> 02:01:05.460
On the issue around the plain language,

02:01:05.460 --> 02:01:07.830
we always seek, in our
commissioning policies,

02:01:07.830 --> 02:01:09.480
in our service specifications,

02:01:09.480 --> 02:01:11.760
to strike a balance between language,

02:01:11.760 --> 02:01:14.370
which is both clinically
and medically accurate,

02:01:14.370 --> 02:01:18.090
but also accessible and well understood

02:01:18.090 --> 02:01:22.413
by patients that may need
to access that information.

02:01:24.150 --> 02:01:27.030
We always consult on our
service specifications

02:01:27.030 --> 02:01:28.710
whenever they are changed precisely

02:01:28.710 --> 02:01:30.150
to ensure that we strike that balance

02:01:30.150 --> 02:01:31.440
as effectively as possible.

02:01:31.440 --> 02:01:32.610
- Okay, thank you.

02:01:32.610 --> 02:01:34.140
And then finally, Mark,

02:01:34.140 --> 02:01:37.020
you've got two questions
about advice and guidance.

02:01:37.020 --> 02:01:40.200
The first is before any further scaling up

02:01:40.200 --> 02:01:41.430
of advice and guidance,

02:01:41.430 --> 02:01:43.740
how does NHS England
determine which groups

02:01:43.740 --> 02:01:45.510
are engaged in the design phase,

02:01:45.510 --> 02:01:48.210
and what mechanisms
ensure this involvement

02:01:48.210 --> 02:01:50.670
constitutes genuine co-production

02:01:50.670 --> 02:01:53.220
rather than passive consultation?

02:01:53.220 --> 02:01:55.170
And the second part to the question

02:01:55.170 --> 02:01:56.880
is whether NHS England plans

02:01:56.880 --> 02:01:58.980
to mandate the use of advice and guidance

02:01:58.980 --> 02:02:00.720
as a requirement for GP referrals

02:02:00.720 --> 02:02:03.870
before April, 2027 with the request

02:02:03.870 --> 02:02:06.540
for details on the
intended scope, timeline,

02:02:06.540 --> 02:02:09.600
and guiding principles
of any such mandate.

02:02:09.600 --> 02:02:10.433
- Great, thanks Penny.

02:02:10.433 --> 02:02:12.450
So I'll go through in the same
order if that's all right.

02:02:12.450 --> 02:02:14.100
So the first one is, I
referred to it earlier

02:02:14.100 --> 02:02:15.420
about advice and guidance,

02:02:15.420 --> 02:02:18.160
and it is a clinical model

02:02:19.050 --> 02:02:21.900
where we've got GPs been able to contact

02:02:21.900 --> 02:02:24.360
and communicate with hospital clinicians

02:02:24.360 --> 02:02:25.530
without a traditional,

02:02:25.530 --> 02:02:28.323
the need for an automatic
referral for a condition.

02:02:29.250 --> 02:02:30.840
We have seen an increased use of that.

02:02:30.840 --> 02:02:32.007
So over the past two years,

02:02:32.007 --> 02:02:34.620
and just as an indication
for this year alone,

02:02:34.620 --> 02:02:38.640
we've seen a 24% increase with
a advice and guidance use,

02:02:38.640 --> 02:02:40.560
which means that patients,
as I said earlier,

02:02:40.560 --> 02:02:43.020
can get their treatment
started much earlier

02:02:43.020 --> 02:02:46.260
than just forming part of a
traditional referral route

02:02:46.260 --> 02:02:48.240
and going onto a waiting list.

02:02:48.240 --> 02:02:50.100
So we've been engaging really broadly

02:02:50.100 --> 02:02:52.020
with colleges, with clinicians,

02:02:52.020 --> 02:02:54.810
operational leads in
different organisations,

02:02:54.810 --> 02:02:56.610
different parts of the system,

02:02:56.610 --> 02:02:59.400
and we've done that to
inform our next steps

02:02:59.400 --> 02:03:01.860
in terms of the further rollout
for advice and guidance.

02:03:01.860 --> 02:03:03.240
What we have expected,

02:03:03.240 --> 02:03:06.000
and I think is happening at a local level

02:03:06.000 --> 02:03:07.740
is that the engagement continues

02:03:07.740 --> 02:03:10.590
at a local level to work
out how the pathways

02:03:10.590 --> 02:03:13.500
that we're going to be adopting

02:03:13.500 --> 02:03:15.450
a greater use of advice and guidance,

02:03:15.450 --> 02:03:17.730
where they can be implemented
as quickly as possible.

02:03:17.730 --> 02:03:20.130
So that's what we've been
doing on the first question.

02:03:20.130 --> 02:03:21.270
- Okay, so going to just check,

02:03:21.270 --> 02:03:24.450
you said "We think," can
we get some assurance

02:03:24.450 --> 02:03:28.320
that local providers are
involving users in that?

02:03:28.320 --> 02:03:30.840
- Yes, so sorry, I should, they have been.

02:03:30.840 --> 02:03:31.998
- Okay.
- Yes, we have.

02:03:31.998 --> 02:03:33.810
So through our conversations with ICBs,

02:03:33.810 --> 02:03:35.360
we know that those conversations
have been happening.

02:03:35.360 --> 02:03:38.580
The quality of them is
something that we can test out,

02:03:38.580 --> 02:03:41.180
but we know those conversations
have been happening.

02:03:42.300 --> 02:03:44.010
The second is about the rollout,

02:03:44.010 --> 02:03:47.910
and as part of our wider
outpatient transformation,

02:03:47.910 --> 02:03:50.010
this will be a cornerstone of that,

02:03:50.010 --> 02:03:52.230
just one element, but nonetheless,

02:03:52.230 --> 02:03:54.390
in the early part of the transformation,

02:03:54.390 --> 02:03:55.800
this will be critical.

02:03:55.800 --> 02:03:59.250
So for the coming year, for '26-'27,

02:03:59.250 --> 02:04:01.410
and it was referenced really clearly

02:04:01.410 --> 02:04:02.760
in our planning framework,

02:04:02.760 --> 02:04:04.470
the medium term planning framework,

02:04:04.470 --> 02:04:05.910
that we would have,

02:04:05.910 --> 02:04:07.860
we're asked every single
provider in the country

02:04:07.860 --> 02:04:11.370
to work with their local,
the ICB and local GPs,

02:04:11.370 --> 02:04:13.740
to look at their top 10 specialties

02:04:13.740 --> 02:04:15.630
where we think they'll
have the greatest impact

02:04:15.630 --> 02:04:16.980
for advice and guidance.

02:04:16.980 --> 02:04:18.660
So that's what we've asked everyone to do.

02:04:18.660 --> 02:04:19.860
I know from conversations

02:04:19.860 --> 02:04:21.720
I'm having with colleagues
up and down the country,

02:04:21.720 --> 02:04:24.120
and that's exactly what's happening now.

02:04:24.120 --> 02:04:25.410
And what we expect to see

02:04:25.410 --> 02:04:28.740
is a phased implementation next year,

02:04:28.740 --> 02:04:30.990
once we've got the digital tools available

02:04:30.990 --> 02:04:32.760
to support referral process

02:04:32.760 --> 02:04:34.740
and the communication between GPs

02:04:34.740 --> 02:04:38.370
as seamlessly as possible,
and hospital consultants,

02:04:38.370 --> 02:04:40.980
we expect to see a significant
rollout of that next year

02:04:40.980 --> 02:04:42.420
for that broader adoption.

02:04:42.420 --> 02:04:44.460
It hasn't been mandated to date.

02:04:44.460 --> 02:04:45.570
The only thing we're mandating

02:04:45.570 --> 02:04:48.270
is essentially the top 10 specialties,

02:04:48.270 --> 02:04:50.130
and we'll assess the
impact throughout the year

02:04:50.130 --> 02:04:51.720
to see what we do next.

02:04:51.720 --> 02:04:53.430
- Okay, great, thank you.

02:04:53.430 --> 02:04:56.643
Okay, does anyone have any other business?

02:04:57.779 --> 02:04:58.612
No, okay.

02:04:58.612 --> 02:05:01.170
So in that case, I'd like to say thank you

02:05:01.170 --> 02:05:02.430
to everyone around the table.

02:05:02.430 --> 02:05:04.950
It's an enormous agenda.

02:05:04.950 --> 02:05:06.900
I think it'll take us
back to where we started.

02:05:06.900 --> 02:05:09.000
It's great to see the progress.

02:05:09.000 --> 02:05:11.820
It feels like we are making
those tangible improvements

02:05:11.820 --> 02:05:14.820
on the ground for
patients and populations,

02:05:14.820 --> 02:05:16.860
which is obviously the
most important thing.

02:05:16.860 --> 02:05:19.860
But we're also doing a lot
of work behind the scenes

02:05:19.860 --> 02:05:23.760
to improve the infrastructure
and the ways of working.

02:05:23.760 --> 02:05:24.684
So thank you for that.

02:05:24.684 --> 02:05:27.840
We've got more to do as
we've discussed today,

02:05:27.840 --> 02:05:30.210
and I look forward to
continuing the conversation.

02:05:30.210 --> 02:05:33.630
Thank you to those who've
tuned in to listen,

02:05:33.630 --> 02:05:35.790
and look forward to
continuing the conversation

02:05:35.790 --> 02:05:36.780
in two months time.

02:05:36.780 --> 02:05:38.183
Thank you, everyone.

